"""Original Security teaching packs. Each body is instructor prose."""

BODIES = {
    'Public keys': {
        'body': "A public key is the shareable half of an asymmetric key pair. Other people use that half to lock a short value so only the matching private key can open it, or to check a signature the private key created. The public half cannot open the locked value, and the public half cannot create the signature. Copying a public key is the design, not a leak.\n\nRSA publishes a modulus and a public exponent, often 65537, and a 2048-bit modulus is a common minimum to accept. Elliptic-curve public keys are points on a named curve, and P-256 is the curve most TLS certificates use. The private scalar or the private primes stay off the wire. Public-key math is slow, so a salary file is not encrypted byte by byte under RSA. The file is encrypted with AES-256, and the AES key is what gets locked to the public key. Only the matching private key unwraps the AES key and then the file.\n\nDana Okonkwo, the finance lead, posts an RSA-2048 public key in the company directory. Each Friday the payroll service encrypts the salary workbook to that key before the file leaves the payroll host. The directory page shows a PEM block whose first line reads BEGIN PUBLIC KEY, plus the fingerprint 3F91A2C4 that Dana read aloud in a staff meeting. The payroll ticket records that fingerprint rather than a shared password. Coworkers can download the PEM. Coworkers who hold only that PEM still cannot open Friday's workbook.\n\nA defender binds the key to Dana's name with a fingerprint checked out of band, or with a certificate whose signer is already accepted. When a new key appears under Dana's name, compare the fingerprint with the one on last week's ticket before payroll uses the new key. If the private key is exposed, retire the pair and publish the replacement through the same checked channel. Watch the directory edit log for a change Dana did not request. A substituted public key would deliver the salary file to whoever holds the new private half.\n\nStudents call this half a shared secret, or they call the certificate itself the key. A symmetric key must stay hidden because the same value both locks and unlocks. A public key is built to be copied, and copying that key does not unlock anything. A certificate wraps a public key with a name and a signature. The bare key does not prove the name. Emailing Dana's public key does not hide a file from Dana. Emailing that key lets other people lock files to Dana.\n",
        'r0': "Dana's directory entry holds an RSA public key with a 2048-bit modulus and the public exponent 65537. Those two numbers are the shareable half. Payroll does not run RSA across the whole salary workbook. The service encrypts the workbook with AES-256 and wraps only the AES key to the public key. A verifier uses the same public numbers to check a signature Dana made with the private half, and one changed byte fails the check. Copying the PEM block does not reveal the private exponent. On an elliptic-curve certificate the public half is a point on the P-256 curve instead of a modulus, and the job of that point is the same. Record the fingerprint 3F91A2C4 beside the algorithm name so a later swap is obvious.\n",
        'r1': 'A public key is safe to share. The public half locks a message to one person. Other people can check a signature with this half. The public half cannot open the locked message. The public half cannot create the signature. The secret half does those two jobs. You may place this half in a directory. Copying it does not expose the secret half. Payroll can lock a salary file to it. Only the owner can open that file. Compare the fingerprint before you trust a new copy. A swapped copy would lock the file to a stranger. Treat a bare key as a name only after a check.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a post office wall of open cubbies. Anyone can drop a letter into Dana's cubby. The slot is wide enough for an envelope and too small to pull a letter back out. A clerk can also hold a seal up to the sample card posted beside the cubby. The sample card shows whether the seal matches. The sample card does not let the clerk make a new seal, and the slot does not let anyone fish mail out. People photograph the sample card all day. The mail stays inside until Dana uses the key kept at home. The cubby slot and the posted sample are the shareable half of the pair. In the real system that half can lock a file to one person or check a signature, and publishing it does not open either one.\n",
        'r4': 'Open the directory entry for Dana Okonkwo and read the PEM header. Write down the fingerprint, the algorithm, and the date the key appeared. Compare the fingerprint with the value Dana read in the staff meeting. Note who can edit the directory entry. Note whether a certificate binds a name to this key. Do not replace the key yet. Do not point payroll at a different fingerprint until the comparison is written down. Leave the private key file untouched while you record the public half.\n',
        'exam': 'Do not call a public key a shared secret. A shared secret both locks and unlocks, so that secret must stay hidden. A public key is meant to be copied, and the copy cannot open a message or forge a signature. Do not call the certificate the key. A certificate binds a name to a public key. The bare key does not establish the name. Emailing the public key does not hide a file from the owner. That email lets senders lock files to the owner. A PEM anyone can download is the public half.\n',
    },
    'Private keys': {
        'body': "A private key is the secret half of an asymmetric key pair. That half opens values locked to the matching public key, and that half creates signatures the public key can check. Anyone who copies the private key can impersonate the owner and can read what was locked to the owner. The file that holds the private key is the asset to protect, not a convenience copy for the team.\n\nAn RSA private key contains the private exponent and the primes behind the modulus, and a 2048-bit modulus is the size to expect as a minimum. An elliptic-curve private key is a scalar on a curve such as P-256, smaller on disk and just as sensitive. A certificate signing request must not carry the private key. The request carries the public key and the name. A passphrase on the file slows casual copying. A passphrase does not help after the unlocked key is sitting in memory on a host an attacker already controls.\n\nLuis Ortega finds the finance lead's key on a shared build server. The directory listing shows mode 644, so every account on that host can read finance-lead.key. The first line of the file reads BEGIN PRIVATE KEY, which is the header the public directory must not hold. The file-integrity ticket quotes the path /srv/share/keys/finance-lead.key and the mode bits rw-r--r--. With that file, a coworker can sign a payment approval that checks out against the published public key, and can open the salary workbook payroll locked to the finance lead.\n\nA defender searches for private-key headers and for modes that let the wrong accounts read them, then records the path, the owner, and the fingerprint before anyone rotates the key. A world-readable private key is exposed: revoke certificates that depend on it, publish a new public key, and rewrap any data the old key can still open. Long-lived private keys belong in a hardware module when the operation allows it, so the server can request a signature without a PEM on disk. Restrict the directory to the service account at mode 600, and alert when the mode changes.\n\nStudents swap the two halves, or they treat a world-readable private key as fine because the public key was meant to be shared. The private key signs and decrypts. The public key checks and encrypts. Sharing applies only to the public half. Key escrow is a different idea: a custodian holds a recovery copy under an audit log. Escrow is not permission to leave the private key in a folder the whole host can read.\n",
        'r0': 'The file /srv/share/keys/finance-lead.key is mode 644, which is rw-r--r-- for every account on the build server. The PEM header BEGIN PRIVATE KEY means this file can decrypt and sign, unlike a BEGIN PUBLIC KEY block. An RSA private key at a 2048-bit modulus includes the private exponent. A P-256 private key is a single scalar and is still enough to forge signatures that the published point will accept. A certificate signing request for this pair should contain the public modulus or the public point and the requested name, never this file. A passphrase stops a casual copy from being used immediately. Once the key has been world-readable, treat the pair as burned: revoke the certificate, place a new private key at mode 600, and rewrap salary files to the new public key.\n',
        'r1': 'The private half stays secret. The private half opens locked messages. The private half also makes signatures. Strangers must not hold this half. A world-readable key file lets others pretend to be the owner. The public half is the one you may post. Posting the public half does not excuse a loose private file. A certificate request should carry only the public half. Leave the private half at home. A hardware box should hold a long-lived private half when it can. Write down the path and the file mode first. Treat a copied private half as exposed. Retire the old pair after a copy leaks.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic keeps one metal key for the narcotics cabinet. Nurses do not hang that key on the board by the door. The metal key opens the cabinet, and the same key stamps the drug log so the log book can recognize the stamp. A copy left on the break-room counter lets anyone open the cabinet and stamp the log. Visitors may study the open-slot diagram posted on the wall. The wall diagram shows where a key would go. The wall diagram is not the metal key. Neighbors are supposed to see the diagram. Neighbors are not supposed to pocket the metal key. The metal key is the private half, and the posted diagram is the public half. Leaving the metal key where every person on the ward can take it is the failure.\n',
        'r4': 'List /srv/share/keys and read the mode bits on finance-lead.key. Write down the path, the owner, the mode, and the PEM header. Write down the fingerprint without copying the key into the ticket. Confirm whether a certificate signing request was built from this file. Do not chmod the file yet. Do not delete the key until the fingerprint and the dependent certificates are recorded. Leave the salary ciphertext in place while you plan the rewrap.\n',
        'exam': 'The private key decrypts and signs. The public key encrypts and checks. Do not reverse those jobs. A world-readable private key is not acceptable just because the matching public key is supposed to be shared. Sharing applies to the public half only. A certificate request carries the public half and the name. Do not send the private key with that request. Key escrow is an audited recovery copy held by a custodian. Escrow is not a world-readable folder. Mode 644 on a private-key file means every account on that host can impersonate the owner.\n',
    },
    'Key escrow': {
        'body': "Key escrow means a trusted holder keeps a recovery copy of a secret key so the organization can open its own data when the owner is gone. The escrowed object is the key, or a recovery key that unwraps it, not a second public key and not a backup of the ciphertext. Ciphertext without the key stays unreadable. The holder is the organization under a policy, not every teammate who asks.\n\nA laptop disk key can be sealed to a chip on that machine and also escrowed as a recovery key in a guarded store. A mailbox private key can be archived under dual control so one person alone cannot pull the archive. The employee still uses the working key each day. The escrowed copy answers a lost passphrase, a resignation, or a death. Every retrieval should write an audit row that names the key id, the ticket, and the two people who checked it out.\n\nPriya Shah leaves on a Friday. The laptop disk was encrypted with a passphrase only Priya knew. The help-desk ticket states that the customer files are on the machine and that no recovery-key id was deposited with IT. The disk prompt will not accept Priya's old domain password. The company owns the hardware and still cannot read the files. With escrow in place, the same ticket would name a recovery-key id in the vault, two custodians would check it out, and the retrieval would appear in the vault log before the volume was unlocked once.\n\nA defender deposits the recovery key before the laptop is handed to the employee. Limit who can read the vault, require two people for a retrieval, and alert when a key is fetched without a ticket. Do not email recovery keys. When a person leaves, confirm the escrowed copy still matches the disk before anyone wipes the machine. Escrow is a recovery control, and the vault is also a concentration of risk, so the vault needs stronger protection than any single laptop.\n\nStudents treat escrow as key exchange, or as publishing the public key. Key exchange lets two parties create a session secret and does not leave a recovery copy with the employer. A public key is shareable by design and cannot open the disk. Escrow is a deliberate copy of a secret, held so the organization is not locked out of its own data. A design that lets any employee read every escrowed key is not escrow. That design is a shared secret with extra steps.\n",
        'r0': "Priya's laptop has no recovery-key id in the vault, so the disk passphrase that exists only in Priya's memory cannot be replaced by a domain password. A proper deposit stores a recovery key, often the 48-digit recovery key used by full-disk products, under an id the help desk can name in a ticket. Two custodians should be required to fetch that id, and the vault log should show the ticket number and the time. The working disk key can still be sealed to the laptop's chip for daily boot. Escrow does not publish the key. A backup of the ciphertext without this recovery key leaves the files unreadable after the employee is gone. Deposit the key before day one, not during the exit interview.\n",
        'r1': "Key escrow keeps a spare secret for the organization. A trusted holder stores that spare. The spare is a key, not a pile of locked files. The spare answers a loss or a departure. Two people should be needed to take the spare out. Do not mail the spare to a personal inbox. A laptop needs this spare before the employee leaves. A passphrase that lives only in someone's head strands the company. Store the spare before you hand over the machine. The rest of the staff does not get a copy. Escrow is recovery for the organization, under a log. A public half cannot open the disk.\n",
        'r2': 'A tenant locks an apartment with a key that stays on a personal ring. The building office keeps a tagged spare in a cabinet that opens only when two staff badges are presented. The spare is not a photo of the lock. Stored furniture is not the spare either, because furniture in a locker stays shut if nobody has a key. When the tenant moves out and the ring is gone, two staffers log the spare out and open the door once. Neighbors do not receive a copy for the sake of convenience. The spare exists so the building is not stuck outside its own apartment. That spare is key escrow, a recovery copy held by a trusted party under a log. A public listing of the apartment number cannot open the door, so a listing is not escrow.\n',
        'r4': "Open the help-desk ticket for Priya Shah's laptop and look for a recovery-key id. Write down the asset number, whether a vault id exists, and who can read that vault. Check the vault log for any past retrieval of that id. Do not wipe the laptop yet. Do not email a recovery key to yourself while you look. Leave the disk locked until two custodians and a ticket are in place, or until the data is declared unrecoverable.\n",
        'exam': 'Key escrow is a recovery copy of a secret, held by a trusted party so the organization can open data after the owner is gone. Do not call escrow a public key. A public key cannot open the disk. Do not call escrow key exchange. Key exchange builds a session secret and does not leave the employer a spare. Dual control and an audit row are part of a real escrow. Deposit the spare before the employee leaves, or the company can own the laptop and still be locked out.\n',
    },
    'Full-disk encryption': {
        'body': 'Full-disk encryption locks the whole drive, including the operating system, swap, and temporary files, so a stolen machine that is powered off does not yield its files. The usual cipher is AES-256 in a disk mode such as XTS. One volume key encrypts the sectors. A person unlocks that key at boot with a passphrase, with a PIN plus a platform chip, or with a recovery key. After the machine is unlocked and running, the operating system sees ordinary files. Theft of a powered-on, unlocked laptop walks past this control.\n\nThe protection is at rest. A file copied to a USB stick during an open session leaves in the clear unless another control encrypts that file. A backup tool that reads the live filesystem writes plaintext unless the backup has its own encryption. Malware running as the logged-in user reads the same files the user can read. Sleep that leaves the key in memory behaves more like a running machine than like a powered-off disk. The lock is the boundary between powered-off ciphertext and an unlocked working system.\n\nJonah Brooks loses a laptop on a train. The imaging record for that asset shows full-disk encryption was never enabled, and the disk is a plain filesystem. Whoever boots the machine gets the customer list in the Documents folder with no prompt. A sister laptop that was encrypted presents a different loss: the boot screen asks for a PIN or a recovery key, and a raw image of the drive is AES ciphertext with no directory listing. The ticket has to record which of those two states the lost asset was in, because they are different incidents.\n\nA defender requires encryption on every portable machine before that machine stores work data, and checks the device status rather than the policy slide. On a managed fleet, look for a protection flag of On and for a recovery key already stored in escrow. Require a PIN in addition to the platform chip so merely powering the laptop on is not enough. When a machine is retired, confirm the disk was encrypted and then wipe it. Alert when a device reports protection off.\n\nStudents confuse a locked disk with file encryption or with a screen lock. A screen lock stops a passerby and does nothing for a disk that is removed and read on another machine. File encryption locks one document and leaves swap and temp files readable. Full-disk encryption covers the drive while the power is off, and it does not cover a packet on the network. A laptop can have a locked disk and still send a password in the clear.\n',
        'r0': "Jonah's lost laptop has no full-disk protection, so the customer list is an ordinary folder at boot. A protected sister machine uses AES-256-XTS for the sectors and asks for a PIN or a 48-digit recovery key before any directory appears. The volume key is released by a platform chip only when the boot measurements match, and the recovery key for that volume should already sit in escrow under the asset id. A raw image of the powered-off encrypted disk is ciphertext. The same image taken from a machine imaged while logged in can include plaintext files the imager could read. Check the management console for Protection On before you call the fleet encrypted. A screen lock is not this control.\n",
        'r1': 'Full-disk encryption locks the whole drive. The lock matters when the power is off. A stolen powered-off laptop should not show its files. One secret locks and unlocks the disk. After unlock, the system sees normal files. A person at the keyboard can read those files. A file copied out during the day can leave in the clear. A screen lock is a different control. Check that protection is actually on. Keep a recovery spare before the laptop ships. Powering on should not be enough by itself. Ask for a second secret at boot.\n',
        'r2': 'A warehouse pulls one shutter over the whole building at night, office included, loading floor included, trash room included. After hours, a person who takes the building still cannot read the invoices inside. In the morning the manager raises the shutter, and staff who are allowed in read the invoices normally. A thief who walks in behind the manager during the day is not stopped by a shutter that is already up. A folder carried out at noon is outside the shutter too. Sleep with the lights on and the shutter half raised is not the same as a closed night. The shutter is full-disk encryption, the whole drive locked while power is off. Once the machine is unlocked, a copied file needs some other lock.\n',
        'r4': "Open the asset record for Jonah Brooks's laptop and read the encryption status. Write down Protection On or Protection Off, the cipher if shown, and whether a recovery key id exists in escrow. Note if the loss report says the machine was asleep or powered off. Do not remote-wipe yet if the status is unknown and counsel still wants the disk. Do not accept a screen-lock setting as a substitute. Leave the recovery key in escrow rather than pasting it into the ticket.\n",
        'exam': 'Full-disk encryption protects a powered-off drive, including system, swap, and temp files. An unlocked running machine is not protected by that lock. A screen lock is not disk encryption. File encryption covers one document, not the whole drive. A raw image of a powered-off encrypted disk should be ciphertext. A management status of Off means the customer list is readable at boot. PIN plus a platform chip is stronger than a chip that unlocks the moment power returns. Escrow the recovery key or the company can lock itself out.\n',
    },
    'Partition encryption': {
        'body': 'Partition encryption locks one partition, a slice laid out by the partition table, and leaves the other slices alone. The control fits a data partition that should stay sealed while the system partition remains a separate volume, often readable so the machine can boot. The cipher is typically AES applied to the blocks of that slice only. Unlocking the slice mounts a normal filesystem. Neighboring slices do not become encrypted because this slice did.\n\nThe boot slice is the piece teams often leave in the clear, even on servers they casually call fully encrypted. Bootloaders, kernels, and sometimes the early boot scripts sit there so the machine can start before a passphrase is entered. Those files can leak host names and configuration, and a tampered bootloader can capture the passphrase. The data slice still does its own job. A copy of that slice taken while locked is ciphertext. A copy of the unlocked filesystem, or of the clear boot slice, is not.\n\nHelen Nguyen reviews a database server the ticket calls encrypted. The block list shows /dev/sda1 as an ordinary filesystem mounted at /boot, and /dev/sda2 as an encrypted container holding /var/lib/data. Customer records live on sda2 and are unreadable while the container is locked. The boot files on sda1 are readable on a bench, including an old network config that names an internal host. Helen writes both device names, both filesystem types, and both mount points into the ticket. Calling the whole disk encrypted would hide the readable slice.\n\nA defender inventories partitions and records which slices hold secrets, which slices are ciphertext at rest, and which slices must stay readable in order to boot. Encrypt every data slice, and do not stop at the first green check on a dashboard. Prefer a measured boot so a modified bootloader is noticed by the platform chip. When imaging a machine, image the locked container and the clear boot slice as separate items and label them. Do not unlock the data slice on an untrusted bench just to browse.\n\nOne locked slice is not full-disk encryption. Full-disk encryption is aimed at the whole drive so a powered-off laptop does not yield its files. Partition encryption can leave the operating-system slice readable on purpose. File encryption is narrower still, one document rather than a slice. If the question shows readable boot files and a locked data slice, the control in front of you is partition encryption.\n',
        'r0': "On Helen's database server, /dev/sda1 is an unencrypted filesystem at /boot and /dev/sda2 is the encrypted container for /var/lib/data. The data slice uses AES on its blocks and stays ciphertext until a key is supplied at mount time. The boot slice remains readable so the loader can start before that key exists. A bench copy of sda1 shows the kernel and a network config with an internal host name. A bench copy of locked sda2 does not show customer rows. Measured boot can hash the bootloader into a platform chip so a swapped loader fails the unseal. Write both device names in the ticket. One encrypted partition does not make the other partition ciphertext.\n",
        'r1': 'Partition encryption locks one slice of the disk. Other slices can stay open. The open slice is often the one needed to start the machine. Boot files on that slice can still be read. A locked data slice stays scrambled until someone unlocks it. Copying the locked slice does not reveal the rows. Copying the boot slice can reveal names and settings. A changed starter program can steal the passphrase. Check every slice, not only the first green mark. One locked slice is not the whole drive.\n',
        'r2': 'The warehouse locks one cage and leaves the front office open. The cage holds the customer files. The office holds the lights, the switch list, and a map of internal phone numbers. A person who cuts the power and hauls the cage away still cannot read the files. A person who walks into the office can read the map. Locking the cage does not lock the office. The office stays open so the lights can come on in the morning. A tampered switch list could ask staff for the cage code and pocket it. The cage is one sealed slice of the disk, with another slice left readable. A shutter over the whole building would be a wider lock, and a single sealed folder would be a narrower one.\n',
        'r4': 'Run a block listing on the database server and read each device, type, and mount. Write down /dev/sda1 at /boot and /dev/sda2 for the data container, including which one is encrypted. Note any host name readable from the boot slice. Do not unlock sda2 on the bench yet. Do not rewrite the ticket as full-disk encryption. Leave the boot files unchanged while you record that they are readable.\n',
        'exam': 'Partition encryption seals one slice and can leave another slice readable, often the boot slice. Readable boot files plus a locked data slice are not full-disk encryption. Full-disk encryption aims at the whole drive while the power is off. File encryption is one document, not a partition. A clear bootloader can still leak configuration or capture a passphrase. Measured boot is how a platform chip notices a swapped loader. Do not mark the server encrypted after seeing a single locked device name. Image the locked slice and the clear slice as separate labeled items.\n',
    },
    'File encryption': {
        'body': "File encryption locks individual files. That is the right control when one spreadsheet must travel, or must sit on a shared disk other people can browse. The rest of the folder can stay readable. A sound pattern encrypts the file with AES-256 and wraps that AES key to a person's public key, so only that person's private key opens the file. A password-based file lock also exists, and then the password has to travel as carefully as the file, which people often fail to arrange.\n\nThe lock follows the file. A ciphertext copy in mail, on a share, or on a laptop stays ciphertext. Full-disk encryption does not give you that property. Once a user copies a document off an unlocked disk in the clear, the disk's encryption is no longer in the path. File names and sizes may still be visible. A temp copy the editor wrote beside the original may be plaintext even while the saved file is locked. Those leftovers are part of the review.\n\nAisha Patel needs the payroll workbook on a finance share the whole department can list. Aisha encrypts that one file to the finance lead's public key and leaves the rest of the home folder in the clear. The share shows salaries-week12.xlsx.p7m beside ordinary notes that open in an editor. The ticket says the workbook is encrypted to fingerprint 3F91A2C4 and that a coworker who can list the folder still cannot read salaries. A second file, salaries-week12.xlsx~, is a plaintext editor backup sitting beside the sealed workbook. That backup is the miss a review should catch.\n\nA defender names the files that leave a protected disk and encrypts those files, rather than hoping the disk lock follows them. Check that the wrapped key names the intended recipient, that leftover temp files are not plaintext, and that the password or private key is not stored in the same directory as the ciphertext. On a shared disk, file encryption is what keeps one workbook private. Record the key fingerprint in the ticket. Do not decrypt the file on the share just to confirm the contents.\n\nThe disk being encrypted does not make an emailed attachment safe. The disk lock ends when the file is copied out in the clear. File encryption is also not partition encryption. Neighboring files stay readable, which is what the finance share requires. Transport encryption can protect the mail hop while the attachment lands in the mailbox as plaintext. The attachment needs its own lock if the mailbox is not the only place the file will sit.\n",
        'r0': "Aisha's share lists salaries-week12.xlsx.p7m, an envelope whose content key is AES-256 wrapped to the finance lead's RSA-2048 public key, fingerprint 3F91A2C4. The matching private key is the only practical way to unwrap that AES key and then the workbook. Beside it, salaries-week12.xlsx~ is a plaintext editor leftover, readable by anyone who can open the share. The file name and size of the sealed workbook are still visible. Full-disk encryption on Aisha's laptop would not have followed a clear copy onto this share. A password-based office lock would require the same password to travel out of band. Record the fingerprint in the ticket and remove the leftover only after the plaintext exposure is noted.\n",
        'r1': 'File encryption locks one file. The rest of the folder can stay readable. The lock travels with the file. Mail and shared folders keep the locked file locked. A locked disk does not follow a clear copy. Names and sizes may still show. An editor can leave a clear spare beside the locked file. Look for that spare. Lock the file to the person who should open it. Do not store the secret in the same folder. A protected trip does not lock the attachment by itself.\n',
        'r2': "Most jars in the kitchen sit open so the cooks can work. One jar that holds the payroll recipe is sealed, and only the finance lead's key opens that seal. The jar can ride to another kitchen and stay sealed. The open jars beside it stay readable the whole way. A cook who photographs the sealed jar gets the label and the shape, not the recipe. A sticky note of the recipe beside the jar is still readable, and the seal does not cover the note. A lock on the kitchen door does not follow the jar once a cook carries it out. The sealed jar is one document locked while the rest of the folder stays usable. The trip between kitchens does not replace the seal on the jar.\n",
        'r4': 'List the finance share and find salaries-week12.xlsx.p7m. Write down the recipient fingerprint and whether a plaintext twin such as salaries-week12.xlsx~ is present. Note who can list the folder. Record the visible file name too. Do not decrypt the workbook on the share. Do not delete the leftover until its presence is in the ticket. Leave neighboring notes unchanged. Neighboring notes are supposed to stay readable.\n',
        'exam': 'File encryption locks chosen files and can leave the rest of a folder readable. A locked disk does not protect a file copied off that disk in the clear. A protected mail hop can still deliver a plaintext attachment. Neighboring readable files mean this is not partition encryption and not full-disk encryption. Watch for editor leftovers beside the sealed file. The wrapped key should name the intended recipient. Do not store the password or the private key next to the ciphertext. Visible file names are not the same as visible contents.\n',
    },
    'Volume encryption': {
        'body': "Volume encryption locks a logical volume as one unit. The volume might be an LVM logical volume, a virtual disk, or a cloud block device, and it may not line up with a single physical partition. While the volume is locked, its blocks are ciphertext, usually under AES. After a host unlocks and mounts the volume, processes on that host read ordinary files. A second volume on the same machine is a separate decision. Forgetting the second volume is a common miss.\n\nExports cross the boundary. A database dump, a csv extract, or a scratch area on a different volume is not covered by the first volume's key. Snapshots of the locked blocks stay encrypted. Backups that read through the mount point capture plaintext unless those backups encrypt on their own. An administrator who can open the mount can read everything on that volume. Volume encryption is an at-rest control against a stolen disk or a copied block device. It is not a control against a logged-in admin, and it is not a control against a file the admin copied elsewhere.\n\nOwen Diaz audits the customer-data host. The mapper list shows vg-customer unlocked and mounted at /srv/customers, with encryption reported on. A second volume, vg-scratch, is mounted at /srv/export as a plain filesystem. The nightly job writes a full customer extract to /srv/export/customers.csv. The ticket that says the data volume is encrypted is true and incomplete. A snapshot of the scratch volume, or a stolen copy of that disk, yields the extract in the clear. Owen records both mount points, both cipher states, and the path of the extract.\n\nA defender inventories every volume that can hold a copy of sensitive data, including scratch, swap, and backup staging. Encrypt each of those volumes, or stop the job that writes plaintext onto them. Record the volume name, the cipher, and where the key lives. Confirm that snapshots and replicas use the same protection. A logical backup is not safe merely because the source volume is encrypted. If the backup was taken through the filesystem, inspect the backup's own lock.\n\nA volume is not a partition, a database engine, or a single column. A partition is a slice in the partition table. A volume is a logical unit that may sit on one disk or across several. Database encryption is the engine locking its own files, which still does not encrypt a csv the engine wrote onto a clear volume. Record encryption locks one column and leaves the volume's other files readable. A mounted data disk beside a forgotten scratch disk is a volume-encryption problem.\n",
        'r0': "Owen's host mounts an encrypted logical volume vg-customer at /srv/customers and a clear volume vg-scratch at /srv/export. The nightly job writes /srv/export/customers.csv in plaintext. A snapshot of vg-customer's locked blocks would be AES ciphertext. A snapshot of vg-scratch is a readable extract, and so is any backup that copied the csv through the mount. The volume key for vg-customer does not wrap vg-scratch, because each logical volume has its own lock. Swap and backup-staging volumes belong on the same inventory. Record both mapper names and the extract path. An admin who has already mounted vg-customer can read it. The lock is at rest, not against that admin.\n",
        'r1': 'Volume encryption locks one logical disk as a unit. A second disk on the same machine is a separate choice. Locked blocks stay unreadable while the volume is shut. After the volume is opened, programs read normal files. A copy written to another volume is not covered. Scratch space and swap can leak that way. Snapshots of locked blocks stay locked. Copies made through an open folder may not. List every volume that can hold a sensitive copy. A locked source does not lock an export.\n',
        'r2': 'One walk-in cooler is locked as a unit. Every shelf inside that cooler is covered while the door is shut. A second cooler, used for boxes heading to the dock, has no lock. Staff who open the first door can read every shelf in that cooler. A printout carried into the second cooler is not protected by the first door. Pictures of the locked cooler from outside stay unreadable. Pictures taken after the door is open capture plain pages. The locked cooler is one logical volume under one key. The dock cooler is a different volume. A locked cooler does not protect a page that someone carried out of it.\n',
        'r4': "List mapper names, mount points, and encryption flags on the customer host. Write down vg-customer, vg-scratch, and the path /srv/export/customers.csv. Note whether snapshots of each volume are ciphertext. Record the cipher name for each volume. Do not delete the extract yet. Do not assume the source volume's key covers the scratch volume. Leave both volumes mounted as they are until the inventory is in the ticket.\n",
        'exam': 'Volume encryption locks one logical volume, which may span disks and may not match a partition. A second volume is a second lock. Scratch, swap, and export volumes are easy to forget. Snapshots of locked blocks stay encrypted. A filesystem copy or a csv extract onto a clear volume does not. An admin who mounted the volume can read it. That is not database-file encryption and not a single encrypted column. If the data volume is sealed and the export volume is not, name both volumes in the finding.\n',
    },
    'Database encryption': {
        'body': "Database encryption locks the database's own files or a tablespace so a copied data file is not readable without the key. Engines often call the feature transparent data encryption. The engine encrypts pages on disk, commonly with AES-256, and decrypts those pages for a query after the instance has opened the key. A person who steals the file, or a raw backup of that file, gets ciphertext. A person who can log in and run a query still sees plaintext rows. Those are different events, and only one of them is stopped here.\n\nThe key must not sit in the same backup set as the data files. A typical design keeps a master key in a key management system or a hardware module, and a data-encryption key wrapped by that master. Restore steps have to name both the files and the key id. A logical dump, the kind that writes insert statements or csv, is a query result. The dump is plaintext unless the dump job encrypts its own output. Turning on file encryption and then shipping a logical dump leaves the dump unprotected.\n\nChris Berg finds a database backup disk on a shelf. The files are the engine's data files, copied while the instance was stopped, and the pages were stored in the clear. Readable strings in those files show customer names and national identifiers. The ticket notes the backup path and that the tablespace encryption flag was off, with key id none. If transparent encryption had been on, the same copy would look like high-entropy pages, and a restore would fail until the key from the management system was supplied. Chris records the file type as a raw data file, not a logical dump, because the fix and the exposure differ.\n\nA defender enables tablespace or file encryption for databases that hold sensitive rows, and stores the master key outside the data backup. A restore onto a clean host should demand that key. Inspect backup jobs so a logical dump is not the plaintext twin of an encrypted tablespace. Alert when encryption is switched off, and when a backup disk also holds an unwrapped key. A successful query by the application account is supposed to work. That query is not evidence the files are safe at rest.\n\nDatabase encryption does not hide columns from an administrator who can query the live instance. Record encryption keeps a chosen field ciphertext even in query results, until a holder of the field key decrypts it. Volume encryption can cover the same bytes when the files live on an encrypted volume, but a logical export written somewhere else leaves that volume in the clear. Match the control to the theft: a copied data file, a query, or an export.\n",
        'r0': "Chris's shelf disk holds raw database files with tablespace encryption off and key id none, so strings in the pages show customer names. Transparent data encryption would have stored those pages under AES-256 and required a master key, held in a key management system or a hardware module, before a restore would succeed. The data-encryption key should be wrapped by that master and should not ride in the same backup folder. A logical dump of insert statements is a different object. The dump is the output of queries and stays plaintext unless the dump file is encrypted on its own. Test the restore on a clean host and expect it to stop until the key id is supplied. A working SELECT from the app account does not prove the files are protected at rest.\n",
        'r1': 'Database encryption locks the database files on disk. A copied file should be unreadable without the key. The running database still answers allowed questions in the clear. A person who can log in can read rows. Keep the master key outside the file backup. A text export is not the same as a copied data file. That export can be clear even when the files are locked. Test a restore and expect a demand for the key. A working login is not proof the files are safe. Watch for a backup that includes the key beside the files.\n',
        'r2': 'The clinic keeps charts in locking cabinets. A person who wheels a locked cabinet out the door cannot read the pages. During the day, a nurse with the cabinet key and a reason can read a chart, because the work requires the cabinet to open. A photocopier in the hall that prints a chart makes a page the cabinet no longer protects. The cabinet key should not be taped to the back of the cabinet. Locked cabinets are the database files sealed at rest. An authorized lookup still returns plain rows. A photocopied chart is an export, and the export needs protection of its own.\n',
        'r4': 'Inspect the backup disk and note whether the files are raw database pages or a logical dump. Write down the tablespace encryption flag and the key id, including none if that is the value. Check whether the key file sits on the same disk. Do not restore the backup onto a shared server yet. Do not turn encryption on until you know where the master key will live. Leave the shelf copy unchanged so the readable strings remain evidence.\n',
        'exam': 'Database encryption makes copied data files unreadable without the key. A successful query still returns plaintext. That is not record encryption, which keeps a chosen column locked even in the result. A logical dump or csv export can be plaintext even when the tablespace is encrypted. Do not store the master key in the same backup as the pages. Key id none plus readable strings in the pages means the files were stored in the clear. A restore test should demand the key.\n',
    },
    'Record encryption': {
        'body': 'Record encryption locks specific fields, such as a national identifier column, while other columns stay usable in the clear. The application or the database encrypts those values before they rest, commonly with AES-256 under a key held outside the table. A report can still group by name or city. The sensitive field, read without the key, is ciphertext. The control is narrower than encrypting the whole database file, and it still applies when a query returns the row.\n\nThe mode changes what an attacker learns. Randomized encryption makes equal values look different, which blocks equality searches and hides duplicates. Deterministic encryption makes equal values look the same, so a match is possible and so is a leak of which rows share an identifier. Keeping a few digits in the clear for a screen is a product choice, and those digits are not protected. The key belongs in a management service. If the application logs the row after decrypting it, the log undoes the column.\n\nNora Kim builds a customer report that must show names and cities and must not show national identifiers. The identifier column is encrypted and the name column is not. The table shows a short name in plaintext and a long opaque value in the identifier column. The report query never selects the identifier, so the output file has neither ciphertext nor a raw identifier. An analyst role that lacks the key receives the opaque value unchanged if that role selects the column. The ticket records the column name national_id, the key id in the management service, and the decision that names remain readable on purpose.\n\nA defender picks the columns whose disclosure is the harm, encrypts those columns, and records who may decrypt them. Prefer randomized encryption unless a real lookup needs deterministic matches, and then document the equality leak. Keep the key out of the database backup. Search logs and exports for the plaintext form of the column. Do not decrypt the column into a view every reporting account can select. Test with an account that should lack the key and confirm the result is still ciphertext.\n\nStudents call this database encryption. Database encryption locks the files and then hands plaintext to any successful query. Record encryption leaves neighboring columns readable and keeps the chosen field locked in the query result until a holder of the key decrypts it. Tokenization is a later neighbor: a token is a stand-in that maps back only inside a vault, and it is not ciphertext you decrypt with a key you hold. If names still run in reports and the identifier does not, the control is record encryption.\n',
        'r0': "Nora's customer table stores name in plaintext and national_id as AES-256 ciphertext under a key id in the management service, not in the table. Randomized mode makes two equal identifiers look different, so an attacker cannot group duplicates by staring at the column. Deterministic mode would allow an equality match and would reveal those duplicates. The report query selects name and city only, so the output file never receives the column. An analyst without decrypt permission who selects national_id sees the opaque value, not the digits. A log line written after the application decrypts the field would undo the control. Do not confuse this column with tablespace encryption, which still returns every column in the clear to a successful query.\n",
        'r1': 'Record encryption locks one field. Other fields can stay readable. Reports can use the open fields. The locked field looks scrambled without the key. A query does not unlock it by magic. Only a holder of the key can read it. Equal secrets should not look identical unless you chose that on purpose. A repeated look can leak who shares a value. Keep the key outside the table backup. Do not write the clear value into a log. Test with an account that should not read the field.\n',
        'r2': 'Each clinic chart shows the name and the city in ordinary ink. The national identifier sits under a small lock on that one line. A report of names and cities never opens the small lock. A reader without the line key sees a smudge where the identifier belongs and still sees the name. Opening the whole cabinet is a different act from opening that one line. Two patients with the same identifier should not be obvious from matching smudges, unless the clinic picked a lock that repeats on purpose. A photocopy of the chart after someone unlocked the line brings the secret back. The line lock keeps one field sealed while neighboring columns stay usable. A cabinet that opens for any successful lookup reveals every line, which is a wider control.\n',
        'r4': 'Select the customer table with an account that should not decrypt, and read the national_id value. Write down the column name, the key id, and whether two known-equal identifiers look the same. Check recent logs for a plaintext identifier. Do not add a view that decrypts the column for every report user. Do not switch the mode until the equality requirement is written down. Leave the name column readable. That readability is the design.\n',
        'exam': 'Record encryption seals chosen fields and can leave other columns readable in query results. Database encryption seals files and then shows every column to a successful query. A report on names that never returns the identifier fits the field lock. Randomized encryption hides duplicate values. Deterministic encryption reveals which rows match. Those leftover clear digits are not protected. A decrypting view or a log of the plaintext undoes the column. Tokenization uses a vault lookup, not a key you apply to ciphertext yourself.\n',
    },
    'Transport encryption': {
        'body': "Transport encryption protects data while it moves between two systems, so a listener on the path cannot read it. TLS is the usual tool. The handshake authenticates the server with a certificate and agrees on a session key, often with ephemeral elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman on P-256. After the handshake, a symmetric cipher such as AES-GCM encrypts the bytes. The listener sees ciphertext, addresses, and sizes, not the password. Each endpoint still holds plaintext. This path lock does not encrypt a disk, a database file, or a mailbox at rest.\n\nPort 443 is the ordinary port for HTTPS. A login page on port 80 is HTTP, and the password crosses the network in the clear. Prefer TLS 1.3. TLS 1.2 remains common and acceptable when the cipher suite is modern and the key exchange is ephemeral. Static RSA key transport, which encrypts the session secret to the server's long-term key, lacks forward secrecy. A later theft of that private key decrypts recorded sessions. Certificate checks matter as much as the cipher. Encryption toward an attacker whose certificate you accepted is still encryption, and it is to the wrong party.\n\nEvan Cole joins a cafe network and opens the company login page. The address bar shows http rather than https, and the form posts to port 80. A packet capture on that network contains the password as readable text beside Evan's username. The same login under TLS would show port 443, a certificate name that matches the site, and an application-data record that is not the password. The ticket should quote the URL scheme and the port. A password field by itself is not a finding. The cleartext post is the finding.\n\nA defender forces encryption on every page that carries a secret and refuses the plaintext fallback. A browser policy that allows only HTTPS, modern cipher suites, and ephemeral key exchange keeps recorded traffic safer if the server key leaks later. Alert when a service accepts a login on port 80, or when a certificate name does not match the host the user asked for. The ends of the pipe still matter. After the server decrypts the request, the password is in memory and may be in a log. Strip secrets from logs. The path lock never claimed to protect that log.\n\nThe session is not encrypted with the server's public key for the whole visit. The handshake creates a session secret, and AES encrypts the bulk bytes because AES is fast. Transport encryption is also not at-rest encryption. A database file on the server is unchanged by the fact that clients use TLS. A cipher suite does not prove the browser reached the real server. The certificate check does that work. Key exchange alone does not.\n",
        'r0': "Evan's cafe capture shows a login POST to port 80 with the password in clear text beside the username. The repaired service listens on port 443, prefers TLS 1.3, and uses an ephemeral P-256 or X25519 exchange plus AES-GCM for application data. TLS 1.2 is acceptable only with a modern suite and an ephemeral exchange. Static RSA key transport must go, because a later copy of the server private key would decrypt today's recorded sessions. The certificate name has to match the host Evan requested. HSTS tells the browser not to fall back to http. After decryption, the server must not write the password into the access log. Port 443 does not encrypt the database file at rest.\n",
        'r1': 'Transport encryption protects data on the trip. A listener on the path should not read the password. The two ends still see the clear message. A login page with no protection lets the cafe read the password. Look at the address line and the capture. A locked disk is a different problem. A locked database file is a different problem too. The fast lock on the trip is a shared session secret. The handshake sets that secret up. Check the name on the certificate. The wrong party can still run a lock. Do not store the password in the server log.\n',
        'r2': "An armored van carries a sealed case from the clinic to the bank. People on the road see the van and cannot read the papers. The clinic still has those papers in the out tray before pickup, and the bank has them in the in tray after delivery. A clerk who sends the same papers in an open pickup lets the street read them. The van does not lock the filing room at either building. The van also fails if the driver never checks the bank's badge and hands the case to a lookalike. Badge checking is separate from the sealed case. The van is protection for the trip, not for the rooms at either end. A sealed case does not prove the recipient, and a correct badge does not hide papers that rode in an open truck.\n",
        'r4': "Open Evan Cole's capture and read the URL scheme and destination port. Write down port 80 or port 443, whether the password bytes are readable, and the certificate name if a handshake exists. Note any log line that stored the password after decryption. Do not turn off the login while you write the finding. Do not tell users to click through a name mismatch. Leave the packet capture intact as evidence.\n",
        'exam': 'Transport encryption hides data on the path and leaves plaintext at both ends. Port 80 with a readable password is a failed trip, not a disk problem. The bulk of the session uses a shared secret and a fast cipher, not the server public key on every byte. Ephemeral exchange keeps old recordings safe if the long-term key is stolen later. Static key transport lacks forward secrecy. A cipher suite does not prove identity. The certificate name proves the host. A database file and a server log are outside this control.\n',
    },
    'Asymmetric encryption': {
        'body': "Asymmetric encryption uses a key pair. One key locks or checks. The other key unlocks or signs. The public key is the shareable half, and the private key stays secret. RSA and elliptic-curve schemes are the families to know. RSA with a 2048-bit modulus is a common minimum for a long-lived pair. ECDSA and ECDH on P-256 are the elliptic-curve choices you will see most often in TLS, with smaller keys for comparable strength. Neither family should encrypt a large file directly. The operations are slow, and the message size is limited. The practical pattern wraps an AES key.\n\nLocking and signing are different operations. To lock a message to Mara, use Mara's public key. Only Mara's private key opens it. To sign a message as Mara, use Mara's private key. Anyone who holds Mara's public key can check the signature. Using the private key is not a confidentiality operation. The private-key operation to name here is the signature. Padding matters. RSA encryption should use OAEP, not a raw exponentiation that leaks structure. A signature names its hash, and SHA-256 is the hash to expect. MD5 is not acceptable.\n\nMara Chen receives the salary workbook as mail locked to her public key. The object in the mail store is an encrypted envelope addressed to her RSA-2048 key id. Teammates cannot open that envelope. Only the private key on Mara's workstation, which is not in the mail store, produces the workbook. The envelope's recipient field matches her certificate, and the spreadsheet's magic bytes do not appear inside the mail blob. A shared password would have left both the sender and the mail administrator holding the secret that opens the file. That design is symmetric encryption, and it is a different control.\n\nA defender chooses asymmetric encryption when the sender must not hold the recipient's secret. That is the normal case for mail to a person and for wrapping keys to many recipients. Protect the private key, require RSA 2048 or an accepted curve, and reject retired padding and retired hashes. Use the public key to encrypt and the private key to sign. For bulk data, generate a fresh AES-256 data key, encrypt the file with AES, and lock only the data key to the public key. Rotate the pair if the private key is copied.\n\nAsymmetric encryption is not what makes a large download fast, and it is not a synonym for one shared secret. Symmetric encryption carries the bulk bytes. Asymmetric operations set up a key or prove a signature. Students who lock a file with the private key and check it with the public key have described a signature, not confidentiality. If both sides use one secret to lock and unlock, the question is symmetric encryption, even when a public key delivered that secret earlier.\n",
        'r0': "Mara's mailbox holds an envelope addressed to her RSA-2048 public key, not a workbook her teammates can open. RSA-OAEP wraps a fresh AES-256 data key, and AES-256 encrypts the workbook. The private key that unwraps the data key stays on Mara's workstation. A signature from Mara would use that same private key over a SHA-256 hash, and other people would check it with the public key. ECDSA or ECDH on P-256 is the smaller alternative you will see in TLS, and it does not change which half is secret. Do not encrypt the workbook under RSA directly. Do not use MD5 as the signature hash. A single shared password for the file would be symmetric encryption instead.\n",
        'r1': 'Asymmetric encryption uses two different keys. You may share one half. The other half stays secret. The shared half locks a message to a person. The secret half opens that message. The secret half also makes a signature. The shared half checks the signature. Do not swap those jobs. These keys are slow for a big file. Lock a small secret, and let a fast cipher lock the file. A shared password for both directions is a different lock. Protect the secret half like a crown jewel.\n',
        'r2': "The clinic gives Mara a mailbox with an open drop slot and a different key for the door. Anyone can slide a thin envelope through the slot. Only the door key pulls mail out. A stamp on Mara's personal ring can mark a letter as hers, and the sample card above the box lets other people compare the mark. The sample card cannot mark a new letter. The drop slot cannot empty the box. A thick crate will not fit through the slot, so staff put the crate's padlock key inside the envelope instead. The slot and the sample card are the shareable half. The door key and the stamp are the secret half. Two different keys are the idea, and one padlock key shared by both sides would be another kind of lock entirely.\n",
        'r4': "Open the mail object addressed to Mara Chen and read the recipient key id. Write down the key size, the wrapping method, and where the private key is stored. Confirm the workbook bytes are not visible inside the blob. Do not decrypt the message in the mail store. Do not export Mara's private key to check the envelope. Leave the envelope encrypted until the workstation with the private key is the place doing the open.\n",
        'exam': 'Asymmetric encryption uses a public half and a private half. The public half encrypts to the owner and checks signatures. The private half decrypts and signs. Do not treat a private-key operation as confidentiality. RSA 2048 is a common minimum. P-256 is a common elliptic-curve size, not a replacement word for a shared password. Bulk data stays under a fast shared cipher. Only the small key is locked to the public half. MD5 is the wrong signature hash. If one secret both locks and unlocks, the question is symmetric encryption.\n',
    },
    'Symmetric encryption': {
        'body': 'Symmetric encryption uses one shared secret to lock and to unlock. AES-128 and AES-256 are the ciphers to teach and to deploy. They are fast enough for disks, backups, and the bulk of a network session. The same key has to exist at both ends, or at the same disk when the two ends are now and later. If that key leaks, every ciphertext it protects is open, in both directions. There is no public half you can safely post.\n\nThe mode is part of the control. GCM and other authenticated modes hide the bytes and detect tampering. ECB mode encrypts each block alone, so repeated plaintext stays visible as a pattern, and ECB is the wrong choice for real records. Disks often use XTS, which is built for sectors. The key still has to live somewhere safer than the ciphertext. A laptop can keep the disk key in a platform chip. A backup can wrap the AES key under a management service. Emailing the key in the same message as the file erases the benefit.\n\nSamir Rahman images a finance laptop whose disk uses AES-256. One key seals and unseals the sectors. The platform chip holds that key and releases it at boot when the measurements match. The key is not mailed to Samir. A copy of the powered-off drive shows ciphertext rather than the customer list. The status screen reads AES-256, a key protector of the platform chip, and a recovery-key id already in escrow. A raw AES key written into a text file on an unencrypted boot slice would ride along inside the image, next to the ciphertext, and the lock would be decorative.\n\nA defender standardizes on AES-128 or AES-256, refuses ECB for structured data, and keeps the key out of the same store as the ciphertext. Generate keys in a management service or a chip, not in a spreadsheet. Rotation means re-encrypting or re-wrapping, then confirming the old key is retired after the new key works. Alert when a config file contains a key beside the path of the file that key unlocks. The cipher name on a slide is the easy part. The secret is the control.\n\nStudents confuse this with hashing, or with a key pair. SHA-256 is a fingerprint. A hash has no key and no inverse that restores the file. Asymmetric encryption uses two halves, and one half may be published. Symmetric encryption fails if you publish the only key. Bit lengths do not compare across families. AES-256 is a 256-bit symmetric key. RSA-2048 is a 2048-bit modulus. Both can be appropriate, and they are not substitutes.\n',
        'r0': "Samir's finance laptop encrypts sectors with AES-256-XTS. One key performs both directions, and the platform chip releases that key only when boot measurements match. ECB must not be the mode for the customer records, because identical blocks would repeat in the ciphertext. GCM is the authenticated mode to expect when the same AES key protects a network session or a file that needs tamper detection. A 128-bit AES key is also an accepted choice. The failure mode is a copy of the raw key in a text file on a clear boot slice, visible in the same image as the ciphertext. Escrow a recovery key. Do not email the AES key beside the file. SHA-256 cannot decrypt this disk, because a hash is not a cipher.\n",
        'r1': 'Symmetric encryption uses one shared secret. The same secret locks and unlocks. A shared secret is the fast choice for bulk data. There is no half you can safely publish. If the secret leaks, old locked data opens too. Hide the secret away from the locked data. A chip or a guarded service should hold it. Do not mail the secret with the file. A fingerprint of a file is not this kind of lock. You cannot unlock a fingerprint. Two different keys would be a different design. Pick a modern lock and a sane size.\n',
        'r2': 'One padlock closes the clinic supply trunk, and the same key both closes and opens it. The night nurse and the morning nurse need copies of that key, or the trunk stays shut. There is no harmless picture of the key to post on the door. A posted picture is enough for a stranger to cut a working copy, because the picture and the key are the same secret. A lost key ring means every past use of that lock is open. A wax seal on a bag is not this padlock, because the seal cannot be opened back into the letter. The trunk lock is one shared secret for both directions, fast enough for bulk storage. Publishing that secret ends the protection, since no second half can stay public.\n',
        'r4': 'Read the laptop encryption status with Samir Rahman and note the cipher and the key protector. Write down AES key length, mode, and the recovery-key id location. Search the boot slice for a text file that contains a raw key. Do not copy the raw key into the ticket if you find one. Do not email that key to the imaging share. Leave the disk key in the chip while you record the status.\n',
        'exam': 'Symmetric encryption uses one shared key for both directions. AES-128 and AES-256 are the ciphers to accept, and they are fast enough for bulk data. There is no public half. Publishing the key breaks every ciphertext under it. ECB leaks repeated patterns. A hash such as SHA-256 is not decryption. Asymmetric encryption uses two different halves. Do not compare an AES key length with an RSA modulus as if they were the same measure. A key stored beside the ciphertext is a failed control even when the cipher name is modern.\n',
    },
    'Key exchange': {
        'body': "Key exchange lets two parties agree on a shared secret over an open path without sending that secret. Diffie-Hellman and elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman, written ECDHE when the values are ephemeral, are the methods to name. Each side holds a private value and sends a public value. Each side combines its own private value with the other side's public value and arrives at the same secret. An observer who copies both public values still cannot compute the secret when the group is large enough. AES then encrypts the data under that secret. The exchange itself is not the bulk encryption.\n\nForward secrecy depends on the private values being ephemeral. ECDHE on P-256, generated for one session and then discarded, means a later theft of the server's long-term signing key does not decrypt old sessions. The long-term key's job is the signature that proves identity. An exchange by itself does not prove who you reached. Signatures prove identity. Static Diffie-Hellman reused for many sessions, and old RSA key transport that encrypts a premaster secret to the server's long-term key, both fail forward secrecy. A recorded handshake plus the long-term private key is then enough to recover the session.\n\nLeah Ward's browser and the company site agree on a session key in the TLS handshake, then use AES for the rest of the visit. The connection details show a suite such as TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 and a group such as X25519 or P-256. Those are separate lines: the bulk cipher on one line, the exchange group on the other. A weak partner site negotiates static RSA and no ephemeral group. Traffic captured there today could be decrypted next year if that server's private key is stolen. Leah's strong session discarded the ephemeral private values when the session ended, so the same future theft would not open this recording.\n\nA defender requires ephemeral Diffie-Hellman or ECDHE, disables static RSA key transport, and rejects small groups. A 1024-bit Diffie-Hellman group is a known weakness. Record the negotiated group and the cipher in the scan, not only the letters TLS. After the handshake, application data should be under AES with an authenticated mode. Do not accept a design that sends the session key itself across the path. The exchange exists so the secret is computed on both sides and never transmitted.\n\nAgreeing on a session secret did not authenticate the server, and that agreement did not encrypt the page by itself. Identity comes from a signature checked against a certificate. Page confidentiality comes from AES under the agreed secret. RSA encryption of a session key is key transport, not Diffie-Hellman. In key transport, the client creates the secret and the server does not add its own ephemeral private value. If a later theft of the server key decrypts last month's traffic, forward secrecy was not in use.\n",
        'r0': "Leah's strong session negotiates TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 with group X25519 or P-256. The group is the ephemeral Diffie-Hellman exchange. The AES-GCM suite is the bulk cipher that uses the resulting secret. ECDHE discards the private values at the end of the session, so a later theft of the server's signing key does not decrypt the recording. A partner still offering static RSA key transport fails that test: the premaster secret was encrypted to the long-term RSA key, and a stolen copy of that key opens old captures. Reject a 1024-bit Diffie-Hellman group. The certificate signature, not the exchange, proves the server's name. Write both the suite and the group into the scan ticket.\n",
        'r1': 'Key exchange lets two sides share a secret safely. The two sides never send the secret itself. Each side keeps a private piece and sends a public piece. Both sides end with the same secret. A listener who hears the public pieces still lacks the secret. The secret then feeds a fast lock for the real data. The agreement does not prove who the other side is. A signature does that job. Throw away the private pieces after the session. Then a later stolen long-term key cannot open old talks. Do not send the session secret across the wire.\n',
        'r2': "Two clerks need the same secret paint, and they must shout across a courtyard. Each clerk picks a private color and never shows it. Each clerk mixes that private color into a public base and sends only the mix. Each clerk then blends the other side's mix with the private color that stayed in the shop. Both arrive at one shared color. Listeners hold both mixes and still cannot name the result. Nobody shouted the final color. The clerks then use that color as the key on a fast trunk lock for the heavy crates. Agreeing on the color did not prove which clerk was standing there. A signed badge does that job. The color agreement is how a shared secret is created in the open, and the trunk lock that follows is a separate step for the bulk load.\n",
        'r4': "Open Leah Ward's connection details and copy the cipher suite and the group. Write down whether the group is ephemeral and whether static RSA key transport appears. Note the certificate name separately from the group. Do not disable the site during the read. Do not call the session authenticated just because a group was negotiated. Leave recorded traffic in the evidence store. A later key theft must not be able to open it if the private exchange values were discarded.\n",
        'exam': "Key exchange, by Diffie-Hellman or ECDHE, creates a shared secret without sending it. The exchange does not prove identity. A signature proves identity. AES encrypts the bulk data after the secret exists. Forward secrecy requires ephemeral private values that are discarded. Static RSA key transport fails that test, because the long-term private key can decrypt old recordings. A 1024-bit group is too small. Do not describe RSA encryption of a session key as Diffie-Hellman. If last month's traffic falls open when the server key is stolen, forward secrecy was absent.\n",
    },
    'Algorithms and key length': {
        'body': "An algorithm is the method, and key length is the size of the secret that method uses. AES, RSA, ECDSA, and SHA-256 are methods. AES takes a 128-bit or 256-bit key. RSA is judged by the modulus, and 2048 bits is a common minimum. A P-256 elliptic-curve key is 256 bits on a curve whose attack cost is not the attack cost of an RSA modulus. A sound goal with a retired method is still a weakness. DES, RC4, and export-grade ciphers are not redeemed because a cipher of some kind was involved.\n\nLengths do not travel across families. RSA at 1024 bits is too small for new work. AES-128 remains strong for bulk encryption, and AES-256 is the conservative choice for long-lived data. ECDSA and ECDH on P-256 are common in TLS and stronger than a naive comparison with a 256-bit number would suggest. A 256-bit RSA key would be trivial, and it is not something to accept. A 256-bit AES key is the large choice inside AES. Writing 256 on a diagram without the algorithm name is how a review approves the wrong thing. MD5 and SHA-1 are retired when you need collision resistance, so they are not an acceptable integrity method next to SHA-256. SHA-256 is the hash to teach. A hash length is not an encryption key length.\n\nRenee Cho's partner gateway still offers an export-grade suite from the era of short keys a modest attacker can break. The scan lists that suite beside a modern one. Renee's team refuses the old suite and requires ephemeral elliptic-curve exchange with AES-256-GCM, and SHA-256 on the signature path. The change ticket quotes the rejected suite name and the accepted suite name, so a later compatibility request cannot flip the old suite back on without a record. Compatibility with a broken method is how the broken method survives.\n\nA defender keeps an allow list of algorithms and lengths and scans for departures. Disable DES, 3DES, RC4, export ciphers, RSA moduli under 2048 bits, and SHA-1 signatures on new certificates. Write the finding as the algorithm plus the length, not as a vague note that crypto looks weak. When a vendor says the product is encrypted, ask which algorithm, which mode, and which length. A longer key inside a broken algorithm is not a fix. RC4 does not become acceptable by adding bits. Replace the retired method with a current one.\n\nThe trap is the cross-family comparison. Students see RSA-2048 and AES-128 and crown a winner from the integers alone. Those two answers do different jobs, and both can be acceptable together. SHA-256 names a hash output, not an AES key. The phrase we encrypt is not a completed review. An export-grade cipher is encryption and is also a weakness. The separating fact is whether the method is still sound and whether the length fits that method.\n",
        'r0': "Renee's scan of the partner gateway shows an export-grade suite next to a modern one. The accepted target is ephemeral ECDHE, AES-256-GCM for the bulk data, and SHA-256 for signatures. RSA moduli below 2048 bits stay disabled, while RSA-2048 remains a common minimum. AES-128 is still a sound symmetric choice. DES, 3DES, RC4, and SHA-1 signatures are not. A P-256 curve key is not weak merely because the number 256 is smaller than 2048. Those numbers belong to different algorithms. MD5 is broken for collisions, so a fingerprint in MD5 does not clear a file. The ticket must name the rejected suite and the accepted suite, or compatibility pressure will turn the short key back on.\n",
        'r1': 'The method and the size of the secret both matter. A lock from a retired method is still weak. A short secret on a good method can be too small. Numbers do not mean the same thing on every method. A big number on a broken method is not a repair. Ask which method, which size, and which mode. A fingerprint method is not a lock. Old fingerprint methods can be forged. Write down the name you refused and the name you accepted. Compatibility is how bad methods linger. Do not approve a diagram that only says a number.\n',
        'r2': 'A kitchen can believe in locks and still hang a toy latch a child can pop. The latch is the method. The thickness of the shackle is the size of the secret. A thick shackle on a latch made of soft candy is still a bad latch. A thin wire on a method the trade retired does not become sound because someone calls it a lock. The pantry stamp pad seals bags, and the stamp pad is not a lock even if a large number is written on the pad. Replacing the toy means a current method and a size that fits that method. Adding digits to the old toy does nothing. The latch and the shackle are the method and the secret size. A big number borrowed from a stamp is not a secret size for the latch.\n',
        'r4': 'Read the partner scan and list every suite still offered. Write down the export-grade suite as rejected and the accepted suite beside it. Record algorithm, mode, and length as three separate fields. Do not enable the old suite for a compatibility test. Do not accept a vendor slide that says encrypted without those three fields. Leave production traffic on the accepted suite while you file the finding.\n',
        'exam': 'Judge the algorithm and the length together, and do not compare lengths across families. RSA-2048 is a common minimum modulus. AES-128 and AES-256 are symmetric key sizes, not RSA sizes. P-256 is an elliptic-curve choice, not a short RSA key. SHA-256 is a hash, not an encryption key. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for collisions. DES, RC4, and export-grade suites are retired methods, not fixed by a larger number. A product that merely says it encrypts has not named a sound method. The ticket needs the rejected suite and the accepted suite.\n',
    },
    'Trusted platform module': {
        'body': "A trusted platform module is a chip on one computer, or a firmware module bound to that one machine. The chip stores keys and records measurements of the boot path so a disk key can be sealed to the platform. The module releases the key only when those measurements match. The key is not a file you copy onto the next laptop. Replace the motherboard or clear the chip, and the disk will not unseal. TPM 2.0 is the generation to expect. The chip can hold RSA and elliptic-curve keys and can perform a limited set of operations. It is not a fast shared appliance for every server in a building.\n\nSealing is the behavior that matters. The volume key is wrapped to the chip and to platform configuration registers. At boot the chip extends those registers with hashes of the firmware and the bootloader. Matching hashes let the chip unwrap the volume key, sometimes only after a PIN. A mismatch makes the machine ask for the recovery key. A thief who boots a stolen laptop with chip-only protection, and who does not change the boot path, may get the disk without knowing a password. A PIN closes that gap. In a normal configuration the chip will not export the private key to the operating system as a copyable blob.\n\nThe desktop team replaces a failed motherboard on Ruth Ahmed's laptop. The next boot asks for a recovery key, because the chip identity changed and the sealed key will not release. The ticket text is motherboard replaced, recovery key required. That recovery key should already live in escrow, not in Ruth's memory. A sister machine whose disk key is only a typed passphrase would not care which chip is soldered down. This laptop cares which chip is soldered down. Write the old and new board identifiers in the ticket before anyone clears the chip.\n\nA defender seals disk keys to the module, escrows the recovery key before any hardware change, and adds a PIN where a stolen laptop must not unlock itself. Do not clear the chip as a troubleshooting step until the recovery key is confirmed in the vault. Record that the module is present so a machine without one is not treated as sealed. Alert when protection falls back to a password on a sticker. The module is local to one computer. It does not manage keys for a fleet of application servers.\n\nStudents call this chip a hardware security module. A hardware security module is a separate appliance or network device that many systems use for high-value keys, and private keys are used in place and not exported. A trusted platform module belongs to one computer and seals keys to that platform. A secure enclave is a third object: a protected region inside a processor that can handle secrets even when the main operating system is untrusted. A recovery prompt after a motherboard swap points at the module on that machine, not at a shared network appliance.\n",
        'r0': "Ruth's laptop uses a TPM 2.0 to seal the disk key. After the motherboard swap, boot measurements no longer match, so the firmware asks for the recovery key instead of unsealing AES disk sectors. Chip-only mode would have unlocked a stolen machine whose boot path was left intact. Add a PIN so power-on is not sufficient. The recovery key, often 48 digits for this class of disk product, must already be in escrow under the asset id. Clearing the chip before that check destroys the chance to unseal. The module will not serve the payment cluster. A hardware security module is a different device, shared by many hosts, and a secure enclave is a protected region inside a processor, not this soldered chip.\n",
        'r1': 'A trusted platform module is a chip on one computer. The chip holds keys for that computer. The chip can seal a disk key to startup measurements. If the board changes, the disk asks for a recovery spare. The spare should already be stored safely. Do not clear the chip before you confirm the spare. Power-on alone should not unlock a stolen laptop. Add a second secret at startup. This chip is not a shared box for many servers. This chip is not a locked room inside the main processor either. Write down the old board and the new board.\n',
        'r2': "One house has a lock cylinder buried in its own front door. The cylinder releases the inner key only when the door, the frame, and the hinge pins match the shape they had when the lock was set. Swap the slab for a new door, and the cylinder refuses, even though the furniture never moved. The owner then needs the spare code the building office already stored. A neighbor's house has a separate cylinder. You cannot unscrew this one and mail it downtown for ten branch offices to share. A stranger who finds the original door untouched may still need a second code, or the door may open just because the shape matches. The buried cylinder is a chip bound to one computer. A shared vault that many branches call is a different object, not this door.\n",
        'r4': "Read Ruth Ahmed's boot prompt and the desktop ticket after the motherboard replacement. Write down the old board id, the new board id, and whether a recovery key exists in escrow. Note if the disk unlocks on power alone or also asks for a PIN. Do not clear the chip yet. Do not order a shared network vault as if it were this chip. Leave the disk locked until the escrowed recovery key is confirmed.\n",
        'exam': "A trusted platform module is one computer's chip. The chip seals keys to that platform's measurements. A motherboard change or a cleared chip makes the disk ask for the recovery key. Escrow that key first. Chip-only unlock can open a stolen laptop that still boots the same way, so add a PIN. Do not call this chip a hardware security module. The hardware module is a separate device many systems share, and it uses keys without exporting them. Do not call it a secure enclave. An enclave is a protected region inside a processor.\n",
    },
    'Hardware security module': {
        'body': "A hardware security module is a dedicated appliance, a plug-in card, or a network service that creates keys, stores them, and performs cryptographic operations without exporting the private key. Many application servers can call one module. That sharing is the opposite of a platform chip soldered to a single laptop. Payment keys, certificate-authority signing keys, and the private keys for important TLS servers are the usual residents. Opening the hardware is designed to zeroize the keys. Administrators authenticate to the module. They do not copy a PEM onto an application server and call the job done.\n\nThe application sends the bytes to be signed or unwrapped. The module returns the signature or the unwrapped data and keeps the private key. Config files show a library path for a token API such as PKCS#11, a network address of the module, and a key label or handle. The private key does not appear in that config. A backup of the module is a wrapped blob that only a quorum of custodians can restore onto another module. Throughput and dual control are why the box exists. A laptop chip cannot serve a cluster of payment hosts, and that chip is not administered as a shared vault.\n\nImani Yusuf runs the payment service. Transaction signatures use a key that lives only in the module. An administrator who can read every file on the application server still cannot copy the private key. The server stores the label pay-2026 and the module address, not the key bytes. The module audit log shows a sign operation, that label, and the service account, with no export event. If the same key had been a PEM on the app server, the administrator's backup of the home directory would have included it. The ticket for a new payment host should show the module address and the label. A reviewer should reject a PEM path in that change.\n\nA defender generates high-value keys inside the module, denies export, and splits administrative control so one person cannot both authorize and extract. Point applications at key labels rather than key files. Review the audit log for unexpected sign or decrypt calls. A stolen application disk image should contain no private key. A restore drill should prove the wrapped backup returns on a second module and that one custodian cannot open it alone. An application that merely speaks TLS has not placed its private key in a module. Look at where the key bytes live.\n\nThe module is not a trusted platform module and not a key management system. The platform module is one computer's chip and seals a disk key to that platform. This module is a separate device shared by many systems, and its private keys are used in place. A key management system creates, rotates, retires, and authorizes keys, and it may call a module as the vault underneath. Owning the box does not create a rotation policy. If an admin with the app server's disk still cannot take the payment key, the control is the hardware module.\n",
        'r0': "Imani's payment hosts store the label pay-2026 and the network address of the hardware security module, reached through a PKCS#11 library. The private key is generated as non-exportable. A sign call returns a signature. An export call should be absent from the audit log. A stolen image of the app server contains no PEM for that label. Restore of the module uses a wrapped backup and a quorum of custodians, not a single operator with a spreadsheet. Tamper response is meant to zeroize keys if the chassis is opened. A FIPS 140-3 citation on the purchase ticket speaks to module testing and does not replace the export ban. This box serves many hosts. A TPM on one laptop does not.\n",
        'r1': 'A hardware security module is a separate box for keys. Many systems can ask that box to sign or unlock. The private key stays inside. Admins should not copy the key onto an app server. The server should store a label, not the key itself. A stolen copy of the app server should lack the key. Opening the box the wrong way should destroy the keys. Two people should be needed to restore a backup of the box. A chip on one laptop is not this box. A rotation policy is a different system. Look at where the key bytes actually live.\n',
        'r2': 'Downtown, a vault room serves every branch. Tellers send a slip in and receive a stamped slip back. The stamp die never leaves the room, and no teller desk keeps a copy of the die. Opening the room the wrong way is built to destroy the dies. Two officers must agree before a stored die is brought back into service. One laptop in one branch cannot be this room, because the room is separate and many branches use it. A policy binder that says when dies are replaced is also not the room. The binder can sit on a shelf while the dies stay inside. The vault room is a separate device that uses private keys without exporting them. The binder is the lifecycle around those keys, and the binder is not the steel room.\n',
        'r4': 'Read the payment host config and the module audit log for label pay-2026. Write down the module address, the label, and whether any export event exists. Confirm a stolen-image test would find no PEM. Do not export the key to satisfy curiosity. Do not paste key bytes into the ticket. Leave the quorum requirement on the wrapped backup until a scheduled restore drill.\n',
        'exam': 'A hardware security module is a separate device many systems use. Private keys are created there and used in place, not exported to the app server. You should see a label and a sign event in the audit log, not a PEM on disk. Do not call this box a trusted platform module. A laptop chip is a different object. Do not call the box a key management system. The management system is the lifecycle around keys and may use the box underneath. One custodian alone must not restore the backup.\n',
    },
    'Key management system': {
        'body': "A key management system creates, stores, rotates, and retires keys so people stop emailing secrets and stop leaving old secrets valid forever. The system is a workflow and a policy point. It is not a single chip, and it is not only a locked appliance. Envelope encryption is the common pattern. A data key, often AES-256, encrypts the data. A key-encryption key inside the management system wraps that data key. Applications ask the system to unwrap a data key when they are authorized. The master key stays in the system, frequently backed by a hardware module. The audit log is part of the control. A key nobody can see being used is a key you cannot govern.\n\nRotation changes which key is active, and teams skip steps. A new version is created. New data is encrypted under the new version. Old ciphertext is re-wrapped or re-encrypted, or it stays readable by the old version for a defined window. Then the old version is destroyed so it cannot decrypt either. Disabling a version with no plan for the data that version still protects is how a database becomes unreadable. Leaving every old version enabled forever is how a stolen key from last year keeps working.\n\nGil Santos rotates the customer-table key on a 90-day schedule. The change ticket names the alias db-prod, the new version, and the previous version that remains available only until old ciphertext is re-wrapped. The console shows the alias pointed at the new version, the old version scheduled for destruction after the rewrap, and an audit row with Gil's name. Last year's failure was the opposite. The application config held a raw AES key in a source repo, and nobody could retire it without a new build. That finding quoted the repo path. The new design quotes a key id and a version.\n\nA defender puts production keys in the management system, grants decrypt rights to the smallest set of roles, and alerts on use from outside those roles. Schedule rotation, write down what happens to old ciphertext, and destroy retired versions once no data needs them. Separate the people who administer the system from the people who read the data when the data is that sensitive. Do not export a master key into a config file to make the app simpler. Review every instance the same way: alias, version, state, and the last audit event. A key that has never rotated and has no owner is a finding even when the algorithm is AES-256.\n\nA hardware security module is not this system. The module is where a master key may live and where operations may run without export. The management system is the record of creation, permission, rotation, and retirement, and more than one module could sit under it. Key escrow is narrower: a recovery copy for the day the owner is gone, not the whole lifecycle. Rotating a key in the console does not make old backups die. Old ciphertext stays readable until it is re-wrapped and the old version is actually destroyed.\n",
        'r0': "Gil's ticket rotates alias db-prod on a 90-day schedule. The new version wraps fresh AES-256 data keys. The previous version stays only long enough to decrypt ciphertext that is not yet re-wrapped, and it is then destroyed. Envelope encryption means the master key-encryption key never leaves the management system, often resident in a hardware module, while applications receive a data key only when authorized. The audit row names Gil, the alias, and the version. A raw AES key in a source repo has no version and cannot be retired without a code change. Disabling a version before the rewrap plan is written can make the database unreadable. An alias with no owner and no rotation date is a finding even though AES-256 itself is sound.\n",
        'r1': 'A key management system births, stores, and retires keys. People should not email secrets around. A data key locks the actual files. A master key locks that data key. The master stays in the system. Apps ask permission before they can unwrap anything. Rotation means a new version for new data. Old locked data still needs the old version until you re-lock it. Then destroy the old version. A box that only holds keys is not the whole system. A spare for a lost owner is narrower too. Write down the alias, the version, and the owner.\n',
        'r2': 'The building key office issues, records, replaces, and collects keys for every lock in the plant. A clerk does not stuff a new key into a desk and forget it. The office writes who may request a copy, which version is current, and the day the old version must come back. Old keys still open old doors until those doors are re-pinned. Collecting the metal without re-pinning the door leaves the old key as a way in. Leaving every retired key active means a stolen key from last year still works. The strongbox under the counter may hold the master blanks. The office is the process around that box. The key office is the lifecycle of create, permit, rotate, and retire. A strongbox with no dates and no owners is not that lifecycle.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the console entry for alias db-prod and read the active version. Write down the owner, the last rotation date, who can decrypt, and the state of the previous version. Find whether old ciphertext has a rewrap plan before destruction. Do not disable the previous version yet. Do not copy the master key into the application repo. Leave the alias in place while the ticket records version and audit details.\n',
        'exam': 'A key management system is the lifecycle: create, authorize, rotate, and retire. A hardware security module may store the master key and refuse export, and the module is not the lifecycle. Key escrow is only a recovery copy for a missing owner. Rotating the active version does not erase old ciphertext. Destroy the old version only after rewrap, or the data becomes unreadable. Leaving every old version enabled lets a stolen old key keep working. A raw key in a source repo has no retirement path. An alias with no owner is still a finding.\n',
    },
    'Secure enclave': {
        'body': "A secure enclave is a protected region inside a processor that can handle secrets even when the main operating system is compromised. Code in that region uses memory the ordinary kernel cannot read. A phone can match a fingerprint inside the enclave and return only yes or no to the app. The biometric template stays in the region. A rooted app that dumps ordinary process memory still does not receive the template. Keys created inside the region can be marked non-exportable, including a device key on the P-256 curve, so the operating system can ask for an operation and never receive the private value.\n\nThis region is not a trusted platform module, and it is not a hardware security module. A trusted platform module is a chip on one computer that stores measurements and can seal keys to that platform's boot state. A hardware security module is a separate appliance or network device that many servers share for high-value keys. An enclave lives in the processor package and serves that one device. It does not rotate a company catalog of keys, and it does not replace transport protection or full-disk encryption. If the enclave's own code measurement fails, the region should fail closed and refuse the secret operation.\n\nJamal reviews field phones for a clinic staff program. Ticket MOB-77 describes a rooted test handset running a memory scraper against the login app. The scraper writes a dump of the app process. Jamal searches that dump for the biometric template and does not find it. The device log at 09:42 records a match result of yes and a template export denial. The app received a boolean, not the template bytes. The same phone holds a non-exportable P-256 key that signs a device check-in. Copying the app files to a lab laptop does not produce a usable private key.\n\nDefenders keep templates and device keys inside the enclave, refuse export, and measure enclave code before trusting a match. A rooted phone is a reason to wipe secrets and re-enroll, not a reason to pull the template into the app for debugging. Alerts should fire if a build tries to log template bytes or raw key material. Students mix the enclave up with the platform chip and with the shared appliance. A question about a processor region that still withholds a biometric template after the operating system is compromised is about the enclave. A question about a motherboard chip that seals a disk key to boot measurements is about the trusted platform module. Payment keys kept on a network appliance that many servers call belong to the hardware security module.\n",
        'r0': "The field phone keeps the biometric template inside a processor enclave and returns only yes or no. A non-exportable P-256 device key signs check-in and never leaves that region. The memory dump on ticket MOB-77, taken at 09:42, contained no template bytes, and the log recorded the export as denied. A trusted platform module is a different chip: it seals keys to one computer's boot measurements. A hardware security module is a shared appliance for many servers, not a region inside this handset. The enclave does not rotate a fleet of AES-256 data keys, and it does not replace a protected session on port 443. If the enclave's code measurement fails, the operation should fail closed instead of handing key material to the operating system.\n",
        'r1': 'A protected processor region can hold secrets. The main system can fall and the region stays shut. A fingerprint match can happen inside that region. The app should receive only yes or no. The template should not show up in a memory dump. A rooted app is not a reason to copy the template. This region is not the chip sealed to one whole computer. This region is not a shared vault for many servers. Disk locks are a separate control. Network locks are a separate control. If the region fails its own check, it should stay closed.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic with a small locked room built inside the main treatment hall. Nurses in the hall can ask whether a fingerprint matches, and the room answers yes or no through a slot. The paper template never comes out, even when someone has propped the hall doors open and is reading every chart on the desks. The inner room is not the drug safe bolted in the basement, and it is not the armored truck the hospital uses for valuables shared by many buildings. If the room fails its own lock inspection, the slot stays shut. Staff do not photocopy the template.\n\nIn the real device, that inner room is the protected processor region, and the open hall is the main operating system. A match result can leave the region while the biometric template stays inside.\n',
        'r4': "Inspect the device log for the match result and the export denial. Search the app memory dump and record whether template bytes were absent. Write the ticket number and the time beside that finding. Confirm the device key cannot be exported. Do not paste template bytes or key material into the ticket. Do not rename this region as a motherboard chip or a shared appliance in the write-up. If the region's own check fails, leave the secret operation refused until the cause is known.\n",
        'exam': "The enclave is not a trusted platform module and not a hardware security module. The platform chip seals keys to one computer's boot state. The shared appliance sits outside the phone and serves many systems. A yes-or-no match is not a copy of the biometric template. Exporting the template for debugging removes the control. A failed code measurement should fail closed. The enclave does not replace disk encryption or a protected network session. Any hardware box that stores a key is not automatically this processor region.\n",
    },
    'Steganography': {
        'body': "Steganography hides a message inside something that looks ordinary, so the existence of the message is the secret. A chat photo can carry extra bits and still open as a picnic picture. Encryption would have turned the attachment into ciphertext that looks random and raises an immediate question. Hiding aims at a different result: a casual viewer should not notice that a second message is present. The carrier is often a picture. A common method edits the least significant bit of many pixels, or appends bytes after the picture's end marker. The visible scene stays plausible. The method does not lock the hidden bytes, and it does not prove who sent the file.\n\nDetection looks for carriers that are slightly wrong. A simple picnic photo several times larger than similar photos is a reason to inspect the file. Data sitting after the end marker, or low bits that look unnaturally smooth, is another reason. An analyst can still miss a careful job, so a normal thumbnail is not proof of innocence. If the hidden payload was encrypted before it was tucked into the picture, finding the bytes still leaves a locked blob. That combination is both concealment and encryption, and the picture was only the disguise.\n\nNora handles a chat alert on ticket IR-220. The attachment picnic.png is 2.4 megabytes, while other photos of the same outing in that channel are near 400 kilobytes. The image opens and shows a picnic table. A file viewer shows a zip archive beginning after the picture end marker. Inside the archive is a short text file with a server name and a schedule. Nothing in the chat text mentioned a file transfer. The thumbnail gave no hint. A fingerprint of the picture would change if those extra bytes changed, but the fingerprint would not announce that a message was concealed.\n\nDefenders treat odd media as content, not as decoration. Compare file size with the scene, look past the end marker, and keep outbound pictures inside data-loss checks when policy already covers file transfer. Blocking every picture is rarely the useful response. Investigating the outlier is the useful response. Students who say the photo is locked with a hash, or encrypted because it is a special file, have mixed three ideas. Encryption makes a message unreadable and usually obvious as ciphertext. A fingerprint shows whether bytes changed. Hiding tries to keep the fact of the message unnoticed. A question that says the bytes look like random noise is about encryption. A question that says a normal photo quietly carries a second file is about steganography.\n",
        'r0': "Nora's chat attachment picnic.png is 2.4 megabytes, against about 400 kilobytes for neighboring photos of the same scene. The hidden zip begins after the PNG end marker, which is a concrete place to look, not a cipher. Least-significant-bit edits are another way to tuck bits into pixels while the picnic scene still looks normal. SHA-256 of the carrier will change if those hidden bytes change, but the digest does not reveal that a second message exists. AES-256 would have made the attachment look like ciphertext instead of a photo. The concealment also does not prove a sender, because no RSA 2048 or ECDSA P-256 signature is required for the hiding step.\n",
        'r1': 'Hiding a message is not the same as locking it. The secret can be that the message exists at all. An ordinary chat photo can carry hidden bits. The photo still looks like a normal picture. Locked text would look like random noise. A very large photo of a simple scene is a clue. Extra data after the picture ends is a clue. A file fingerprint does not describe this hiding. The hiding method does not prove who sent the photo. Look at odd pictures instead of trusting them. Do not use hiding as the only protection.\n',
        'r2': "Think of a lobby painting that looks like a normal landscape. A folded note sits behind the canvas, and visitors walk past because the frame looks calm. The note is not inside a locked box on the wall. If the canvas bulges or the frame is much heavier than the other frames, a guard has a reason to look. The painting does not prove which visitor tucked the note there. A wax seal on an envelope would have announced that something secret was in transit. This frame tries not to announce anything.\n\nIn the real transfer, the painting is the ordinary photo and the note is the hidden file. The point is concealment of the message's existence, which is different from locking the message or stamping a fingerprint on it.\n",
        'r4': 'Open the chat attachment and note the picture size next to similar photos. Look for data after the picture end marker and record what you find. Do not install or run anything tucked into the carrier. Write the ticket number, the file name, and the size. Separate this finding from a locked blob and from a file fingerprint in your notes. Do not delete the carrier until the responder has a copy. Check whether outbound picture reviews already cover this channel.\n',
        'exam': 'Steganography hides the existence of a message. Encryption hides the content and usually looks like random ciphertext. A file fingerprint detects change and does not conceal a second file. A normal thumbnail is not proof that no payload is present. Finding hidden bytes can still leave a locked payload if encryption was applied first. The hiding method does not identify the sender. A question about random-looking ciphertext is not a question about a carrier that still looks ordinary.\n',
    },
    'Tokenization': {
        'body': 'Tokenization replaces a sensitive value with a stand-in that maps back to the original only inside a protected vault. The register, the receipt store, and the support tool keep the stand-in. The card number itself stays in the vault. The stand-in is not a scrambled form of the number that a key can unlock on any machine that holds the key. It is a new value, often random, chosen by the vault and saved beside the original in a table. A person who copies the register database gets tokens and cannot rebuild card numbers from them. A person who also copies the vault map can.\n\nThe vault should sit on a separate system, accept a small set of roles, and log every reversal. A token may be shaped like the original so older fields do not break, but a familiar shape is not reversibility. If the application can recompute the card number from the token with a formula and no vault, the stolen database is useful again and the design is no longer a vault lookup. Tokenization also does not decide what a person sees on a screen. A screen can show a fragment while the database holds a token. Those are two controls with two jobs.\n\nLuis reviews a payment incident on ticket PAY-19 at a retail store. The register log line reads token tok_9f3a2c, last four digits 4242, vault payments-vault. The column card_token in the store database contains that token and no card number. A developer asked the vault to reverse the token from a laptop role named pos-debug, and the vault denied the call. The denial is in the vault audit log at 15:06 UTC with the reason role not permitted. The real number never entered the ticket, because Luis had no reversal rights either. He recorded the token, the denial, and the vault name.\n\nDefenders limit who may reverse a token, alert on reversals from new roles, and keep the vault off the register network except for the allowed call. A dump of the store database is still serious, and it should not be a dump of card numbers. Students confuse a token with ciphertext and with a masked display. Ciphertext is the original value transformed so the same key can turn it back wherever the key is present. A mask is a partial or fake value shown to a person. A token is what systems store so they can refer to the secret without holding it. If the question says the stand-in is useless without the vault map, the control is tokenization.\n',
        'r0': 'The store database column card_token holds tok_9f3a2c, and the card number stays in payments-vault. Ticket PAY-19 records a reversal denial at 15:06 UTC for role pos-debug. That token is a vault lookup, not AES-256 ciphertext, so a copy of the register files does not yield the primary account number. RSA or any other key pair is not what turns the token back into the card number. Only the vault map does, and only for a permitted role. A last-four display such as 4242 can appear in the log without being the token and without being the full number. Log every successful reversal the same way the denial was logged.\n',
        'r1': 'A token stands in for a secret value. The real value stays inside a protected vault. A cash register can keep the stand-in. The stand-in is useless outside that vault. Only allowed staff should reverse the swap. Each reversal should land in a log. A stolen register file should not show card numbers. Locking the number with a cipher is different. Showing a partial number on screen is different. A leaked vault map defeats the swap. Limit who may ask the vault for the original. Write down every role that can reverse a token.\n',
        'r2': 'A theater coat check takes a coat and hands back a numbered tab. The tab is useless as clothing. Only the rack in the locked closet can turn that tab back into the right coat, and the attendant writes the name of anyone who asks. A clever folding of the coat into a locked suitcase would be a different trick, because the suitcase could be opened wherever the suitcase key traveled. Showing a guest a sketch of the buttons, while keeping the coat in the closet, is a third trick. The tab is the thing other rooms are allowed to hold.\n\nIn the real payment flow, the numbered tab is the token and the closet rack is the vault map. Systems can store the tab, and only a permitted reversal returns the original card number.\n',
        'r4': 'Read the register log and write down the token, the vault name, and the last four digits. Confirm the store database column does not contain a full card number. Check the vault audit for reversal attempts and note allowed and denied roles. Do not ask the vault to reverse a token just to fill in the ticket. Do not paste a recovered card number into chat. Record whether the stand-in is a vault lookup rather than a locked copy of the number.\n',
        'exam': 'A token is a vault stand-in, not ciphertext and not a screen mask. Ciphertext can be unlocked wherever the key is. A mask shows a fragment to a person and may not be a stable vault reference. The token is useless without the vault map. A familiar shape does not make the token reversible by a formula. Reversal is a privileged, logged action. A stolen register database should not contain card numbers when tokenization is in place. Calling the token a cipher misses the vault.\n',
    },
    'Data masking': {
        'body': "Data masking shows a fake or partial value so people can work without seeing the real secret. Support staff can see the last four digits of a national identifier while the full value stays in a guarded column. The mask is a presentation rule or a substituted copy. It is not, by itself, a statement that the stored production value has been locked. Dynamic masking decides at read time, from the caller's role, to return a fragment or a scrambled stand-in. Static masking builds a separate dataset, often for a test refresh, in which the sensitive column is replaced with fake values that do not reverse to the real ones.\n\nA screen that hides digits does not protect a stolen backup of a clear column. If production still stores the national identifier in the clear, and only the support application masks it, the backup is the exposure that matters. Masking is also not a payment token. A token is a stable stand-in that a vault can map back to the original for a system that must refer to the same secret. A last-four fragment is not unique, and it is not what a vault would redeem. Using the two words as synonyms hides which control failed.\n\nOn ticket HD-4401, support agent Luis opens a caller record and the national identifier field shows a string that ends in 6789 with the earlier digits hidden. The production column remains in the guarded table, and the role support_last4 is the policy the database applies. The same week, a test refresh of the reporting database writes ID-FAKE-00421 into that column on the copied row. Analyst Dana compares a sample of the test copy with production and confirms that no real identifier landed in the test system. She writes both facts on the ticket: the support view is dynamic, and the test copy is static.\n\nDefenders put static masking on non-production copies, bind dynamic masks to roles that need only a fragment, and list the roles that can still read the clear column. They do not treat a masked screenshot as proof the database is safe. Students mix masking with tokenization and with encryption. Encryption would store a locked form of the full value that a key can open. Tokenization would store a vault stand-in in place of the value. Masking changes what a person, or a test copy, is allowed to see. A question about last-four digits on a support screen is about masking, even when a token also exists somewhere else in the design.\n",
        'r0': "Luis's support role support_last4 sees a national identifier ending in 6789, with the other digits hidden, on ticket HD-4401. The test refresh stores ID-FAKE-00421 instead of the real value, which is static masking of the copied column. Dynamic masking is the live query rule. Neither one is AES-256 encryption of the production column, and neither one is a vault token that can be redeemed for the original. If the backup of the clear column is stolen, the screen mask does not help. SHA-256 of the fake test value only fingerprints the fake, which is not the control. List every role that can bypass the mask.\n",
        'r1': 'Masking shows a fake value or a fragment. People can finish work without the real secret. Support may see only the last four digits. The full national identifier stays guarded. A test copy should hold scrambled values. A screen mask does not lock the stored column. A stolen backup may still contain clear values. A vault stand-in is a different tool. Note which roles are allowed to see the full value. Do not copy real secrets into a test system. Ask who is allowed to turn the mask off.\n',
        'r2': 'A kitchen posts the staff recipe on a card for guests who are touring the line. Amounts are covered with tape, and a few dishes are renamed, so the guests can follow the lesson without leaving with the real formula. The taped card is not a locked box in the safe, and it is not a numbered claim tab that the pantry can redeem for the original card. A photocopy of the untaped card, taken for a practice class, should already have fake amounts written in. If someone steals the untaped original from the office drawer, the tape on the tour card did nothing.\n\nIn the real system, the taped card is the masked view a person is allowed to see. The practice copy with fake amounts is static masking, and neither one locks the original column.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the support record and write down exactly which digits are visible. Record the role name that produces that view. Check a recent test copy and confirm the sensitive column holds fake values. Do not export the clear column to prove the mask exists. List roles that can read the full value, and do not change those grants yet. Note whether production storage is still clear, locked, or replaced by a vault stand-in. Attach those notes to the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'Masking shows a fake or partial value. It is not encryption of the stored column, and it is not a vault token. A last-four support view can exist while a backup still holds the clear value. Static masking belongs on test copies. Dynamic masking is a role-based view at read time. A token maps back inside a vault. A locked column opens with a key. A question about what a person is allowed to see is the masking question.\n',
    },
    'Hashing': {
        'body': "Hashing turns data into a fixed fingerprint. The fingerprint stays the same size no matter how large the input is, the same input always yields the same fingerprint, and a tiny change yields a different one. You cannot rebuild the file from the fingerprint. The hash to teach for integrity checks is SHA-256, which produces 256 bits and is written as 64 hexadecimal characters. There is no key, and there is nothing to decrypt. Calling SHA-256 encryption is a mistake. Encryption is reversible with a key and exists to hide content. A hash exists to detect change.\n\nCollision resistance is why a file check should not rely on MD5 or SHA-1 when the sender can choose the bytes. An attacker can craft two different inputs that share one of those older fingerprints, so a match does not prove the file is the intended one when the attacker can choose the bytes. SHA-256 is the fingerprint to compare for a downloaded installer. A bare hash still does not prove who created the file, because anyone who edits the file can compute a new hash. A signature is what binds a fingerprint to a private key. Password storage is a different problem: a fast hash of a password, including a bare SHA-256, is too quick to guess, which is why a salt and a slow function exist. Those extras do not change what a file hash is.\n\nSam downloads agent.msi on ticket SW-88. The vendor notice lists a SHA-256 value of 64 hex characters. Sam hashes the local file with SHA-256 and gets a different 64-character string. The two strings diverge at the twelfth character. Sam does not install the file. The fingerprint cannot be decrypted to recover a clean installer, and the mismatch does not by itself name who altered the bytes. Sam reads the published fingerprint again from the vendor's signed release note, not only from the unmarked page that hosted the file, in case that page was changed together with the installer.\n\nDefenders publish SHA-256 for release files, verify it before install, and stop accepting MD5 or SHA-1 where a collision would let a tampered file pass. They keep hashes of golden images so a later copy can be compared. Students who say the installer is encrypted with SHA-256 have swapped a fingerprint for a lock. Students who accept an MD5 match against an attacker-controlled file have trusted a collision-broken function. A question about a one-way 64-character check of a download is about hashing. A question about unreadable content and a key is about encryption. A question about a private key proving the author is about a signature, not about the hash alone.\n",
        'r0': 'SHA-256 is the file fingerprint to teach: 256 bits, 64 hex characters, no key, and no way to rebuild agent.msi from the digest. On ticket SW-88 the vendor digest and the local digest differ from the twelfth hex character, so Sam does not install. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for collisions, meaning two different files can be built to share one older digest. AES-256 is a cipher with a shared key and is a different tool. A signature over a SHA-256 digest, using RSA 2048 or ECDSA P-256, is what names a signer. A fast SHA-256 of a password is still a poor password store.\n',
        'r1': 'A hash is a fixed fingerprint of data. You cannot rebuild the file from the fingerprint. One small change makes a new fingerprint. The same bytes always make the same fingerprint. A fingerprint does not lock the file. There is no key that opens a fingerprint. Compare a downloaded installer with a published fingerprint. Prefer a long modern fingerprint for that comparison. Old short fingerprints can collide on purpose. A fingerprint does not name the author. Passwords need more than a fast fingerprint. Do not say the installer was locked by a fingerprint.\n',
        'r2': "A warehouse stamps each outbound crate with an ink pattern taken from the crate's contents. The same contents always make the same pattern, and moving one item inside makes a new pattern. The stamp is not a padlock, and nobody can rebuild the crate by staring at the stamp. Two older, worn stamps can be pressed by a dishonest packer so that two different crates share a mark. A signature in the clerk's private book would say who packed the crate, which the ink pattern alone never says. Staff compare the stamp with the one written on the shipping notice before they load the truck.\n\nIn the real check, the ink pattern is the fingerprint of a file. It detects change, and it does not lock the file or name the author.\n",
        'r4': 'Hash the downloaded installer and write down the full fingerprint. Compare it with the value on the signed release note, character by character. Record the ticket and the file name. Do not install while the values differ. Do not accept a short old fingerprint when a long modern one is published. Do not describe the fingerprint as a lock in the ticket. Keep a copy of the release note with the comparison.\n',
        'exam': 'Hashing is not encryption. SHA-256 is the fingerprint to use for a file check, and it has no key to reverse. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for collisions. A matching fingerprint shows the bytes match the published value, not who wrote the file. A signature is the control that uses a private key. A fast fingerprint of a password is not a slow password store. If the question says the file was encrypted with a hash, the wording has swapped a fingerprint for a cipher.\n',
    },
    'Salting': {
        'body': "A salt is a unique extra value mixed in before a password is hashed, so identical passwords do not produce identical fingerprints. The salt is not a secret. It is stored beside the hash, often in the same column, where a reader of the database can see it. A length to teach is 16 bytes, which is 128 random bits, generated separately for each password. The hash then covers the salt and the password together. Two people who pick the same password get two stored results because the salts differ. One salt reused for an entire site is weaker, because a single precomputed table can be built for that salt and tried against every row.\n\nWhat a unique salt stops is a rainbow table. A rainbow table is a precomputed map from fingerprints of common passwords back to those passwords. An attacker builds it once and then looks up a stolen database in a single pass. A per-user salt means the table does not contain the fingerprint of this salt combined with the password, so the lookup misses. The attacker must compute guesses again for each person. The salt does not, by itself, make each guess slow. A fast function such as SHA-256 of the salt and the password still runs at a huge rate on a graphics card. Slowing each guess is key stretching, done with PBKDF2, bcrypt, or Argon2. Salt and stretching answer two different problems, and a password store wants both.\n\nOn ticket IAM-12 the account table shows that Alex and Blair chose the same password phrase. Alex's row stores salt 4f9c1a beside one fingerprint. Blair's row stores salt 91e07b beside a different fingerprint. A rainbow table built for unsalted SHA-256 of common passwords matches neither row. The salts sit in the clear, which is expected, because the salt's job is uniqueness rather than secrecy. The ticket also notes that the function is still a fast hash. That second finding is about stretching. It does not undo the fact that the salts already made the precomputed table miss.\n\nDefenders require a long random salt for every password, refuse one site-wide salt, and keep the salt with the hash. Identical fingerprints across many users are a sign that salts are missing. A salt is not encryption, and it does not make guessing a single password pointless. The exam trap is to swap the salt with key stretching. If the question says identical passwords must not share a fingerprint, or that rainbow tables must miss, the control is the salt. If the question says each guess must be expensive, the control is stretching. A real store should use the two together without pretending they are the same idea.\n",
        'r0': "Use a fresh 16-byte salt, 128 random bits, for every password, and store it beside the hash in the clear. On ticket IAM-12, Alex's salt 4f9c1a and Blair's salt 91e07b make two different fingerprints for one shared password, so an unsalted SHA-256 rainbow table misses both rows. The salt stops that precomputed lookup. It does not slow a graphics card that can test SHA-256 quickly. PBKDF2, bcrypt, and Argon2 are the slow functions, and they are key stretching rather than the salt itself. A single site-wide salt still lets one table be built for every user. Do not treat the visible salt as a leaked secret.\n",
        'r1': 'A salt is a unique extra mixed into a password fingerprint. Give every person a different extra. Store the extra beside the fingerprint. The extra is not a secret. Two people may choose one password. Their stored fingerprints should still differ. Prebuilt password tables then miss both rows. Those prebuilt tables are rainbow tables. One extra shared by the whole site is weaker. The extra alone does not slow each guess. A slow password check is a separate control. Look for identical fingerprints as a missing-extra sign.\n',
        'r2': 'Two patients in a clinic are born on the same day and give the same family name at the desk. Each chart gets its own long random chart number, written on the folder where any clerk can see it. A thief who printed a book of birthdays and family names finds that the book does not match those random numbers. The numbers are not a second password whispered to the patient. They also do not make the filing-room door slow to try. A heavy door that takes a long crank per try would be a different fix. The chart number only stops the preprinted book.\n\nIn the real password store, the chart number is the salt stored beside the fingerprint. It makes prebuilt rainbow tables miss, and it does not by itself slow each guess.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the password table and write down whether each row has its own salt. Confirm the salts differ for two accounts that share a password. Note that the salt is stored in the clear and do not treat that as a new leak. Check whether one salt is reused for every row. Do not reset passwords yet, and do not change the hash function in the same step. Record if the function still looks fast, and label that observation as a separate finding.\n',
        'exam': 'A salt stops rainbow tables. It is a unique extra stored beside the hash, and it is not a secret. Identical passwords then produce different fingerprints. A site-wide salt is weaker than a per-user salt. The salt does not make each guess slow. PBKDF2, bcrypt, and Argon2 are key stretching, the neighboring idea. Salting is not encryption. If the question is about precomputed lookup tables, choose the salt. If the question is about the cost of each guess, choose stretching.\n',
    },
    'Digital signatures': {
        'body': 'A digital signature is produced with a private key so anyone with the matching public key can check two facts. The holder of that private key signed the content, and the signed bytes were not edited afterward. The usual steps hash the content with SHA-256 and then sign that hash. RSA 2048 is a common minimum for the key pair. ECDSA on the P-256 curve is the other common choice, including on many web certificates. The verifier hashes the bytes now present and checks the signature with the public key. A one-byte change produces a different hash, so the check fails. A stranger who lacks the private key cannot make a replacement signature that verifies.\n\nA signature is not encryption. The script or message can stay fully readable. The public key does not unlock hidden text. It only checks the signature. A bare hash is not a signature either. Anyone who changes a file can compute a fresh SHA-256 and replace the published hash, and a reader who only compares hashes will accept the altered file. The private key is what that person lacks. The public key must also be the intended one, usually tied to a name by a certificate. Otherwise an attacker creates a new key pair, signs with it, and hopes nobody notices the signer changed. Checking a signature without deciding whose key is acceptable is an unfinished check.\n\nOn ticket REL-55 the release script deploy.ps1 carries a signature from the release mailbox at Northwind. The check tool reports a valid signature, a SHA-256 digest, and an RSA key of 2048 bits. An analyst copies the script, flips one byte inside a comment, and the check reports a hash mismatch. The script text is still plain to read, which shows the signature did not conceal it. The private key is not in the source repository. A coworker who edits the script cannot repair the signature without that key. The team refuses to run the altered copy.\n\nDefenders verify signatures before execution, restrict the signers they accept, and keep signing keys hard to copy. A hardware module is appropriate when the signatures protect widely trusted software. A compromised signing key gets revoked so later checks can reject it. Students call a published SHA-256 a signature, or they say the file was encrypted because a signature exists. The separating facts are the private key and the still-readable content. A question about a fingerprint alone is hashing. A question about a private key that makes a one-byte edit fail is a digital signature.\n',
        'r0': 'Sign the SHA-256 digest of deploy.ps1 with a private key, RSA 2048 or ECDSA P-256, and let others check it with the public key. Ticket REL-55 shows a valid signature that becomes a hash mismatch after a one-byte edit. The script stays readable, so this is not AES-256 encryption and not a bare SHA-256 comparison. Anyone can recompute SHA-256 after an edit. Only the private key can produce a new signature that matches the accepted public key. MD5 and SHA-1 are poor digest choices here because they are broken for collisions. Protect the private key, and decide which signer is acceptable before you trust the result.\n',
        'r1': 'A signature is created with a private key. Others check it with the public key. A valid check ties the file to the key holder. A valid check also shows the bytes stayed put. Change one byte and the check fails. A stranger without the private key cannot forge a new one. The file text can remain readable. Signing does not hide the file. Anyone can recompute a bare fingerprint after an edit. A recomputed fingerprint is not a signature. Keep the private key hard to copy. Check the signature before you run a script.\n',
        'r2': "A clinic clerk seals a discharge packet by signing the clerk's own name in ink across the flap, after noting a short pattern taken from the pages. Anyone can look at the pages. The ink does not hide them. If a page is swapped, the short pattern no longer agrees, and the ink cannot be honestly redrawn by a stranger who lacks the clerk's hand. A rubber stamp of the pattern alone would be weaker, because any visitor could stamp a new pattern after changing a page. The front door still has to know which clerks' hands it accepts. A copied hand is a serious loss.\n\nIn the real check, the ink across the flap is the signature made with a private key. The pages can stay readable, and a one-byte change should make the check fail.\n",
        'r4': 'Verify the script signature and write down the signer, the result, and the key size if the tool shows it. Flip no bytes in the copy that you intend to run. If you make a test copy, change one byte there and record that the check fails. Do not replace a failed check with a fresh fingerprint and call it signed. Confirm the private key is not in the shared repository. Do not add a new trusted signer while you are only investigating.\n',
        'exam': 'A digital signature uses a private key to sign and a public key to check. It is not encryption, so the file can stay readable. It is not a bare fingerprint, because anyone can recompute a fingerprint after an edit. SHA-256 is a typical digest under the signature. RSA 2048 and ECDSA P-256 are common signing keys. A one-byte change should fail the check. Trust depends on whose public key you accept. A published hash beside a file, with no private key, is not a signature.\n',
    },
    'Key stretching': {
        'body': 'Key stretching deliberately slows password hashing so guessing millions of passwords becomes expensive. People choose passwords with far less randomness than a cryptographic key, and a fast hash such as SHA-256 lets an attacker test enormous lists. Stretching repeats hard work on purpose. The functions to teach are PBKDF2, bcrypt, and Argon2. PBKDF2 runs an inner function many times, and a teaching setting is on the order of 600,000 iterations of HMAC-SHA256. Bcrypt uses a cost factor, and cost 12 is a common setting to recognize. Argon2, in the Argon2id form, is memory-hard: it demands memory as well as time. A familiar Argon2id setting uses 64 mebibytes, three iterations, and parallelism of four.\n\nThe stored result is a verifier, not an encrypted copy of the password. At login the server runs the same slow function with the stored salt and compares. Nothing is decrypted to reveal the password. A unique salt should still be present, because stretching and salting do different jobs. The salt stops rainbow tables, which are precomputed lookups. Stretching makes each live guess cost time and, with Argon2, memory. A store can salt a fast hash and still be easy to grind. A store can be slow and still reuse one salt. Both controls belong in the password store, and neither one is a substitute for the other.\n\nTicket IAM-30 shows the accounts column beginning with an Argon2id marker, memory parameter 65536, time 3, and parallelism 4. An older row begins with a bcrypt marker and cost 12. Neither row is a raw 64-character SHA-256 string. Login servers are tuned so one check takes about a quarter of a second. People barely notice, and an attacker pays that cost on every guess. If this table is stolen, the slow verifiers buy time. They do not make a terrible password safe. The response still includes a password reset, because a patient attacker can eventually test the weak choices.\n\nDefenders reject password columns that are bare fast hashes, set the work high enough for an attacker to feel it, and keep a unique salt in the verifier. They raise the cost as hardware gets faster. The exam trap is to call stretching a salt, or to call it encryption. Identical passwords producing different verifiers point at the salt. Expensive guesses from PBKDF2, bcrypt, or Argon2 are key stretching. A public ledger and a certificate authority do not slow password guesses, even though they sit near this topic in some outlines.\n',
        'r0': 'Stretch passwords with PBKDF2, bcrypt, or Argon2, not with a single SHA-256. A teaching PBKDF2 setting is about 600,000 HMAC-SHA256 iterations. Bcrypt cost 12 and Argon2id with m=65536, t=3, p=4 are the other numbers to recognize. Ticket IAM-30 shows those Argon2id parameters on the live rows and bcrypt cost 12 on an older row. One check is tuned near a quarter of a second. The salt inside the string still exists to stop rainbow tables. Stretching is the slowness. The stored string is a verifier, not AES ciphertext of the password, and a weak password can still be guessed by waiting.\n',
        'r1': 'Key stretching slows password checks on purpose. Each guess then costs noticeable time. A fast fingerprint allows huge guess lists. A slow check makes those lists expensive. The password is not stored in locked form. What is stored is a verifier for the next login. A unique extra should travel with the verifier. The extra blocks prebuilt tables. The slowness blocks rapid guessing. Use both controls together. A stolen verifier list is slower to attack. A weak password can still fall if someone waits. Pick a cost people barely notice at login.\n',
        'r2': "The side door of a warehouse has a crank lock that takes a long, tiring turn for every code you try. An honest worker turns it once in the morning and barely minds. A stranger who wants to try a suitcase of codes must stand there cranking all day. Painting a different serial number on each worker's card, so a stolen codebook of codes no longer lines up, is a separate idea. The crank does not hide the code in a locked envelope. It spends the thief's time. If the crank is removed and the door accepts a code instantly, the serial numbers alone will not make guessing expensive.\n\nIn the real password check, the long crank is the deliberate slowness of each guess. The serial number is the unique extra that defeats a prebuilt book, and both are wanted.\n",
        'r4': 'Read one stored password verifier and write down which slow method it uses. Record the cost, memory, or iteration numbers the string shows. Confirm a unique salt is present, and label the salt as a separate control. Do not replace the column with a fast fingerprint as a cleanup. Do not decrypt anything, because the verifier is not a locked password. Note whether a stolen copy would still reward guessing of weak passwords. Leave a reset decision to the incident owner.\n',
        'exam': "Key stretching is PBKDF2, bcrypt, or Argon2. It makes each password guess slow on purpose. It is not a salt. A salt stops rainbow tables and can sit beside a fast hash that is still easy to grind. Stretching is not encryption, and the stored value is a verifier. Argon2's memory use is part of the cost. A weak password can still fall if the attacker has time. A public ledger or a certificate signer does not perform this job.\n",
    },
    'Open public ledgers': {
        'body': 'An open public ledger is an append-only record that many parties can read and hold. New entries are added where the other holders can see them, and old entries are meant to stay. Each block of entries can carry a fingerprint of the previous block, commonly SHA-256, so a quiet edit breaks the chain that the other copies still show. Agreement among those copies is what stops one holder from rewriting history alone. The design fits a shared history that everyone is allowed to see, such as a public transfer between parties who do not want a single clerk to be the only record. It is a poor place for anything that must stay private.\n\nPatient notes, passwords, and raw customer files do not belong on that ledger. Writing them publishes them to every copy, and ordinary deletion is not how the ledger works. A later request to forget does not pull the bytes back from parties who already stored them. What can be published instead is a fingerprint of a private document. The document stays in the private system. The ledger shows the fingerprint, so someone who already has the document can check that it matches the announced version. The fingerprint is not the document, and posting it is not encryption of the document.\n\nPriya reviews a vendor proposal on ticket PRIV-14 for a clinic. The vendor wants patient notes written to a public ledger so the parties share one history. Priya rejects that plan in the ticket, because the notes would replicate outward and the clinic could not recall them. She allows a narrower idea. A SHA-256 fingerprint of a signed discharge summary can be appended as an anchor, while the summary itself remains in the private chart. A public viewer of the ledger would show the fingerprint and a time, not the diagnosis. The chart system still decides who may open the summary.\n\nDefenders classify data before anyone writes it to a widely copied log. Secrets and health details stay off public ledgers. An integrity anchor is allowed when the underlying file remains in a system that can still apply access rules. Students hear a ledger called secure and assume the contents are hidden. The strength here is a public, tamper-evident history, not a lock. A certificate authority, which signs a name-to-key binding, is a different actor. A question about everyone being able to read the history is about an open ledger. A question about hiding the bytes is about a cipher.\n',
        'r0': 'Blocks on an open ledger commonly chain with SHA-256, each block carrying the 256-bit digest of the previous one. Ticket PRIV-14 rejects patient notes on that public history. Priya allows only a SHA-256 anchor of a signed discharge summary, and the summary stays in the private chart. A public viewer then shows a 64-character digest and a time, not a diagnosis. AES-256 is not applied to the notes by the ledger, and a certificate authority is not what appends the blocks. Copies multiply, and there is no ordinary delete that recalls every copy. Classify the data before the write.\n',
        'r1': 'An open ledger is a history many parties can read. New lines are added and old lines stay. Many copies exist, not one private book. Shared public history fits this design. Patient notes do not fit this design. A secret written on it becomes a published secret. You should not expect to erase a public line. Post a fingerprint of a private file instead. The fingerprint post is not the file itself. This is not a locked cabinet. A certificate signer is a different job. Classify the data before anyone writes it down.\n',
        'r2': 'The town hall pins notices on a lobby board that any visitor can read. New sheets go under the older sheets, and the clerks do not take a sheet down later. Each sheet cites the sheet before it, so a person who copied the board can tell when a page was swapped. Patient folders stay in the records room. A small stamp taken from a folder may be pinned so others can confirm a copy they already hold, while the folder itself never hangs in the lobby. The board is not a locked safe. The clerk who signs identity cards at the side desk is doing another job.\n\nIn the real design, the lobby board is the open public ledger everyone can read. A stamp of a private file can be shared there without sharing the file.\n',
        'r4': 'Read the proposal and write down whether the ledger would store the records or only a fingerprint. If the records are patient notes or other secrets, mark the proposal as a rejection. Note who would hold a copy. Do not place a sample record on a public ledger to test the idea. Record the ticket number and the data type. Leave the private chart system as the place the record lives.\n',
        'exam': 'An open public ledger is a shared history many parties can read. It is not encryption, and it is not a certificate authority. Append-only means you should not plan on quiet deletion. Patient notes do not belong there. A fingerprint of a private file can be published without the file. Security in this design means a tamper-evident public history, not hidden contents. A question about hiding the bytes is about a cipher. A question about everyone reading the history is about the ledger.\n',
    },
    'Certificate authorities': {
        'body': "A certificate authority signs certificates. The signature, made with the authority's private key, binds a subject name to a public key. A relying party trusts that binding only when the authority is already trusted. Devices ship with a set of root certificates, and a company may add a few authorities of its own. A certificate signed by a stranger is rejected even when the name on it looks right. Day-to-day issuance often uses an intermediate. The root signs the intermediate, the intermediate signs the leaf, and the root private key can stay offline. The leaf is what a server presents. The authority is the signer, not the leaf itself.\n\nSigning a certificate is not the same act as encrypting a session. After a browser accepts the certificate, the browser and the server still agree on a shared secret. Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman or ECDHE can create that secret, and the exchange does not by itself prove identity. The certificate and its signature are what tie the key to the name. A symmetric cipher such as AES-256 then encrypts the data. Forward secrecy comes from discarding the ephemeral private values, not from the authority's signature. People who say the authority encrypted the website have merged steps that stay separate.\n\nOwen requests a certificate for mail.example.edu on ticket CERT-201. The authority named Example Trust signs the leaf with its intermediate. The chain file shows the leaf, then Example Trust Intermediate, then Example Trust Root. Browsers that already hold that root accept the name-to-key binding when the name matches. A lab certificate signed by a test authority Owen created on a laptop is rejected. The test log records a bad certificate from an issuer that is not in the company trust store. Owen does not install that test authority on staff laptops to clear the alert.\n\nDefenders keep the set of trusted authorities small, protect authority private keys in a hardware module, and treat a new root as a serious change rather than a shortcut. They watch for issuance they did not request. Students mix the authority with the certificate, with the signing request, and with the anchor already sitting in the trust store. The authority is the signer. The request is what you send before anything is signed. The certificate is the signed result. The root of trust is the anchor the device already believes. If a stranger's signature is refused because that signer is not trusted, the topic is the certificate authority.\n",
        'r0': 'Example Trust signs the leaf for mail.example.edu with an intermediate, and the chain ends at Example Trust Root on ticket CERT-201. The signature uses the authority private key. RSA 2048 or ECDSA P-256 are common sizes for that signing key. A test issuer absent from the trust store produces a bad-certificate alert. After the name is accepted, ECDHE can create a shared secret, and AES-256 can encrypt the session bytes. The authority signature does not perform that encryption. The signing request is not the certificate. Keep the root private key offline and the set of trusted authorities small.\n',
        'r1': 'A certificate authority signs certificates. That signature binds a name to a public key. Trust the binding only when you already trust the signer. Reject a signature from a stranger. The signer uses a private key to sign. Locking the later session is a separate step. A signature on a certificate is not session locking. A request for a signature is not a certificate yet. The anchor already stored on the device is related. Keep the signer private key in a hard box. Limit the signers your computers trust. A published list of bad certificates is not the signer.\n',
        'r2': "A building hires a notary whose stamp other buildings already accept. When the notary stamps a card, the stamp ties a person's name to that card, and the door trusts the card because it trusts the notary. A stamp from a stranger with a homemade seal is turned away. The notary is not the card itself, and the form you filled out to ask for the stamp is not the card either. The notary does not ride along and lock every conversation after you enter. The door still checks that the name on the card is the name you claimed at the desk.\n\nIn the real exchange, the notary is the certificate authority and the stamped card is the certificate. Trust in the card comes from trust in the signer, not from the signer locking the later session.\n",
        'r4': 'Write down the issuer of the leaf and each certificate above it until the anchor. Confirm the anchor is one the devices already trust. Do not install a new authority to clear a test warning. Note the subject name and whether it matches the site. Keep the authority private key out of the ticket. Separate, in your notes, the signature on the certificate from the later session lock. Record the ticket before you change any trust store.\n',
        'exam': "A certificate authority signs certificates. The signature binds a name to a public key. The authority does not encrypt the session. After trust, a key exchange creates a shared secret and a symmetric cipher protects the data. The signing request is not the certificate. The root already in the trust store is the anchor, not the act of signing. A stranger's authority is rejected. Mixing the signer up with the leaf, the request, or the session lock is the trap.\n",
    },
    'Certificate revocation lists': {
        'body': 'A certificate revocation list is a published list of certificates that an authority says are no longer trusted. The authority signs the list. Each entry is a serial number. A client downloads the whole list and searches for the serial of the certificate in front of it. If the serial is present, the client refuses that certificate. The list carries a time when a fresher copy is due. A copy older than that time can miss a revocation that happened after the copy was made. Clients often fetch the list on port 80, and they trust the contents because the authority signed the list, not because the download itself was secret. Deleting a certificate file on one laptop does not publish a revocation.\n\nThis list is not a live status check. A live check asks about one serial and receives one answer. The list is the catalog of revoked serials, downloaded and searched locally. Revocation is also not expiration. Expiration is the end date printed on the certificate. Revocation is an early announcement that the serial is untrusted even though the dates may still look current. A stolen code-signing certificate can sit inside its dates and still appear on the list. Clients that never fetch the list keep accepting it.\n\nOn ticket CERT-330 a stolen code-signing certificate, serial 4A19C208, is added to the authority list. The next update time on that list is 6 November 2026. A build server that refreshes the list refuses a signature from that serial and writes an event naming the serial and the next update time. A second build server, with checking turned off, still accepts the signature. The theft is the same in both rooms. The difference is whether the client consulted the list. Ruth files both results on the ticket so the gap is visible. She records the list address next to both server names.\n\nDefenders turn checking on, decide what a stale list should do, and prefer a failure for high-value code rather than a silent continue. They do not call the matter fixed because one workstation deleted a file. Students swap the list for a live one-serial check, or they swap it for ordinary expiration. A downloaded, signed catalog of serials is the revocation list. Asking a service about one certificate at that moment is the live status check. A question that only says the end date has passed is about expiration.\n',
        'r0': 'A revocation list is a signed catalog of serial numbers. Serial 4A19C208 was added after a code-signing key theft, with a next update of 2026-11-06, on ticket CERT-330. Clients often fetch that list on port 80 and trust it because the authority signed it. A build server that loads the list refuses the serial. A server that skips the fetch still accepts the signature. This is not a live status check, which would ask about one serial and receive good, revoked, or unknown. It is also not the certificate end date. Expiration can arrive while the list is empty. Make high-value clients fail when their copy is stale.\n',
        'r1': 'A revocation list names certificates that are no longer trusted. The authority signs the list. Each row is a certificate serial number. Clients download the whole list and look. A stolen code-signing certificate can be added. Clients that read the list should refuse that serial. Clients that never download it will not know. The list states when a fresher copy is due. An outdated copy can miss a new theft. A live question about one serial is different. Passing the end date is expiration, not revocation. Deleting a local file does not publish the list.\n',
        'r2': "The lobby wall holds a posted sheet of badge numbers the building will no longer honor. The sheet is signed at the bottom, and guards take a fresh copy rather than phoning about a single badge. If a number is on the sheet, the badge is refused even though the printed expiry is still ahead. A sheet past its replacement date can miss a badge that went bad yesterday. Tearing up one guard's photocopy does not update the wall. A phone call about one badge would be a different habit, with one answer instead of the whole sheet.\n\nIn the real check, the posted sheet is the revocation list of certificate serials. Guards who never pick up a new sheet keep honoring a badge the building has already rejected.\n",
        'r4': 'Find the certificate serial and search the current revocation list for that serial. Write down the next update time and whether the client fetched the list. Do not treat a deleted local file as a published revocation. Note if checking is turned off. Do not confuse the certificate end date with an entry on the list. Record the ticket, the serial, and where the list is fetched. Leave client settings unchanged until the owner agrees.\n',
        'exam': 'A certificate revocation list is a published, signed list of serials. Clients download the list and search it. A live status check asks about one serial and is not this list. Expiration is the printed end date, not revocation. A serial can be inside its dates and still be revoked. Clients that never fetch the list will not see the revocation. Deleting a file on one computer does not publish the list. A stale list can miss a new theft. The list is trusted because it is signed.\n',
    },
    'Online certificate status': {
        'body': 'Online certificate status is a live check that asks whether one certificate is still good, instead of downloading a whole revocation list. The check sends that certificate\'s serial number to a responder. The responder answers good, revoked, or unknown, and it signs the answer. The answer is smaller than a full list and is usually fresher. During a handshake on port 443, the server can attach a recent signed answer it already fetched. That attachment is stapling. The browser then does not call the responder itself, so the responder does not learn every site the browser opens, and the browser can still verify the signature on the answer.\n\nA missing answer is not a good answer. If the browser treats a failed check as permission to continue, an attacker who blocks the responder can present a revoked certificate while the check fails open. Important services should prefer a stapled answer and a rule that does not treat silence as success. A status of good means the responder does not list the serial as revoked. It does not mean the name matches the site, and it does not mean the chain ends at a trusted anchor. Those checks are separate. The authority signed the certificate when it was issued. This live check does not issue a new certificate. It reports status.\n\nOn ticket NET-90 a browser connects to mail.example.edu. The handshake on port 443 carries a stapled status for serial 18F2. The recorded answer is good, with an update time of 30 October 2026 and a next update of 1 November 2026. The browser does not open a second connection to the status service. When that serial is later revoked, a fresh stapled answer says revoked and the connection stops. A revocation list would have been a file of many serials. Chris notes that this exchange was one serial and one signed answer, and he files the two timestamps with the ticket.\n\nDefenders enable stapling, watch whether the servers that must fetch answers can reach the responder, and avoid a fail-open rule on high-value names. They still check the name and the chain. Students call this check a revocation list because both ideas deal with bad certificates. The list is a downloaded catalog. The live check is one signed answer about one serial. Students also read "good" as proof the site is the right site. Good means not revoked, which is narrower than trust.\n',
        'r0': 'Online certificate status is one signed answer about one serial. Ticket NET-90 shows a stapled good answer for serial 18F2 inside the handshake on port 443, updated 2026-10-30 and next due 2026-11-01. The browser does not make a separate call. The status values are good, revoked, or unknown. A later revoked answer stops the connection. Treating no answer as success lets a blocked check hide a bad serial. A good answer does not prove the hostname matches and does not replace the chain to a trusted root. A revocation list would have been the whole catalog, not this one serial.\n',
        'r1': 'Online status is a live check of one certificate. The question names one serial number. The answer may be good, revoked, or unknown. The answer is signed and can be checked. This is not a download of every bad serial. A fresh answer can ride along with the handshake. The browser then need not call out by itself. Silence is not the same as a good answer. Blocking the check must not hide a revoked serial. A good answer does not prove the name matches. Check the name and the signer separately. Prefer an answer the site already fetched.\n',
        'r2': 'A guard can telephone the front desk and ask about one badge number as a visitor arrives. The desk answers good, revoked, or unknown, and the guard notes that the answer carried a seal. The call does not ask for the entire posted sheet of bad badges. The building can also hand the visitor a fresh desk answer to carry, so the guard need not place the call. Silence on the line is not a good answer. A good answer still does not prove the name on the badge matches the person, which the guard checks by looking at the badge itself.\n\nIn the real handshake, the phone call is the live status check of one certificate. A carried answer is the fresh status the site presents, and it is not the full list of revoked certificates.\n',
        'r4': 'Capture the status answer for the one serial and write down good, revoked, or unknown. Note whether the answer arrived with the handshake or from a separate call. Record both update times. Do not treat a missing answer as a good one. Check the name and the issuer as separate lines in the notes. Do not describe this answer as a full revocation list. Leave any fail-open setting unchanged until the policy owner confirms it.\n',
        'exam': 'Online certificate status is a live check of one certificate. The signed answer is good, revoked, or unknown. A revocation list is the downloaded catalog, not this check. A stapled answer rides with the handshake. Silence is not a good status. A good status does not prove the hostname matches or that the chain is trusted. The check does not issue the certificate. A fail-open rule hides a revoked serial when the check is blocked. Students who see any revocation idea and pick the list have skipped the one-serial question.\n',
    },
    'Self-signed certificates': {
        'body': "A self-signed certificate is signed by its own private key. The issuer and the subject are the same, and nothing outside that key vouches for the name. Anyone can create one for almost any name in a few minutes. A browser warning is the expected result on the public internet, because the device has no basis for believing the signer. Trust is reasonable only when you already expected this exact certificate. You can pin it by writing down its SHA-256 fingerprint and comparing that value before you continue. Clicking through the warning because it is inconvenient trains people to ignore the same warning when a stranger presents one.\n\nA self-signed certificate is not the same thing as a certificate from a private company authority. If that authority's root is installed on the devices, the devices trust an outside signer, and the leaf is not signed by its own key. The leaf's issuer is the company authority. Calling every internal certificate self-signed mixes the two designs. A signing request is not a self-signed certificate either. The request asks an authority to sign and is not yet a certificate. A certificate of this kind is already signed by its own key and still has no outside sponsor.\n\nThe lab database lab-db.internal listens on port 443 and presents a certificate whose issuer name equals its subject name. Ticket LAB-6 stores the SHA-256 fingerprint in the lab runbook, and Ellis compares that fingerprint before accepting the lab exception. The same warning on payroll.example.edu, for a certificate nobody pinned, is a stop. The verify error says the certificate is self-signed. Ellis does not add an exception for the payroll name and does not install that certificate into the trust store to silence the alert. The lab exception stays limited to the fingerprint written in the runbook.\n\nDefenders allow this pattern only for known lab systems with a fingerprint recorded in advance. They forbid click-through on public names, and they alert when a new exception appears. Students treat the warning as a nuisance, or they treat every certificate the company made as self-signed. The separating fact is who signed. If the certificate's own key is the only signer, it is self-signed, and you trust it only when you expected that fingerprint. If a known authority signed it, the question moves to that authority and to the name.\n",
        'r0': 'The lab host lab-db.internal on port 443 presents a certificate whose issuer equals its subject. Ticket LAB-6 pins the SHA-256 fingerprint in the runbook, and Ellis compares it before any lab exception. The same verify error on an unpinned payroll.example.edu certificate is a stop. RSA 2048 as a key size does not create an outside signer. A company authority whose root is already trusted is a different design, because the leaf issuer is that authority. A PKCS#10 request is not yet a certificate. Do not import the unexpected certificate into the trust store to quiet the warning.\n',
        'r1': 'A self-signed certificate is vouched for by its own key. No outside signer stands behind the name. Anyone can create one for a name they do not own. A lab box may use one you already pinned. Compare the fingerprint with the one in the runbook. The same warning on the public internet is a stop. Do not click past that warning from habit. A company signer you chose to trust is different. That company signer is still an outside signer. The request you send for a signature is not this certificate. Trust this pattern only when you expected it.\n',
        'r2': "A visitor writes a badge, signs it in their own hand, and holds it up at the door. Nobody else has vouched for that handwriting. A public entrance should refuse it. A lab door may accept it when the expected handwriting was copied into the runbook and the guard compares the two before opening. A badge signed by the company's notary is different, because that notary is an outside signer the door was told to trust. The form that asks the notary to sign is not a badge the visitor already signed alone. \nIn the real connection, a certificate signed only by its own key is that self-made badge. Trust it only when that exact certificate was expected, and do not wave through the same warning on a public site.\n",
        'r4': 'Read the issuer and the subject and write down whether they match. If this is a known lab host, compare the fingerprint with the runbook before any exception. Do not click through a warning on a public name. Do not place the unexpected certificate into the trust store. Record the hostname, the ticket, and the verify error. Note when a company authority is the real signer instead. Leave exceptions unchanged until the comparison is written down.\n',
        'exam': 'A self-signed certificate is signed by its own key. No outside authority vouches for the name. Anyone can mint one for a name they do not control. A browser warning on the public internet is the control, not a glitch to click past. A lab exception needs a fingerprint you already expected. A private company authority that devices trust is not this pattern. A signing request is not a certificate. Clicking through trains the wrong habit. Trust this certificate only when you planned to see that exact one.\n',
    },
    'Third-party certificates': {
        'body': 'A third-party certificate is signed by an authority that devices already trust. That is the ordinary choice for a public site. The authority\'s root, or a chain that leads to it, sits in the trust store that came with the computer or the browser. The server presents a leaf for its own name. The browser checks that the signature chain ends at a trusted anchor, that the dates are current, and that the name matches the site the person requested. A trusted issuer paired with the wrong name is still a failure. A revocation list or a live status check remains part of a careful validation. The leaf is not the root, and this certificate is not signed by its own key.\n\nIssuance uses the authority\'s private key. RSA 2048 and ECDSA on P-256 are common sizes for the leaf key the certificate binds to the site name. The certificate does not encrypt the mail. After the chain and the name pass, the browser and the server still agree on a shared secret, often with an ephemeral exchange, and a symmetric cipher then protects the messages. A trusted issuer is necessary and still not the whole of validation.\n\nThe public mail service is the case to keep. On ticket MAIL-12, mail.example.edu listens on port 443 and presents a leaf issued by a public authority already in the laptop trust store. The name on the certificate is mail.example.edu, the dates include the day of the visit, and the status check returns good. A certificate for other.example.net, signed by that same authority, is rejected for this hostname because the name does not match. A well-known issuer does not repair a wrong name. Fran writes down the issuer and the matched name. She does not write only that a lock icon was present.\n\nDefenders obtain these certificates for services on the internet, renew them before the end date, and automate the renewal so a weekend expiry is not a surprise. They inventory issuers. They do not add extra roots to make an unknown issuer look trusted. Students call any certificate that "works" a third-party certificate, including a self-signed one somebody clicked through, and including a leaf from the company\'s own private authority. The separating fact is the signer. A third-party certificate was signed by an authority the device already trusted without a special exception. A self-signed certificate was signed by its own key. A matching name is still required.\n',
        'r0': 'mail.example.edu on port 443 presents a leaf from a public authority already in the trust store. Ticket MAIL-12 records a subject of mail.example.edu, dates that include the visit, and a revocation status of good. A leaf for other.example.net from the same authority fails the name check. The chain should end at a trusted root. The leaf key may be RSA 2048 or ECDSA P-256. Neither size turns a self-signed certificate into a third-party one. Do not add a root to invent trust the device did not already have. Renew before the end date rather than waiting for the outage.\n',
        'r1': 'A third-party certificate is signed by a known authority. Devices already trust that authority. Public sites normally use this kind. The mail name must match the certificate. A trusted signer plus a wrong name still fails. The dates must still be current. Revocation should still be checked. A certificate that signed itself is different. The trust anchor is not the leaf on the site. Do not add a new anchor to silence a warning. Renew before the end date arrives. Record the issuer name beside the site name.\n',
        'r2': "The public entrance accepts badges stamped by a notary the building already knows. That is the normal way guests come in. The guard still reads the name on the badge and compares it with the name the guest claimed. A famous notary and a wrong name is still a refusal. The badge in the guest's hand is not the framed list of notaries kept in the office safe, and it is not a badge the guest signed alone. The dates on the badge still have to be current. A guard who only says the stamp looks fancy has stopped too early.\n\nIn the real visit, the known notary's stamp is the third-party certificate on a public site. The name must match the site, and a trusted signer does not repair a mismatch.\n",
        'r4': 'Open the site certificate and write down the issuer and the name that matched. Confirm the issuer chains to an anchor the device already trusted. Check the dates and note the end date. Do not describe only a lock icon. Expect a wrong name to fail even when the issuer stays the same. Do not add a root to force trust. Record the ticket and the renewal date. Leave the trust store unchanged while you write the notes.\n',
        'exam': 'A third-party certificate is signed by an authority the device already trusts. That is the normal choice for a public site. A trusted issuer with the wrong name still fails. Dates and revocation still matter. A self-signed certificate is vouched for only by its own key, which is a different pattern. The leaf the server presents is not the root of trust. Adding a new root to create trust is a separate and serious act. A lock icon without a matching name is an unfinished check.\n',
    },
    'Root of trust': {
        'body': 'The root of trust is the anchor you already believe. For certificates, that anchor is usually a root certificate in the trust store. Validation walks from the leaf through any intermediates and stops when it reaches that anchor. The root is typically signed by its own key. It is trusted because a platform vendor or an administrator placed it in the store, not because a higher authority signed it. There is no higher authority in that chain. If a new root is added, certificates that chain to it can look valid, including certificates a stranger creates the same day. Some inspection tools work this way. A quiet addition on a laptop is what a compromise of trust looks like.\n\nAdding a root is a serious act. It is not a routine cure for a browser warning. A warning about a wrong name, or about a certificate signed only by its own key, is not honestly fixed by trusting a new anchor. Removing vendor roots to tidy a store will break ordinary sites, so the useful work is to notice additions and unexplained removals. A trusted platform module that measures startup is a different use of similar words. That chip seals keys to one computer. It is not a root certificate. Mixing them because the phrase overlaps selects the wrong control.\n\nOn ticket END-404 a finance laptop holds a root named Helpdesk Filter Proxy that is absent from the standard image. The thumbprint begins B702, and the install time is Tuesday. After that install, a test site whose chain ends at the new root displays as valid, even though the anchors on the standard image would not have trusted it. Gabe compares the laptop trust store with the image, records the extra thumbprint, and treats the addition as a security event until someone explains it. The leaf certificates of ordinary public sites are not what changed. The anchor changed.\n\nDefenders inventory roots, alert on additions, and ship a managed trust store so laptops do not collect local anchors. They do not install a root to silence a warning. Write the extra thumbprint in the incident record before any removal. The exam trap is to call the website leaf the root of trust, or to repair an untrusted certificate by adding whatever signer it offers. The root is the anchor already believed. The leaf is what the server presented. A new anchor changes what the computer is willing to believe.\n',
        'r0': 'Ticket END-404 finds an extra root named Helpdesk Filter Proxy, thumbprint beginning B702, missing from the standard image. After installation, certificate chains that end at that root look valid. A root is trusted because it was placed in the store, not because a parent signed it. The website leaf is not this anchor. Adding the root does not repair a name mismatch or a self-signed warning. A trusted platform module is a chip that seals keys to one computer, which is a different anchor. Inventory additions. Do not delete vendor roots merely to make the list shorter. Compare thumbprints, not display names alone.\n',
        'r1': 'The root of trust is an anchor you already believe. Later certificates chain up to that anchor. A root certificate in the trust store is the usual anchor. It is trusted because someone placed it there. No higher signer stands above it. A new anchor makes many certificates look valid. Adding one is a serious act. Compare the laptop store with the standard image. Investigate a surprise anchor the same day. Do not add an anchor to quiet a browser warning. A leaf from a website is not the anchor. A startup measurement chip is a different idea.\n',
        'r2': "Inside the office safe sits a framed list of notaries the building already believes. A badge is accepted only if its chain of stamps ends at a name on that list. The list is believed because the building put it there, not because a higher notary signed the frame. Adding a new name makes badges from that notary look acceptable, including badges printed the same afternoon. The addition is a serious act. It is a poor way to quiet a guard. The badge in the visitor's hand is not the framed list. A lockbox bolted inside one truck is another kind of anchor entirely.\n\nIn the real computer, the framed list is the root of trust in the trust store. Adding a root changes what the computer will believe, and the site certificate is only a leaf.\n",
        'r4': 'Export the roots on the laptop and compare them with the standard image. Write down any extra name and its thumbprint. Note the install time if it is shown. Do not remove vendor roots while you are only comparing. Do not add a root to silence a warning. Record the ticket and whether a test site became valid only because of the extra anchor. Treat an unexplained addition as an incident until it is explained.\n',
        'exam': 'The root of trust is the anchor already in the trust store. The website leaf is not that anchor. The root is trusted because it was placed there, not because a higher signer exists. Adding a root makes certificates that chain to it look valid. That addition does not fix a name mismatch or a self-signed warning. A trusted platform module is a different anchor, a chip on one computer. An unexplained root is a serious finding. Deleting vendor roots as housekeeping will break ordinary sites.\n',
    },
    'Certificate signing requests': {
        'body': "A certificate signing request is the request for a signature, not the certificate. An administrator creates a key pair on the machine that will use the key. The request, in PKCS#10 form, carries the subject name, the public key, and a signature made by the private key. That inner signature lets the authority see that the requester holds the private key. The private key is not part of the request. It should not be mailed, attached to a ticket, or uploaded to the authority. The authority checks control of the name and returns a signed certificate. Until that certificate is installed, clients have nothing they can trust. Putting the request file where a certificate belongs does not create trust.\n\nThe two files look alike because both are encoded and both mention the name. The request has no issuer signature from the authority. The certificate does. After the certificate comes back, compare its public key with the public key in the request so a swapped file is not installed. Extra names the site needs belong in the request, so they can appear on the certificate. A pattern that covers many hosts is a separate choice about how wide the name should be. Neither choice is a reason to send the private key away.\n\nMei builds a request for mail.example.edu on ticket CERT-18. The file mail.csr shows that subject and an RSA 2048 public key, and it does not contain the private key. She uploads only the request. The private key file stays on the mail server, readable only by its owner. The file mode on that private key is 0600. A coworker starts to attach the private key to the ticket, and Mei stops the upload. The authority later returns mail.crt. Mei compares the public key in the certificate with the public key in the request, then installs the certificate. The service is not ready while only the request file sits on disk.\n\nDefenders create keys where they will be used, send the request only, and keep private-key files tightly permissioned. They match the public keys before installation. Students who install the request and wait for browsers to trust it have skipped the authority's signature. Students who mail the private key so the authority can help have handed over the secret the process is built to keep. The request asks for a signature. The certificate is what the authority signs and returns.\n",
        'r0': 'A PKCS#10 request, mail.csr, for mail.example.edu carries an RSA 2048 public key and no private key. On ticket CERT-18 Mei uploads only that request. The private key stays on the server, mode 0600, owner-read. The authority returns mail.crt. Installing mail.csr does not satisfy clients, because the request is not the certificate. Compare the public key in the certificate with the public key in the request before install. ECDSA P-256 could replace RSA 2048 as the key type. Either way the private half stays home. Extra names are fields in the request, not an excuse to attach the key.\n',
        'r1': 'A signing request asks an authority to sign a name. The request holds the public key. The private key stays on the home machine. Never send the private key with the request. The request file is not a certificate. Installing the request will not satisfy browsers. The authority returns a signed certificate later. Confirm the returned public key matches the request. Let only the owner read the private key file. A wide name pattern is a separate decision. Put extra names in the request when you need them. Stop if a coworker attaches the private key.\n',
        'r2': 'A person who wants a badge fills out a form that shows the desired name and a public sample of their stamp. The private stamp stays at home and is not glued to the form. The notary later returns a finished badge. The form is not a badge, and pinning the form on the door will not convince a guard. Before the badge is worn, the public sample on the badge should match the sample on the form, so a swapped badge is caught. Extra room names, when they are needed, are written on the form so they appear on the badge. \nIn the real process, the form is the certificate signing request and the finished badge is the certificate. The private key stays home, and the request alone is not something clients trust.\n',
        'r4': 'Confirm the file you will send is the request and not the private key. Write down the subject name and the public key type. Check that the private key file stays on the server and is absent from the ticket. When the certificate returns, compare its public key with the request before install. Do not install the request as though browsers will trust it. Do not loosen key permissions while you are only recording the current mode. Note the ticket number beside those checks.\n',
        'exam': "A certificate signing request is the request, not the certificate. It carries the public key and the wanted name. The private key stays home. Installing the request does not create trust. The authority returns the signed certificate afterward. Match that certificate's public key to the request before you install it. Mailing the private key is the mistake this process is meant to prevent. A wide name pattern and extra names are choices about the name. They are not reasons to send the key. The request file is not a finished certificate.\n",
    },
    'Wildcard certificates': {
        'body': 'A wildcard certificate covers one label directly under a domain. The pattern *.example.edu matches mail.example.edu and files.example.edu, because each of those names puts a single label in front of example.edu. It does not match the bare name example.edu, which has no label in that position. It does not match dev.lab.example.edu, because dev.lab is two labels. The asterisk stands for one label. It is not a promise to cover every depth of name under the domain. People who say the star means everything have widened the pattern past the rule.\n\nOne certificate for many hosts at that single level is convenient. The cost is the blast radius of the private key. Whoever holds the key can impersonate every name the pattern covers, including names that do not exist yet. A leak is not limited to the host that happened to be live on the day of the leak. A list of exact names on a certificate is a different feature. Those exact names do not grow to cover a host you add next month. The wildcard does. Choosing the pattern means accepting that width on purpose, and protecting the key as if every matching name depended on it.\n\nOn ticket CERT-77 the installed certificate bears *.example.edu. Hana requests mail.example.edu and the name matches. She requests files.example.edu and the name matches. She requests example.edu and the name fails. She requests dev.lab.example.edu and the name fails because two labels sit where the pattern allows one. Hana logs the failure reason as wildcard depth for the nested name. She writes those four results on the ticket. She also notes that a stolen private key would let an attacker present a new single-label name and still match. The key file is handled with the care owed to every host under that parent, not to one mail server.\n\nDefenders choose explicit names when a leaked key would be too wide, and they test the bare domain and a nested name instead of assuming coverage. If the key leaks, the whole certificate is revoked, not one hostname. Students think a wildcard includes the apex and every nested level. It covers one label. Students also treat a wildcard and a written list of extra names as the same feature. The pattern is a rule. The list is a set of names written one by one.\n',
        'r0': 'The pattern *.example.edu matches exactly one label. mail.example.edu and files.example.edu match. example.edu does not, and dev.lab.example.edu does not. Ticket CERT-77 records those four tests. A leaked private key, whether RSA 2048 or ECDSA P-256, can impersonate every one-label name, including names nobody has deployed. The pattern is not a list of exact subject alternative names, and it is not a PKCS#10 request. Revoke the whole certificate if that key leaks. Test the bare name and a two-label name before calling them covered. One asterisk is one label, not every depth.\n',
        'r1': 'A wildcard covers one name label under a parent. Mail and files directly under that parent can match. The bare parent name does not match. Two labels under the parent do not match. One leaked key risks every name the pattern covers. The damage is wider than a single host name. A list of exact names is a different feature. Exact names do not create a pattern. Guard the private key as if many hosts shared it. Try a nested name and expect failure. Try the bare parent and expect failure too. Future names you have not built yet can still match.\n',
        'r2': "A floor pass opens every office that sits directly on one floor. It opens the mail office and the file office on that floor. It does not open the building's front nameplate, and it does not open a room reached by an extra hallway inside an office. One lost pass puts every office on that floor at risk, including offices that have not been built. A pass that lists two exact room names is a different object, because those written names do not grow when a new office appears. The floor pass is a pattern for a single level, and the lost pass is as wide as that whole level.\n\nIn the real certificate, the floor pass is the wildcard for one label under a domain. The bare domain and a two-label name stay outside that pattern.\n",
        'r4': 'Read the certificate pattern and write it down exactly. Test one label under the domain and expect a match. Test the bare domain and expect a failure. Test a name with two labels and expect a failure. Do not assume a name you have not built is safe from a leaked key. Record all four results on the ticket. Do not edit the pattern by hand. Leave revocation to the incident owner unless the key is already known to be exposed.\n',
        'exam': 'A wildcard covers one label under a domain. The pattern *.example.edu matches mail.example.edu and files.example.edu. It does not match example.edu, and it does not match dev.lab.example.edu. It is not a list of exact extra names. A leaked key affects every name the pattern covers, including names that do not exist yet. The bare domain needs its own entry if it must match. Two labels need a different name. Students who treat the asterisk as every depth have overstated the pattern.\n',
    },
    'Subject alternative names': {
        'body': 'A subject alternative name is an extra name listed on one certificate, so the certificate can match several exact names. The list often holds the bare domain and the www name together, plus any other name the service really uses. Current clients compare the name the person requested with that list. If the name is absent, the connection fails even when the issuer is trusted and the dates are current. A name that appears only in the older common-name field, and not in the list, is not a dependable match. The list is the field clients check.\n\nA list of exact names is not a wildcard. Listing example.edu and www.example.edu does not cover mail.example.edu, and it does not create a pattern. A wildcard may occupy one line in the list, and that line still means a single label, but two exact names do not behave like an asterisk. The order of the names does not rescue a missing one. Adding a name means obtaining a new certificate. It does not mean clicking past a mismatch, and it does not mean editing the certificate by hand. The signing request can ask for the extra names. The issued certificate is where you confirm they were actually included.\n\nOn ticket WEB-41 the public certificate lists two DNS names, example.edu and www.example.edu. Idris requests the bare name and it matches. He requests the www name and it matches. He requests mail.example.edu and the client reports a hostname mismatch. Mail is simply not on the list. The issuer is trusted and the dates are current, so the failure is the name rather than the signer. The common-name field repeats example.edu and does not add mail. The repair is a new certificate that includes every name the site serves, followed by a test of each name. Idris does not tell users to bypass the warning.\n\nDefenders read the name list before installation, test every name the service answers for, and reissue when a name is added. They do not treat the common name as a stand-in for the list. The exam trap is to confuse the list with a wildcard. The list states extra names one by one. A wildcard covers one label under a parent and can include names nobody wrote down. If mail fails because that exact name was never listed, the topic is the subject alternative name. If one pattern covers every single label under the parent, the topic is the wildcard.\n',
        'r0': 'Ticket WEB-41 lists DNS names example.edu and www.example.edu. A request for mail.example.edu fails with a hostname mismatch on port 443, even though the issuer is trusted and the dates are current. Clients match the subject alternative name list. A common name that only repeats example.edu does not add mail. This list is not the wildcard *.example.edu, which would cover one label and therefore mail. A new name needs a new certificate. Put the names in the PKCS#10 request, then read them back on the issued certificate. Test each name. Do not bypass the mismatch.\n',
        'r1': 'A subject alternative name is an extra name on a certificate. The bare site and the www form are a common pair. Clients compare the requested name with the list. An absent name does not match. Mail fails when the list has only the bare name and www. The old common name field is not enough alone. A one-label pattern is a different feature. Two exact names do not cover a third name. Read the name list before you install. Add a missing name by getting a new certificate. Do not click past a name mismatch. Test every name the site actually serves.\n',
        'r2': "A badge can list several exact rooms, such as the lobby and the lobby's front desk. The guard compares the room you asked for with that written list. If the mail room is not written down, the badge does not open it, even when the notary is trusted and the dates are still current. Writing two rooms does not invent a rule that covers every room on the floor. An older line that repeats the lobby name does not add the mail room. Adding a room means issuing a new badge. Waving the visitor through does not write the room onto the badge.\n\nIn the real certificate, the written rooms are the extra names on the certificate. Clients match the requested name to that list, and an unlisted name fails.\n",
        'r4': 'Open the certificate and write down every name on the alternative name list. Request each name the site serves and record match or mismatch. Do not rely on the common name alone. Do not click through a mismatch to finish the test. If a required name is missing, note that a new certificate is required. Do not edit the certificate by hand. Leave the current certificate in place until the replacement is ready. Attach the name list to the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'A subject alternative name lists extra names on the certificate. Clients match the requested name against that list. The names example.edu and www.example.edu do not cover mail.example.edu. A missing name fails even when the issuer is trusted and the dates are current. The common name is not a substitute for the list. A wildcard is a one-label pattern, not a written list of exact names. Adding a name requires a new certificate. Bypassing a mismatch does not place the missing name on the list.\n',
    },
    'Nation-state actors': {
        'body': "A nation-state actor is an operator paid by a government to pursue espionage or disruption. Time, money, and patience come from that sponsor rather than from a personal hustle. Research labs, defense suppliers, ministries, and energy operators sit high on the list because another country wants their designs or their controls. Ransom notes and public bragging hurt a mission that depends on staying unnoticed. Resources here are a state budget. Skill is usually high because specialists are hired and kept. The motive stays strategic even when the first traces look like ordinary theft.\n\nEntry often begins with a carefully written message to one trusted employee, or with a supplier the victim already allows inside. After that foothold, operators move slowly and prefer administration tools the staff already trust, so a file copy resembles maintenance. Custom implants appear when a public kit would be recognized by ordinary scanners. Command hosts are prepared early, sometimes months before the first successful login. The desired result is a copy of a secret, or a reliable way to interrupt a service on a chosen later date.\n\nA materials lab watched the pattern on its mail gateway. A contractor account authenticated at 02:14 from an address that account had never used. A mailbox rule named with a single dot then forwarded any message whose subject contained the word patent. Across eleven weeks, design archives left in pieces smaller than a few megabytes, so a simple volume alert stayed quiet. No payment demand reached the lab. The contractor laptop, once imaged, held a custom implant rather than a kit copied from a public forum.\n\nResponse staff treat the activity as an intrusion and preserve evidence before cutting access. The mailbox rule, the forward destination, and a full laptop image should be saved first. Disabling the contractor account and isolating the laptop are containment. Searching other mailboxes for that same odd rule name continues detection and analysis. Wiping the laptop at the start would destroy the implant needed for comparison. A quiet skilled theft is still not proof of a government sponsor, because a crime crew with a large buyer can also purchase strong tools.\n\nExaminations push students to call every skilled quiet theft a government job, or to treat a loud borrowed scanner as the same thing. An unskilled attacker sprays someone else's kit and trips alarms the same day, without a months-long plan. A hacktivist wants the act seen and ties it to a cause, often with a slogan. Nation-state actors hide, spend a government budget, and aim at espionage or strategic disruption without needing a fast payment.\n",
        'r0': 'Government pay is the resource that defines a nation-state intrusion. Operators can remain for eleven weeks or longer, which a weekend hobby does not fund. In one lab case a contractor account logged in at 02:14, and a mailbox rule named with a single dot forwarded patent mail. Design files left in small pieces, and no ransom note appeared. The laptop image showed a custom implant rather than a public kit. Containment means disabling that account and isolating the host only after the rule, the destination, and the image are preserved. Detection and analysis continue by searching other mailboxes for the same rule. A hacktivist would want a public slogan. An unskilled scanner would have made noise on day one. Espionage or strategic disruption for a government is the mission.\n',
        'r1': 'A government pays these operators. Patience stretches across many weeks. Quiet theft of secrets is a common aim. Breaking a strategic service later is the other aim. A ransom note would give the work away. Custom tools show up in these jobs. A loud borrowed kit is a different person. High skill is common in this group. Money alone does not prove the label. A crime crew can also stay quiet. The sponsor is a government. The mission does not need a fast sale. Long quiet collection is one clue. No payment demand is another clue. Public slogans point somewhere else.\n',
        'r2': 'A foreign reading-room team copies a rare atlas a few pages at a time. Salaries and a long calendar sit behind the visit, and nobody hangs a banner in the lobby. A tourist with a borrowed camera makes noise and leaves the same afternoon. Staff who only watch for smashed glass miss the pencils and the careful list of shelf numbers. The atlas is wanted by a ministry at home, not by a pawnshop. Quiet hours are the method, and credit is avoided. That reading room is government intrusion: a real budget, high patience, and a strategic copy. A slogan on the door or a smashed display would be a different visitor.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the mail gateway log for the contractor account and write down the login time, the source address, and the rule name. Record the forward destination and a hash of the laptop image. Do not disable the account until those notes exist. Do not wipe the laptop yet. After the image is stored, isolate the host and disable the account, and label both acts as containment. Search other mailboxes for the same rule before naming a government sponsor.\n',
        'exam': 'Do not treat every skilled quiet theft as a nation-state job. A crime crew can also dwell and buy strong tools. Silence is tradecraft, not a flag by itself. A missing ransom note is only one clue. Unskilled kit scans are noisy, borrowed, and usually short. Hacktivists want a slogan and an audience. Disabling the stolen account is containment, not eradication. Wiping the laptop first destroys the implant still needed for comparison.\n',
    },
    'Unskilled attackers': {
        'body': "An unskilled attacker uses tools and instructions written by someone else, and the attempt is often noisy. Resources are small: a personal computer, a free scanner, and a guide posted for anyone to copy. Skill is the skill of following steps, not of designing a quiet campaign or writing a new implant. Motive is often curiosity, a wish to brag, or a small theft, which is a different engine from a government mission or a public cause.\n\nThe method is repetition at volume. The person points a borrowed kit at a huge address range, hunting one known weakness, and does not adapt when the first door is closed. Alarms fire because the same request is repeated from many addresses with a kit's recognizable fingerprint. There is no months-long dwell, no custom command network prepared in advance, and no careful use of the target's own admin tools. Harm can still be serious if the weakness is unpatched. Low skill is not the same as low damage.\n\nA campus clinic saw the pattern on its patient-portal sensors. In a single hour the portal received about forty thousand identical requests for one old login path. The browser tag on every request was the kit string l9scan. Addresses rotated, but the path and the tag did not. Every sensor fired. Nobody moved sideways into the imaging servers, and nobody left a slogan or a payment demand. The operator was running another person's kit across the whole network and tripping every alarm.\n\nDefenders treat the flood of probes as an attack, not as a drill. Write down the path, the kit tag, the hour, and a sample of source addresses before blocking. Rate limits and a block on that path reduce the noise, and an emergency patch closes the weakness the kit was built to find. Patching after the evidence is saved is eradication of the exposure. Blocking addresses alone, while the weakness stays open, only moves the noise. Do not shrug because the operator looks clumsy.\n\nStudents confuse this person with a nation-state crew because both can touch the same login page. A government crew stays longer, uses custom or carefully chosen tools, and does not advertise itself with forty thousand identical hits. Students also confuse the noise with hacktivism. A hacktivist picks a target for a cause and usually wants the result seen. An unskilled attacker is someone running another person's kit, often without a cause and without a long plan.\n",
        'r0': 'An unskilled attacker follows a kit written by someone else. One clinic portal logged about forty thousand identical requests to a single old login path inside one hour. Every request carried the kit tag l9scan, and every sensor alarmed. No sideways move, no slogan, and no ransom followed. Resources are a free tool and a copied guide. Skill is low even when the bug is serious. After a sample of the requests is saved, rate limits blunt the noise and a patch closes the hole. A patch, once evidence is kept, is eradication of the exposure. A nation-state dwell would not look like this hour. A hacktivist would have left a message meant to be read.\n',
        'r1': "An unskilled person runs someone else's kit. The attempt is usually loud. A copied guide supplies the steps. Money for the work is small. Custom tools are missing. One known hole is hunted at huge scale. Alarms fire the same day. Harm can still be real. A clumsy operator can still break a weak site. Curiosity or bragging is a common reason. A public cause is not required. A government payroll is not behind it. Long quiet hiding is not the pattern. Save a sample, then block and patch. Do not ignore the noise.\n",
        'r2': "Someone in a shared kitchen follows a photocopied recipe and sets off every smoke alarm. The cook did not write the recipe, cannot adjust the heat, and keeps banging pans after the first batch burns. Dinner can still be ruined. A trained night crew that copies the rare book in silence is a different guest, and so is the protester who hangs one banner and waits for a crowd. Noise, a borrowed recipe, and no long plan mark the cook in this kitchen. That cook is the unskilled intruder: loud, dependent on another person's steps, and still able to cause a real loss.\n",
        'r4': 'Pull the sensor log for the busiest hour and write down the repeated path, the kit tag, and the request count. Save a handful of source addresses and the exact minute the burst started. Do not wipe the portal server. Do not close the ticket before the vulnerable build number is written down. After the sample is stored, apply the patch and note that the patch is eradication of the exposure. Keep the block list attached to the same ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'Noise does not by itself prove a nation-state, and it does not prove a cause. A borrowed kit plus a huge identical scan points to an unskilled operator. Damage can still be serious when the weakness is open. Hacktivists want an audience and a slogan. Government crews prefer time, custom tools, and silence. Blocking addresses while leaving the hole open does not finish the job. The patch, after a sample is saved, is eradication of the exposure.\n',
    },
    'Hacktivists': {
        'body': "A hacktivist is driven by a cause and usually wants the act seen. Resources are uneven and often modest: volunteers, donated time, and tools the group can beg or build, not a ministry payroll. Skill ranges from a single clever page edit to a noisy flood, and it is not the same thing as the anger behind it. Motive is the belief, plus an audience. Money is not the point, and a quiet year of stolen research is not the point either.\n\nThe work is staged for witnesses. A site is defaced with a slogan, a dump is posted where reporters will look, or traffic is poured on a public page the day a decision is announced. Credit is claimed in a channel the group controls. Stealth would defeat the purpose, so the fingerprint is the message itself. A nation-state crew with a political goal may hide instead, because that crew's sponsor wants the secret or the future outage, not a photograph of a slogan. An unskilled scanner hits everything on the internet and has no manifesto.\n\nAfter a city council voted to close a clinic, the public-works homepage was replaced. The web log showed one POST to a forgotten upload path at 21:06, and the index file's hash changed in that same minute. The new page carried a slogan about the vote and a short manifesto. Within the hour a public channel posted a screenshot and the group name. No customer table was offered for sale, and no payment demand arrived. The audience was the point.\n\nDefenders treat the defacement as an attack. Save the replaced page, the 21:06 log line, and the screenshot before restoring the honest file from backup. Restoring the page is recovery. Closing the forgotten upload, after those copies are stored, is eradication of the hole that let the edit land. Rate limits matter if a flood arrives with the slogan. Do not argue with the manifesto on the production homepage. Do not assume the volunteers have a state's staying power, and do not ignore them because their budget looks small.\n\nStudents call any political incident a nation-state event, or they call any noisy incident an unskilled scan. A government crew often hides and can dwell for months. An unskilled attacker sprays a borrowed kit at random hosts and does not care whether a slogan is read. Hacktivists want the cause seen. Believing a policy is wrong is also not the same as reporting a flaw through a security mailbox and waiting for a fix.\n",
        'r0': 'A hacktivist wants a cause to be seen. After a clinic-closure vote, one POST to a forgotten upload path at 21:06 replaced the public-works index file. The new page held a slogan and a short manifesto, and a public channel posted a screenshot within the hour. No ransom and no data sale followed. Resources are volunteer-scale. Skill may be only enough for that one path. Restore the honest page from backup as recovery, and close the upload only after the bad page and the log line are saved. A nation-state crew would more often hide. An unskilled kit would have sprayed many hosts, not staged one message.\n',
        'r1': 'A cause drives this person. An audience is part of the goal. A slogan is a common mark. Credit is often claimed on purpose. Money is not the prize. A government payroll is unlikely. Volunteer time is the usual budget. Skill can be high or low. Anger does not equal skill. One chosen site is typical. A random scan of every address is a different actor. Hiding for months is a different actor. Save the changed page before you restore it. Restoring the page brings the site back. Closing the hole comes after the copy is saved.\n',
        'r2': 'Protesters hang one banner across the front of city hall the night a vote goes the wrong way. The protesters want the morning crowd to read the words, and the protesters sign the cloth. A clerk who photographs the blueprints in the basement and never speaks is doing a different job. A stranger who rattles every door on the block with a copied key is doing a third job. The banner is there to be seen, and the budget is whatever the group could carry. That banner is hacktivism: a cause, an audience, and an act meant to be noticed. Quiet theft or a random noisy tour of every door is not the same visit.\n',
        'r4': 'Save the defaced page, the 21:06 log line, and the public screenshot before changing the site. Write down the upload path and the new file hash. Do not delete the bad page until those copies exist. Do not edit the slogan in place. Restore the honest page from backup and mark that restore as recovery. Close the forgotten upload afterward and mark that close as eradication of the hole.\n',
        'exam': 'A slogan and a wish to be seen mark hacktivists, not a hidden government campaign. Nation-state crews more often avoid credit and stay for a long time. An unskilled kit sprays random hosts and does not stage a manifesto. A belief is not the same as ethical disclosure through a mailbox and a wait. Restoring the honest page is recovery. Closing the hole after evidence is saved is eradication. Volunteer budgets can still do real harm.\n',
    },
    'Insider threats': {
        'body': "An insider threat is a person who already has some legitimate access. The person may be an employee, a contractor, or a partner account. Motive can be money, a grudge, or a careless shortcut. Resources are mostly the access already granted, not a foreign lab. Skill ranges from a clumsy file copy to an administrator who can delete backups. Budget, skill, and motive do not line up with a stranger who must break in first, and those three do not line up with a crime company selling access as a product.\n\nTwo patterns share the label and should not be mashed together. A malicious insider copies, sells, or damages on purpose. A careless insider breaks a rule to finish work, such as mailing a spreadsheet home, without meaning to help an enemy. Both already passed the trust boundary by belonging there. A stranger who stole a password is not an insider. The account is internal. The actor is external and still had to get in.\n\nPayroll clerk Maya Chen worked the malicious pattern. On Thursday at 17:42 the file server logged a copy of customers.csv, about 48 megabytes, from Maya's session onto a personal USB disk. The resignation email arrived Friday morning. No foreign implant was on the workstation. No slogan appeared. The resource was Maya's normal read right. The skill was ordinary. The motive fits money or a grudge timed to the exit, and the ticket fact is the USB copy the day before the resignation.\n\nDefenders need preparation before the exit conversation: remove access when the decision is made, not the next morning. Once the copy has happened, preserve the file-server event, the USB serial, and the resignation date. Disabling Maya's account is containment. A data-loss rule that alerts on large copies to removable disks is detection. Reviewing who still holds broad reads is how the next case shrinks. Do not wipe Maya's laptop before the copy event is saved. Do not treat the USB copy as proof of a government spy.\n\nStudents call every misuse of an internal account an insider, even when a stranger is holding the password. Students also call a personal cloud drive used for daily work the same thing. Shadow IT is staff using an unsanctioned system to do the job, not a resignation-week theft. Organized crime is an external business of theft, fraud, or extortion. An insider already holds a key, and the motive may be money, a grudge, or a shortcut.\n",
        'r0': "An insider already holds some access. Motive may be money, a grudge, or a careless shortcut. Payroll clerk Maya Chen copied customers.csv, about 48 megabytes, to a personal USB disk at 17:42 the day before a resignation email. The file-server log and the USB serial are the evidence. No custom implant was required, because the read right was already hers. Disabling the account is containment. A stranger using Maya's stolen password is an external actor, not an insider. A personal cloud used only to finish ordinary work is shadow IT, a different label. Organized crime sells access as a business and starts outside.\n",
        'r1': 'An insider already has a key. The person belongs inside. Money can be the reason. A grudge can be the reason. A sloppy shortcut can be the reason. Skill may be ordinary. The danger is the access, not a fancy tool. A stranger with a stolen password is still an outsider. The account can be internal while the person is not. A resignation-week file copy is a warning sign. Write down the file name and the time. Disable the account after the log is saved. Do not call every internal login an insider. Unsanctioned work tools are a different problem.\n',
        'r2': 'An employee with a records-room key walks out with a folder the afternoon before quitting. No window is broken, because the key was issued for the job. A crew that buys a copied key on the street is a business of theft. A coworker who shoves boxes into an unapproved closet because the company cage is slow is taking a shortcut, not stealing the folder. The records-room walkout uses access the person already had. That walkout is an insider: legitimate entry, then a copy or a shortcut that betrays the trust. A bought key or a storage shortcut answers a different question.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the file-server log and write down the account, the file name, the size, the time, and the USB serial. Attach the resignation date to the same note. Do not wipe the workstation yet. Do not give the account a new password and call the case finished. Disable the account only after the log line is saved, and mark that disable as containment. List other removable-disk copies by that account from the prior month before you close the review.\n',
        'exam': 'An insider already has legitimate access. A stranger holding a stolen password is an external actor using an internal account. Money, a grudge, and a careless shortcut are different motives under the same actor label. Shadow IT is unsanctioned tooling for work, not a resignation-week theft. Organized crime is a money business that starts outside. Disabling the account is containment. Wiping the laptop before the copy event is saved destroys evidence.\n',
    },
    'Organized crime': {
        'body': "Organized crime treats intrusion as a business. The product is theft, fraud, or extortion, and the motive is money. Resources sit in the middle or higher: crews, rented servers, and a market where access is bought and sold. Skill is professional and repeatable, a playbook rather than a one-night experiment, though it need not be a novel implant from a government lab. A personal grudge is not the engine. A public slogan is not the engine. Staff using an extra file host to finish work are not this actor.\n\nThe business has roles. One person finds a way in, another sells that access, and a third uses a mailbox or a database to move money. The same steps are run against many victims. A compromised finance mailbox is valuable because it can change a payment. Extortion is one product line: lock or leak unless paid. Fraud is another: send a believable invoice and collect. Espionage for a ministry is a poor fit, because a ministry does not need to cash a wire on Tuesday.\n\nA city hospital saw the business in one afternoon. An access listing offered the accounts-payable mailbox for sale that morning. At 15:10 the same mailbox sent invoice 4412 to a known supplier contact, and the bank account on the invoice was not the supplier's account. The supplier's real clerk did not recognize the destination. No research files moved. No political slogan was posted. The crew wanted the wire. The resource was purchased access. The skill was a familiar fraud play. The motive was financial gain.\n\nDefenders treat the invoice and the mailbox abuse as an attack. Preserve the message, the new account number, and the mailbox log before disabling the mailbox. Disabling that mailbox is containment. Calling the supplier on a known phone number stops the wire, which is part of stopping the loss. After the log is saved, resetting the mailbox password and deleting an attacker forwarding rule removes the cause, which is eradication, not another disable. Hunting the rest of the crew's play is detection and analysis. Do not pay the new account to see what happens.\n\nStudents fold this into insider theft because an employee mailbox was used. The employee was not the actor. The crew bought the access and started outside. Students also call the personal-cloud habit of a busy team the same business. Shadow IT is unsanctioned systems used by staff to do the job, not a market for stolen mailboxes. Organized crime is a money business with roles, a playbook, and a payday.\n",
        'r0': 'Organized crime sells intrusion as a business. Theft, fraud, and extortion are the product lines, and money is the motive. A hospital mailbox was listed for sale in the morning. At 15:10 that mailbox sent invoice 4412 with a bank account the supplier did not own. Purchased access, not a government implant, was the resource. The playbook was repeatable fraud. Disabling the mailbox after the message and the log are saved is containment. An insider would already have held the access for a job. Shadow IT would be staff storing work files on an unsanctioned host, with no invoice fraud and no access market.\n',
        'r1': 'Organized intrusion runs as a business. The goal is money. Theft is one product. Fraud is another product. Extortion is another product. Crews split the work. Access can be bought and sold. The steps repeat against many victims. A government research theft is a different mission. A personal grudge is a different mission. Staff using an extra tool for real work are not this crew. Save the bad invoice before you disable the mailbox. Call the supplier on a known number. Do not pay the new account.\n',
        'r2': 'A warehouse crew sells copies of the loading-dock key, then sends a bill with the payee stamp changed. Each person has a job, and the point is the payment, not a speech and not a personal feud. An employee who already carries a real key and pockets one crate is a different problem. A team that stacks overflow boxes in a rented locker down the street is skipping the slow cage, not running a theft company. The changed stamp and the sold key are a criminal business. That business is organized intrusion for money: roles, repeatable steps, and a payday.\n',
        'r4': 'Save the fraudulent message, the new bank account, and the mailbox log before you change anything. Write down the invoice number and the time it was sent. Do not pay the new account. Do not delete the mailbox. Disable the mailbox after the copies exist, and mark that disable as containment. Phone the supplier on a number you already trust and tell them to hold the wire.\n',
        'exam': 'Organized crime is a money business: theft, fraud, or extortion. Using an employee mailbox does not make the buyer an insider. The insider already belonged. Shadow IT is staff on unsanctioned systems for work, not a market for access. A government espionage case has no need to cash a fake invoice. Disabling the abused mailbox is containment. Paying the new account is not a defense.\n',
    },
    'Shadow IT': {
        'body': 'Shadow IT is a system people in the business actually use that security and technology teams did not approve and may not even know about. Staff adopt the unsanctioned system to get work done. The motive is convenience, speed, or a missing feature, not espionage, not a payday from stolen access, and not a grudge. Resources are whatever a person can sign up for with a credit card or a free account. Skill is ordinary work skill. An unsanctioned work system is not a foreign spy, and an unsanctioned work system is not a criminal crew.\n\nThe mechanism is bypass. The approved share is slow, the ticket queue is long, or the sanctioned chat cannot hold a large file. A team opens a consumer file host, a personal chat, or a small database on a laptop and starts running real work there. Customer lists, designs, or passwords land on a system with no company access review, no retention rule, and a password the company cannot reset. The data is now outside the controls built for it. Exposure follows if that account is phished, reused, or abandoned when someone leaves. The exposure is a consequence. The original act was unsanctioned work, not an intrusion campaign.\n\nMarketing coordinator Priya Shah kept the spring customer list on a personal cloud drive. The company file share took too long from home, so the team link became the real system of work. DNS logs for the marketing subnet showed daily sessions to that consumer storage host. Technology staff had no record of the host and had not approved it. Priya was doing the job, not forwarding patent mail to a foreign drop and not selling a mailbox. The artifact is the daily DNS name plus the customer files on a drive the company does not own.\n\nDefenders discover the host and bring the work back under control. Search DNS and cloud-access logs for storage and chat names that have no owner in inventory. Talk to Priya, offer a fast approved share, copy the files onto it, and close the personal link. Moving files onto an approved share is risk reduction, not eradication of a spy. Do not open a counterintelligence case as the first move. Do not shame the team and leave them with the same slow share, or the files will move again. A leaked personal password on that drive is a separate incident and should be handled as one.\n\nStudents mark this as an insider attack or as organized crime because data sits in the wrong place. An insider threat misuses access to steal, damage, or cut a corner that breaks a duty, such as a resignation-week USB copy. Organized crime sells theft, fraud, or extortion. A foreign intelligence service is a different actor with a strategic mission. Shadow IT is staff using unsanctioned systems to do the job.\n',
        'r0': 'Shadow IT is an unsanctioned system that staff use for real work. Marketing kept a customer list on a personal cloud drive because the company share was slow from home. DNS logs on the marketing subnet showed daily sessions to that consumer host, which inventory did not list. The motive is convenience. The skill is ordinary office skill. The resource is a consumer account. Shadow IT is not a foreign spy and not a crew selling access. Offer a fast approved share, move the files, and close the personal link. A resignation-week USB theft is an insider case instead. Invoice fraud from a bought mailbox is organized crime.\n',
        'r1': 'Staff use a system nobody approved. Staff are trying to finish real work. The usual reason is speed or convenience. A foreign spy is a different person. A crime crew is a different person. The tool may be a personal file drive. The company cannot see who has the link. A weak personal password puts the files at risk. Daily network lookups can reveal the host. Ask why the approved share failed them. Give them a fast approved place. Move the files there. Close the personal link. Do not start by calling them spies.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic team stores patient folders in a rented locker down the street because the basement cage takes too long to open. The team is still doing clinic work. A spy photographing the folders for a foreign capital is not the same act. A theft crew selling copies of the cage key is not the same act. The locker has no clinic badge reader and no record of who holds a key. That rented locker is shadow tooling: staff, an unsanctioned place, and a motive of convenience. Spying for a foreign capital and a criminal key market are different problems with different people.\n',
        'r4': 'Search DNS logs for storage names that inventory does not list, and write down the host and the team subnet. Ask the team what files live there and why the approved share was avoided. Do not delete the personal drive before the files are copied to a company location. Do not reset the case into a spy hunt. Move the files onto an approved share, then close the personal link. Note the date the link was closed.\n',
        'exam': 'Shadow IT is staff using unsanctioned systems for work. A foreign spy is a different person. Selling stolen access is organized crime, a different label. An insider threat misuses trusted access to steal, harm, or break a duty. Convenience is the usual motive, and ordinary work skill is the usual skill. Discover the host, offer a usable approved path, and move the files. Deleting the only copy before a move creates a new loss.\n',
    },
    'Internal versus external actors': {
        'body': "Internal actors start from inside the trust boundary. External actors must first get in. The boundary is the set of accounts, badges, and networks the organization already treats as its own. The internal-versus-external comparison is about starting position. Starting position is not a measure of budget, not a measure of skill, and not a motive. A careless employee and a paid government unit can both be described only after you answer where they started.\n\nThe same stolen file can come from either side, and the traces differ. An internal copy rides an interactive session of a person who badged in, on a managed computer, during a normal shift. An external copy rides a path that had to cross in: a new remote session, a password reset the owner did not request, or traffic from a hosting network toward a server the employee never uses. Stolen credentials do not move the stranger across the category. The account may be internal. The person holding it is still external if that person did not belong.\n\nCustomers.csv left the clinic network in two different stories that students mix up. In the internal story, Jordan Lee badged the side door at 08:12, logged in on a managed laptop, and copied the file to a USB disk. In the external story, no badge event exists, Jordan's password was reset from an address Jordan did not use, and the bytes left a server Jordan never logs into, bound for a rented host. The file name matches. The starting point does not match. Resources in the external story might be a cheap kit or a state budget. Resource and skill questions come after the position question.\n\nDefenders log enough to tell the stories apart. Badge events, interactive logon type, and whether the source is a managed device should sit next to the file-copy event. Write down which story the evidence supports before choosing a label. Disabling a stolen account is containment in the external story. Removing a departing employee's access before the last day is preparation in the internal story. Do not wipe either machine before the log lines are saved. Do not treat position as if it named the motive.\n\nThe exam trap treats every use of an internal account as an internal actor. A stranger with Jordan's password started outside and had to get in. The neighboring mix-up with resources or skill is just as common. A well-funded external crew and a broke external scanner are both external. A highly skilled administrator and a clumsy clerk can both be internal. Position, budget, and skill are three different questions.\n",
        'r0': "Internal actors begin inside the trust boundary. External actors must cross it. Jordan Lee's internal copy of customers.csv followed a badge swipe at 08:12 and a session on a managed laptop. The external copy had no badge, used a password reset Jordan did not make, and left from a server Jordan never uses. The file name can match while the start point does not. Stolen credentials keep the stranger in the external category. Budget and skill are separate questions asked after position. Disabling a stolen account is containment. Removing access before a last day is preparation. A badge event is the concrete clue that separates those two ticket stories.\n",
        'r1': 'Internal people start inside. External people must get in first. A badge and a work laptop suggest inside. A surprise password reset suggests outside. The file taken can look the same. The starting place is the difference. A stolen password does not make a stranger an insider. The account and the person are not the same thing. Rich and poor attackers can both be external. Clumsy and skilled people can both be internal. Write down the badge facts before you label anyone. Position is not the same as motive. Position is not the same as budget.\n',
        'r2': 'A clerk who already works in the warehouse carries a crate out the employee door. A stranger who wants the same crate must cut the fence or talk a way through the gate. The crate can be identical. The door that was used is not. A huge rented crew and a lone person with a cheap cutter can both be strangers at the fence. A master locker-opener and a new hire can both already work inside. Starting inside or starting outside is its own question. Budget and skill are asked next, and they do not rewrite where the person began.\n',
        'r4': 'Place the file-copy event next to badge records and logon type, and write down whether a managed device was used. Note any password reset the owner did not request. Do not label the case from the file name alone. Do not wipe the laptop or the server yet. If a stranger holds the account, disable it after the notes exist and mark that as containment. If the person is leaving, record whether access was removed before the last shift.\n',
        'exam': 'Internal actors start inside the trust boundary. External actors must get in. A stolen password does not turn a stranger into an insider. The account can be internal while the actor stays external. Resources and skill are different questions. A rich crew and a cheap scanner can both be external. A skilled admin and a clumsy clerk can both be internal. Disabling a stolen account is containment, not a change of actor category.\n',
    },
    'Resources and funding': {
        'body': "Resources and funding describe how much time, money, and infrastructure an actor can spend. The attribute answers a spending question. Spending power does not answer how elegant the method is, and spending power does not answer what the actor wants. A government can buy months of time. A crime crew can rent servers and purchase access. An unskilled person may have only a laptop and a free kit. Collapsing those differences into one cartoon attacker hides the case.\n\nFunding shows up in concrete infrastructure. Domains registered many months before first use, command servers that stay online through nights and weekends, prepaid hosting in several regions, and people available to work shifts are spending clues. A hobby kit has none of that preparation. Money can buy dwell time and machines. Money does not automatically write a subtle tool, and a nearly broke person can still be clever. A furious motive does not increase the budget. A noble-sounding motive does not either.\n\nA manufacturing firm kept three command hosts that called back from one compromised design PC. Each host's domain had been registered eleven months earlier, and the hosting bills were prepaid for a year. Activity continued on Saturday nights when the plant was empty. Prepaid hosts and Saturday coverage cost money and planning a weekend scanner does not have. The clue supports a funded actor. A funding clue does not, by itself, prove a ministry, because a criminal business can also prepay servers when the expected sale is large. Motive still has to be read from what was taken and whether a demand appeared.\n\nDefenders write funding clues beside the hypothesis instead of stapling them to a flag. Record domain age, host count, prepaid terms, and whether the activity covers off hours. Compare that note with a separate note on tool quality and a third note on motive. Do not skip containment while debating the budget: isolate the design PC and disable the abused account after images and logs are saved. Do not treat a thin budget as harmlessness. A cheap rented flood can still knock a small public site offline.\n\nStudents equate rich with nation-state and poor with harmless. Organized crime is often well funded and still wants money. An unskilled attacker is usually poor in infrastructure and still causes damage when a known hole is open. Sophistication is the neighbor attribute students swap in. A funded actor can run a clumsy kit. A refined method can appear in the hands of a buyer who did not write it. Resources answer what they can afford.\n",
        'r0': 'Resources and funding measure time, money, and infrastructure. Funding totals do not measure elegance or motive. One design PC called three command hosts whose domains were registered eleven months earlier, on hosting prepaid for a year, including Saturday nights. Eleven-month domains and prepaid hosts fit a funded actor and do not fit a weekend free kit. A crime business can prepay servers too, so the budget is not a flag. Record domain age, host count, and off-hour coverage as their own note. Isolating the PC after logs are saved is containment. A cheap flood can still take a small site down. Tool quality belongs in a separate sentence of the report.\n',
        'r1': 'Funding means how much an actor can spend. Time costs money. Servers cost money. A long quiet stay costs money. A free kit costs almost nothing. A big budget is not a national flag. Criminals can rent strong infrastructure too. A poor attacker can still do harm. Money does not equal skill. A rich crew can be clumsy. A clever person can be broke. Write down domain age and host count. Keep that note apart from motive. Do not skip containment while you debate the budget.\n',
        'r2': 'A moving crew either owns several vans and can work all weekend, or it has one borrowed car and a Saturday afternoon. The size of the fleet does not tell you whether the driver is a fine lock-opener, and it does not tell you whether the cargo is meant for a rival firm, a pawnshop, or a protest. Fleet size is only the budget inside the story. Funding and resources are that fleet: what the actor can afford to rent, staff, and sustain. Lock craft is sophistication, and the reason for the cargo is motive, which fleet size never decides.\n',
        'r4': 'Write down domain creation dates, how many command hosts appear, and whether the hosts were prepaid. Note if the activity continues on nights and weekends. Keep those lines in a funding note, separate from tool quality and from motive. Do not relabel the case as a government job from the invoices alone. Do not wipe the affected PC yet. After the logs are copied, isolate the PC and record that isolation as containment.\n',
        'exam': 'Resources and funding answer what an actor can spend. Funding totals do not answer skill, and funding totals do not answer motive. A large budget is not proof of a government. Organized crime can prepay infrastructure for a payday. A small budget is not proof of safety. A funded actor can still run a clumsy kit. Sophistication is a different attribute. Isolating a host is containment, not a funding conclusion.\n',
    },
    'Sophistication and capability': {
        'body': "Sophistication is how advanced the tools and the tradecraft are. Capability is what that skill lets the actor actually accomplish. The attribute grades the method. The capability score does not grade anger, a slogan, a payday, or a government seal. A novel technique can serve espionage, crime, or a protest. A clumsy technique can serve those same motives. Students who treat skill as a motive will misfile the case every time.\n\nHigh sophistication looks like judgment. The actor avoids known fingerprints, uses the target's own administration tools in a way that blends with real work, and may set file times back so a new binary looks as old as the install. Low sophistication looks like a default kit: one unchanged tag, one public how-to, no adaptation when an alarm fires. Buying a sophisticated kit does not promote the operator. If the person only presses a button that scans everything, the elegance belongs to the author, and the operator stays unskilled.\n\nA design-office incident showed the split cleanly. The dropped file matched no signature the sensors knew, and its recorded timestamps matched the operating-system install date rather than the night it appeared. A second, earlier case the same quarter was the opposite: forty thousand hits from a public kit with a fixed tag. Both were intrusions. Only the first showed high capability in the method. Neither file, by itself, named a sponsor or a reason. A crime group can buy a refined tool. A government crew can sometimes reuse a loud kit. Motive is read from goals, not from elegance.\n\nDefenders record method facts in their own column. Note whether the tool was novel or copied, whether timestamps were altered, and whether the operator adapted after detection. Put funding clues in a second column and motive evidence in a third. Preserve the binary and the logs before cleanup. Deleting the novel file after the image is stored is eradication of that tool. Deleting it before the image is stored is how you lose the only proof of what was sophisticated. Do not raise the skill score because the victim is angry or the data is sensitive.\n\nThe exam trap turns a signature-evading file into a nation-state badge, or it turns a fancy kit into a fancy operator. Resources are the neighbor students also swap in: money can purchase a refined tool without the buyer knowing how it works. Political heat and personal anger do not increase capability. Sophistication answers how refined the method is.\n",
        'r0': 'Sophistication grades tools and tradecraft, not motive. In one design office a dropped file matched no known signature, and its timestamps had been set back to the operating-system install date. A second case that quarter was about forty thousand hits from a public kit with a fixed tag. The first method was high capability. The second operator was not. A crime buyer can purchase the refined file. A no-signature result is not a government seal. Preserve the binary before deletion. Deleting it after the image is stored is eradication of that tool. Funding and motive belong in other notes. Write the judgment score on its own line of the ticket.\n',
        'r1': 'Sophistication grades the method. Fancy tools are one clue. Careful habits are another clue. Anger does not raise skill. A payday does not raise skill. A slogan does not raise skill. A bought kit can be clever while the user is not. Pressing one big scan button is low skill. Avoiding every known fingerprint is high skill. Crime groups can buy refined tools. A government is not proven by elegance. Save the file before you delete it. Keep skill notes away from motive notes.\n',
        'r2': "A lock on a clinic door can be opened by a master who feels the pins, or by a novice shaking a fancy pick bought that morning. The pick may be excellent. The novice's hands still give the skill away. Rage at the clinic does not steady those hands, and neither does a plan to sell what is inside. Sophistication is that feel for the pins: how refined the method is, and what the person can actually accomplish with it. The price of the pick is only the budget, and the shouting outside is only the motive, never a substitute for skill.\n",
        'r4': 'Write down whether the file matched any known signature and whether its timestamps match the install date or the night it appeared. Note if the operator changed course after an alarm. Do not score motive in that same line. Do not delete the file before an image is stored. After the image exists, remove the tool and mark that removal as eradication. Keep the funding notes on a separate page of the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'Sophistication grades the method, not the sponsor and not the motive. A file with no known signature is a capability clue, not a flag. Buying a refined kit does not make the operator refined. Anger and a slogan do not increase skill. Resources are a different attribute: money can purchase a tool the buyer does not understand. Deleting the file before an image is saved destroys the proof. Deleting it afterward is eradication of that tool.\n',
    },
    'Data theft as a motive': {
        'body': 'Data theft as a motive means the goal is to take information and keep it or sell it. The service can stay online. A slogan is unnecessary. A threat to publish is unnecessary. The actor is a separate question. A crime crew, a government unit, or an insider can all steal data, and their budgets and skills will not match. Data theft says only that the information itself is the prize.\n\nThe mechanism is a copy that leaves. Someone with a path to the data, bought or already held, exports rows or files to a place the organization does not control. A bulk job is common when the plan is a sale, because a buyer wants the whole table. A system that remains up is a clue, not a comfort. Leaders sometimes feel the business was struck and reach for a disruption label. If orders still complete and the rows are gone, the goal was the rows.\n\nA national retailer woke to an overnight export. The orders database sent about 1.2 million customer rows to an outside storage bucket at 01:16. The website kept taking orders through the morning. No ransom demand appeared. No manifesto replaced the homepage. The query text showed a full-table read by a service account that normally only runs reports. The ticket facts are that time, that row count, that destination, and that service account. Whoever holds the rows can sell them or mine them for fraud.\n\nDefenders turn on database audit for large reads and alert when an export lands outside the known vault. Preserve the query text, the row count, and the destination before disabling the service account. Disabling that service account is containment. Blocking the destination while a copy is still running is containment too. Patching the weakness that exposed the account, after the logs are saved, is eradication. Do not call the export a traffic flood. Do not wipe the database server and lose the audit trail.\n\nEspionage is the neighbor students assign whenever the data sounds important. Espionage seeks a strategic advantage, usually in small pieces over a long time, for a sponsor who will not sell the file on a market this week. Service disruption is the other neighbor. There the goal is to stop the work, and the data may never leave. A one-time bulk copy of a customer table, with the site still up and no ransom, is data theft. Possession of the information is what separates this motive.\n',
        'r0': 'Data theft as a motive takes information to keep or sell. A retailer export copied about 1.2 million customer rows to an outside bucket at 01:16. The website kept taking orders. No ransom note and no manifesto appeared. The service account that ran the full-table read normally only builds reports. Actor type stays a separate label: crime, government, or insider. Disabling that service account after the query text is saved is containment. Espionage would more often drip small secrets for months. Service disruption would stop the orders. The rows themselves are the prize in this motive. Actor type stays on its own line of the report.\n',
        'r1': 'The goal is the information itself. The taker wants to keep it or sell it. The service can stay up. A slogan is not required. A threat note is not required. A crime crew can do this. An insider can do this. A government unit can do this too. Crime, insider, and government are actor labels. A bulk copy is a common shape. Small secret drips for months are different. An outage with no copied rows is different. Save the query and the destination. Disable the account only after that save. Do not call a copy a flood.\n',
        'r2': 'A cook lifts the whole recipe box and leaves while the kitchen still serves dinner. The meals continue. The prize is the box, which can be sold or cooked from at home. Copying one secret page a week for a rival house is a slower, quieter job with a different purpose. Barring the door so no meals leave, while the box stays on the shelf, is a third job. The lifted box is data theft: the information is taken, and the work can continue. A long secret drip or a blocked door answers a different motive than simply taking the box home.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the database audit and write down the account, the time, the row count, and the destination bucket. Save the query text with those notes. Do not wipe the database server. Do not disable the account before the audit line is copied. After the copy exists, disable the service account and mark that disable as containment. Check whether orders kept succeeding during the export and write that fact down too.\n',
        'exam': 'Data theft wants the information itself, kept or sold. A site that stays up does not cancel the motive. Espionage is strategic, quiet, and usually stretched over a long time. Service disruption wants an outage, and the rows may never leave. Actor labels still vary: crime, insider, or government. Disabling the exporting account is containment. Wiping the server before the audit is saved destroys the proof of what left.\n',
    },
    'Espionage': {
        'body': 'Espionage seeks secrets for a strategic advantage, quietly and over a long time. Bragging and ransom notes would spoil the value. The taker wants the knowledge for a government, a rival program, or another long game, not for a listing on a criminal market tomorrow. Resources are often large, because someone must watch for months, yet one well-placed insider can drip files out on a smaller budget. Skill shows up as patience and as blending into ordinary jobs. The motive is the strategic secret, which is narrower than taking any data at all.\n\nThe method is low and slow. Small archives leave on a schedule. Staging folders appear and vanish. Daily volume stays under the alert that would catch a full database dump. Unpublished designs, negotiation notes, and test results are typical targets. A customer table grabbed once for resale can be quiet for a week and still be ordinary data theft for money. Tempo, target, and the absence of a resale are what push the label here.\n\nA propulsion lab found an archive job that wrote about 200 kilobytes of notebook scans into a temp folder every Tuesday at 03:10, then deleted the folder after the transfer. The job had run for four months. No ransom demand arrived. No public claim appeared. The files were unpublished test results. The scheduled task name, the temp path, and the Tuesday time are the ticket facts. A single export of a shopper table would not fit this shape, even though both cases involve copying.\n\nDefenders hunt repeated small archives, odd scheduled tasks, and accounts that read far outside their role. Preserve the task definition, the temp path, and the destination before disabling the account. Disabling the lab account is containment. Removing the scheduled task after those copies are saved is eradication of that collection job. Do not wait for a ransom this motive does not need. Do not treat one huge dump with a sales trail as espionage merely because the dump was quiet for a few days.\n\nService disruption is the other mix-up. Success there is an outage, and the notebooks can stay unread. Data theft for sale is the closer mix-up. Information leaves, but the purpose is to keep or sell it, often in one large copy, with money as the engine. Espionage collects secrets for advantage across a long dwell. Purpose and tempo, taken together, keep this motive distinct.\n',
        'r0': 'Espionage collects secrets for strategic advantage and stays quiet. A propulsion lab job wrote about 200 kilobytes of notebook scans to a temp folder every Tuesday at 03:10 for four months, then deleted the folder. No ransom and no public claim appeared. The files were unpublished test results, not a resale customer list. A single well-placed insider can do this with a modest budget, though many cases are well funded. Disabling the account after the task is saved is containment. Removing the task afterward is eradication of that collection job. A one-time bulk sale of shopper rows is data theft instead. An outage without copied files is service disruption.\n',
        'r1': 'Secrets are taken for a long game. The collection stays quiet. Bragging would spoil the value. A ransom note would spoil it too. Small pieces leave over months. A full table in one night is a different shape. Unpublished work is a typical target. A customer list for sale is a different target. Patience is the skill on display. A huge budget is common but not required. One insider can drip files slowly. Save the scheduled task before you remove it. Stopping the account is containment. Do not wait for a payment demand that will not come.\n',
        'r2': "A clerk in a closed archive copies a few pages of a secret recipe each week for a rival house and never sells the pages on the corner. The kitchen stays open. The clerk's value is the slow advantage, not a Saturday pawnshop price. Grabbing the whole box in one trip to sell downtown is a faster, greedier visit. Nailing the dining-room door shut, and leaving the pages untouched, is neither visit. Espionage is that weekly copying: strategic secrets, a long dwell, and no market stall. A one-night sale or a blocked door is a different motive, even when copying also occurred.\n",
        'r4': 'Find the scheduled task and write down the name, the Tuesday time, the temp path, and the destination. Note the size of each archive and how many weeks it ran. Do not delete the task yet. Do not wipe the lab workstation. After those notes and a copy of the task exist, disable the account and mark that as containment. Remove the task only afterward and mark that removal as eradication of the collection job.\n',
        'exam': 'Espionage wants strategic secrets, quietly, over a long time. A missing ransom is expected, not proof by itself. A one-time bulk copy for sale is data theft, even if it was quiet for a week. Service disruption succeeds when work stops, whether or not files leave. A modest insider budget can still fit espionage. Disabling the account is containment. Deleting the collection task before the definition is saved destroys the evidence.\n',
    },
    'Service disruption': {
        'body': 'Service disruption aims to stop the work, whether or not any data is stolen. The prize is the outage. Bandwidth, memory, or staff attention is exhausted until the real service fails. Who does it still varies. A cause-driven group may buy a cheap flood in order to be seen. A crime crew may disrupt as the opening of extortion, and the motive shifts once a demand appears. A conflict may disrupt a utility with no demand at all. The disruption motive says only that stopping the service is the goal.\n\nA booking site for a regional clinic died at 08:05 on the busiest Monday of enrollment season. The edge graph showed inbound traffic pinned at the circuit maximum, and patients timed out while confirming a visit. No database rows were missing when the site returned. No ransom demand arrived. The people affected were the morning patients. The system was the booking site. The ticket fact is a saturated circuit beside an empty theft column. A flood tries to exhaust bandwidth or server capacity so the genuine service fails.\n\nFiltering and rate limits are the defenses, sometimes with help from the carrier upstream. When the flood is a reflected DNS amplifier, closing open recursion and refusing spoofed queries reduces the amplifier. DNSSEC is a different control. DNSSEC checks that a DNS answer is authentic. DNSSEC does not absorb a flood. Defenders keep a traffic sample, note the absence of data loss, and restore service by filtering. Restoring bookings is recovery. A new rate limit is a control change, not proof of who paid for the flood.\n\nCompare the buyers before naming a sponsor. A volunteer group can rent a short flood for the price of a dinner and still want a slogan seen, which is low budget, modest skill, and a political motive. A crime crew can use the same outage shape as a prelude, then send a demand, which moves the motive toward money while the skill stays inside a purchased playbook. A conflict actor can darken a utility and never ask to be paid. Availability is the shared effect. Budget, craft, and desire stay three different notes on the ticket. A traffic flood is an attack, not a defense, and a traffic flood does not keep the clinic healthy.\n\nStudents name DNSSEC as the fix for the outage, or they call the outage data theft because the clinic lost money. Lost revenue is not copied rows. Espionage would have shown small secret files leaving for months, with the site still up. If a payment demand arrives, read the demand as blackmail or financial pressure and keep the outage as the method. Service disruption wants the system unable to do its job.\n',
        'r0': 'Service disruption stops the work, whether or not data is stolen. A clinic booking site died at 08:05 on the busiest enrollment Monday. Inbound traffic sat at the circuit maximum, patient sessions timed out, and no rows were missing afterward. No ransom note arrived. Filtering and rate limits are the direct defenses. A DNS amplifier is reduced by closing open recursion and by not answering spoofed queries. DNSSEC only checks that a DNS answer is authentic. DNSSEC does not stop a flood, and the flood is an attack rather than a defense. Restoring the booking service is recovery. Espionage would have left the site up while secrets dripped out.\n',
        'r1': 'The goal is to stop the work. Stolen files are optional. A full road of junk traffic is one method. Real visitors then cannot get through. The site can lose money without losing rows. A seal on a name check does not clear a flood. Filtering and slowing repeats are the defenses. A demand for payment is a different motive. Quiet secret copies are a different motive. Save a sample of the traffic. Bring the service back after the sample is kept. Do not treat the flood as protection. Do not call lost fees stolen records.\n',
        'r2': 'Someone blocks the clinic loading dock so no meals leave, while the recipe box stays locked in the office. The harm is the stopped service. A wax seal that proves a letter is genuine does not move the trucks. Carrying the recipe box away, with the dock still open, is a different harm. Copying one secret page a week and leaving the dock clear is a third harm. Service disruption is that blocked dock: the work cannot proceed, and theft is optional. A genuine-letter seal does not clear the blockage, and the seal does not turn the stopped dock into theft.\n',
        'r4': 'Capture the traffic graph and write down the clock time, the circuit maximum, and whether patient bookings timed out. Record that no rows were missing when the site returned. Do not delete the edge logs. Do not point at a name-check control as the flood fix. After the sample is saved, apply filtering or a rate limit and mark restored bookings as recovery. If a payment note appears later, file it as a separate motive.\n',
        'exam': 'Service disruption wants the service unable to work. Missing rows are not required. Lost revenue is not the same as data theft. Espionage keeps the service up and collects secrets slowly. DNSSEC authenticates a DNS answer and does not absorb a flood. A flood is the attack, not a defense. Filtering and rate limits are defenses. Restoring service is recovery. A later payment demand is a different motive sitting on top of the outage.\n',
    },
    'Blackmail': {
        'body': 'Blackmail threatens to reveal or destroy something unless the victim pays or complies. The threat is the motive, not merely an earlier theft. Data may already have left, but the goal now is leverage. Money is a common demand. Silence, a decision, or some other act of compliance can be the demand instead. Resources and skill of the threatener still vary from a lone person to a professional crew. Budget and skill do not change the defining fact of a threat. A threat is what makes this motive.\n\nA note to the general counsel of a shipping firm said the executive mail archive would be published on Friday unless a payment arrived. The note quoted real subject lines from the chief executive mailbox, so staff checked the mail server and confirmed the lines were genuine. No political slogan appeared. No research drip had run for months. The artifact is the note itself: the Friday deadline, the quoted subjects, and the payment instruction. The systems are the executive mailbox and the counsel inbox. The person is whoever sent the threat.\n\nHold the actor axes apart from the threat. A lone former partner can send the Friday note with almost no budget and with ordinary writing skill. A professional crew can send the same kind of note after buying a mailbox, with more money and a repeatable play. Both notes are blackmail if the lever is exposure or destruction. A disclosure that waits for a fix and demands no payment is not blackmail. Damage offered with no deal is not blackmail either. The deal is the motive, whatever budget sits behind the sender.\n\nDefenders preserve the note and the mailbox log, then check whether the mail actually left. Disabling the stolen executive mailbox is containment. A panicked payment from a personal wallet is not containment and is not eradication. Counsel belongs in any decision about payment. If a destructive program is what would carry out the threat, delete that program only after evidence is preserved. Deleting the destructive program after evidence is saved is eradication. Do not describe the threat note as a control that protects the firm.\n\nFinancial gain is the neighbor students use for every money case. Invoice fraud and selling a database also seek money, yet they do not require a threat to reveal or destroy. A political motive seeks a cause more than a payment, even when the act is public and hostile. Without the lever of pay-or-we-expose, or pay-or-we-destroy, the case may still be criminal, but it is not blackmail. The threat used as leverage is what separates this motive.\n',
        'r0': 'Blackmail uses a threat to reveal or destroy unless the victim pays or complies. Counsel at a shipping firm received a note promising publication of executive mail on Friday unless payment arrived. Quoted subject lines matched real messages on the mail server. No slogan and no months-long research drip were present. Disabling the stolen mailbox, after the note and the log are saved, is containment. Paying from a personal wallet is neither containment nor eradication. Invoice fraud seeks money without that threat and is financial gain instead. A slogan tied to a public cause is a political motive. The Friday threat is the center of blackmail.\n',
        'r1': 'A threat is the center of this motive. Pay, or something is exposed. Pay, or something is destroyed. The earlier theft is not the whole story. Money is a common demand. Other kinds of compliance can be demanded too. A fake invoice with no threat is different. A public slogan with no demand is different. A grudge with no demand is different. Save the note before anyone pays. Check whether the mail really left. Disable the stolen mailbox after the save. A personal payment is not a cleanup step. Ask counsel before any payment talk.\n',
        'r2': 'A note slid under a clinic door says the photos go on the front window unless an envelope arrives by Friday. The harm being sold is exposure, and the lever is fear. Changing the payee on a supply bill, with no warning at all, tricks the payer instead of threatening the payer. Painting a slogan about a vote, and never asking for an envelope, is a message rather than leverage. Blackmail is that note under the door: comply, or a secret is revealed or a thing is destroyed. A trick and a slogan do not carry the same lever of fear.\n',
        'r4': 'Save the threat note, including the deadline and the quoted subjects, before any reply. Compare the quotes to the mail server and write down whether they match. Do not pay from a personal wallet. Do not delete the mailbox. Disable the stolen mailbox after the note and the log are copied, and mark that disable as containment. Bring counsel into any payment decision and record their advice in the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'Blackmail is the threat to reveal or destroy unless the victim complies. Theft that already happened does not by itself create this motive. Invoice fraud wants money without a threat and belongs under financial gain. A political slogan wants a cause, not leverage. A personal grudge can destroy something and walk away with no demand. Disabling the stolen mailbox is containment. Paying from a personal wallet is not eradication and is not containment.\n',
    },
    'Financial gain': {
        'body': "Financial gain is the motive when the attacker wants money, either as a direct payment or by selling what was taken. A public cause is not required. A personal grudge is not required. A strategic secret for its own sake is not required, even when the stolen files would interest a competitor. Resources range from one fraud kit to a professional crew. Skill ranges the same way. The motive label stays money across those levels, which is why a rich crew and a poor scammer can share it.\n\nOn a Tuesday the accounts-payable clerk at a university bookstore received invoice 7781 from a vendor address the clerk recognized. The bank account printed on the PDF was new. The vendor mailbox had an unusual login from another city at 09:05 that morning. The sender wanted the wire. No slogan was attached. No notebook archive had been leaving for months. The artifact is the changed beneficiary on invoice 7781 plus that 09:05 login. The system is the vendor mailbox. Selling a stolen customer table is the same motive on another cash path. The rows would be inventory, and the money would come from a buyer.\n\nRead resources and skill without letting them rename the motive. One scammer with a free template and a single stolen login is poor in infrastructure and low in craft, yet the goal is still the wire. A funded crew with a playbook, prepaid servers, and a market for stolen tables is richer and more capable, and the goal is still money. Political actors can be just as loud and still refuse a payday. A researcher who mails a flaw and waits is not chasing a wire. Money sought, not noise and not elegance, is the test for this motive.\n\nThe crew behind a fake invoice may be organized crime. Organized crime is the actor label on that invoice. Financial gain is why the crew acted. Blackmail also wants money, but blackmail's defining act is a threat to reveal or destroy. Invoice fraud has no such threat. The victim is tricked, not coerced by exposure. A political or philosophical motive is the other trap. A slogan and a date tied to a policy fight point away from a payday, even if someone later asks supporters for donations.\n\nDefenders verify payment-detail changes on a phone number already on file, preserve the mailbox log, and disable the abused vendor account as containment. Do not pay the new account as a test. Do not call the fraud a protest because the loss was large. Espionage can involve secrets with economic value and still not be this motive, because the taker wants the strategic advantage rather than a wire or a resale. Money as the thing sought is what separates financial gain.\n",
        'r0': 'Financial gain wants money, paid directly or earned by selling what was taken. Bookstore invoice 7781 left a known vendor mailbox at the same time as an unusual login at 09:05, and the bank account on the PDF was new. No slogan and no long research drip were attached. The actor may be a crime crew. The motive is still money. Blackmail would add a threat to expose or destroy. A policy slogan would point at a cause. Disabling the abused mailbox after the log is saved is containment. Do not pay the new account as a test. Espionage wants the strategic secret, not the wire.\n',
        'r1': 'The attacker wants money. A direct payment is one path. Selling stolen data is another path. A cause is not required. A personal grudge is not required. A rich crew and a poor scammer can share this motive. A threat to publish is a different motive. A slogan about a vote is a different motive. Quiet strategic secrets are a different motive. Check payment changes on a known phone number. Save the mailbox log first. Disable the abused account after the save. Do not pay the new account as a test. The actor name and the money motive are separate labels.\n',
        'r2': "A supplier bill leaves the warehouse with the payee stamp changed, and the payment walks to the wrong office. Nobody threatens to post photos. Nobody paints a slogan about a vote. The clerk is tricked, and the prize is the money. A note that demands an envelope or else the window fills with secrets is leverage, not this quiet stamp swap. A long project to copy secret recipes for a rival house wants advantage, not this week's wire. Financial gain is that altered stamp: money is the thing being sought. Threats and causes are other motives, scored apart from the money.\n",
        'r4': 'Write down the invoice number, the new bank account, and the time of the unusual mailbox login. Save the message before you disable anything. Phone the vendor on a number already on file and say to hold the wire. Do not pay the new account as a test. Do not delete the mailbox. After the message is saved, disable the abused account and mark that disable as containment.\n',
        'exam': 'Financial gain wants money, by payment or by sale. Organized crime is a common actor for that motive, and the actor label is separate. Blackmail adds a threat to reveal or destroy. A political motive chases a cause more than a payday. Espionage can touch valuable secrets and still seek advantage rather than a wire. Disabling the abused account is containment. Paying the new account is not a defense.\n',
    },
    'Political or philosophical motives': {
        'body': "A political or philosophical motive aims at a cause, a government, or a belief, more than at a payday. The fuel is the idea. Timing often follows a public decision. A message asks to be read. Money may be refused or never mentioned. Resources and skill still vary and must be scored on their own. Volunteers with a wish to be seen often carry this motive. A government can also act from politics, but then the budget is a state's and the operators may hide. The motive question is not the actor question.\n\nThe week a regional board announced a clinic closure, the board's public site and a partner charity's donor portal were both interrupted. The replacement homepage carried a slogan about the closure. The web log showed the change at 18:40 on the announcement day. No invoice was altered. No research archive had been dripping out. No payment address appeared. The artifact is the slogan page, the 18:40 timestamp, and the announcement sitting in the same week. The systems are those two public sites. The people are whoever stood behind the slogan.\n\nSeparate the belief from the bank account and from the toolbox. Volunteers who replace a homepage often have little money, uneven skill, and a strong wish to be seen. A ministry can pursue a political goal with a large budget and high skill, and that ministry may hide rather than sign the page. Hiding does not erase a political motive, and a slogan does not create a state budget. Ethical disclosure can also be driven by belief, but the action is a waited report, not an interrupted donor portal. Score the cause on one line, the spending on another, and the craft on a third.\n\nEthical reporting is the trap, because both situations can involve a belief about right and wrong. Ethical reporting uses an agreed channel and waits for a fix. A defacement timed to a vote seeks an audience and accepts harm to the site. Financial gain would have chased a wire or a resale. Blackmail would have named a price for silence or for not destroying something. A personal grudge would aim at a former boss or a partner, not at a public policy. Defenders capture the slogan, the timing, and the absence of a payment path, then restore the honest page from backup as recovery.\n\nStudents call the incident espionage because a public body was named, or they call it ethical because the actors believed they were right. Espionage would hide and collect secrets. Being convinced does not create a disclosure program. Political or philosophical motives want the cause advanced more than they want a payday.\n",
        'r0': 'A political or philosophical motive is fueled by a cause or a belief, more than by money. The week a board announced a clinic closure, two public sites were interrupted and a slogan about that closure replaced a homepage at 18:40. No invoice changed and no payment address appeared. Volunteer actors often want the slogan seen. A state actor can share the political goal and still hide, with a much larger budget. Restoring the honest page from backup is recovery. Ethical reporting would have used an agreed mailbox and a wait, not a defacement. Blackmail would have named a price. Financial gain would have chased a wire.\n',
        'r1': 'A belief is the fuel. A payday is not the point. The timing often follows a public decision. A slogan asks to be read. Money may never be mentioned. Volunteers often want an audience. A government can share the belief and still hide. Hiding versus signing the act is an actor clue. The belief itself is the motive clue. A wire transfer would be a different motive. A threat note would be a different motive. A quiet mailbox report would be a different motive. Save the slogan and the clock time. Restore the honest page from backup.\n',
        'r2': 'People paint a slogan across a clinic wall the night a public vote goes against them. The painters want the morning street to read the words. The painters are not there to change a payee stamp or to photograph the filing cabinet in silence. A diner who quietly tells the kitchen about a broken latch, and then waits, is trying to get a repair. The painted wall seeks an audience for a belief. Political or philosophical motive is that wall: a cause advanced in public, ahead of any payday. A repair request and a payee trick are different aims entirely.\n',
        'r4': 'Save the slogan page and write down the clock time next to the public announcement date. Note that no payment address and no altered invoice were present. Do not argue with the slogan on the live site. Do not delete the page before a copy exists. Restore the honest page from backup and mark that restore as recovery. Record whether anyone claimed credit, but do not treat belief as a mailbox disclosure.\n',
        'exam': 'A political or philosophical motive chases a cause or belief more than money. A slogan timed to a public decision is the usual clue. Financial gain would show a wire or a resale. Blackmail would name a price for silence or for skipping destruction. Ethical disclosure uses an agreed channel and waits for a fix. Being convinced does not equal that channel. Espionage hides and collects secrets. Restoring the honest page is recovery.\n',
    },
    'Ethical disclosure motives': {
        'body': "An ethical disclosure motive seeks a fix, not harm. The finder reports the flaw through an agreed channel and waits before any public statement. A waited report through an agreed channel is not revenge. A waited report through an agreed channel is not chaos or war. Resources may be one skilled person working alone. Skill may be high, which reminds you that skill does not decide motive. The motive is repair. A government budget, a crime payday, and a fired administrator's grudge do not fit this path.\n\nA researcher mailed security@northwind.example on a Monday. The subject line named a query flaw in the donor form. A written report was attached. The program's published window was 90 days, and the researcher stayed silent while the ticket moved. No site was defaced. No backup was deleted. No utility was shut off. No payment was demanded. The artifact is that mailbox message, the Monday timestamp, and the lack of a public post during the wait. The person is the researcher. The systems are the security mailbox and a test copy of the donor form.\n\nPreparation on the organization side is a monitored mailbox, a public reporting path, and a rule against threatening a good-faith reporter. Staff acknowledge the letter, reproduce the flaw on a test copy, preserve the report, and patch the hole. Patching after the flaw is understood is the fix. Treating the researcher as an intruder to eradicate is the wrong story. If a working exploit is posted the same hour to embarrass the team, the agreed wait was skipped. Embarrassment as the aim is not this motive.\n\nCompare the three axes so a skilled report is not misfiled as an attack. Resources here are often just the researcher's own time. Skill may exceed the skill of a noisy kit user, because finding a real query flaw takes care. Motive is repair, which stands opposite a grudge and opposite a desire to watch systems fail. A believer can still deface a page, and that act is political harm rather than a waited report. A fired admin can know the systems better than the researcher and still delete backups out of revenge. Knowledge is not a motive.\n\nRevenge targets people the actor knows and damages their work, often a former employer. Chaos and war seek damage, fear, or a conflict advantage, and they do not want a patch. A defacement for a cause wants an audience, not a quiet ticket. Ethical disclosure motives use the front door and want the hole closed. The channel and the aim, together, separate this motive from revenge and from chaos.\n",
        'r0': 'Ethical disclosure seeks a fix through an agreed channel and a wait. A researcher mailed security@northwind.example on a Monday, named a query flaw in the donor form, and attached a report. The published window was 90 days. No defacement, no deleted backup, no utility outage, and no payment demand followed. Skill can be high. The motive is still repair. Staff should reproduce the flaw on a test copy, preserve the report, and patch. A same-hour public exploit posted to embarrass the team skips the wait. Revenge would damage people the actor knows. Chaos would seek harm with no wish for a patch.\n',
        'r1': 'The finder wants the hole closed. The report uses an agreed door. A wait comes before any public talk. Harm is not the goal. Revenge is a different motive. Chaos is a different motive. High skill can still be friendly. A government budget is not required. A payment demand does not belong here. A deleted backup does not belong here. A smashed public site does not belong here. Answer the letter. Test the flaw on a copy. Keep the report. Patch after you understand it. Do not treat the reporter as an enemy.\n',
        'r2': "A diner tells the clinic kitchen that the walk-in door will not latch, then waits outside while the cook fixes it. Plates stay whole. No backup stew is thrown out. No one demands cash for the warning. A fired cook who dumps the reserve stock that same night is settling a personal score. A person who wrecks the town water tap to spread fear is not asking for a latch. Ethical disclosure is the diner's warning: a front door, a wait, and a wish to see the fault repaired. Grudge and wreckage are different motives, and neither one is a request for repair.\n",
        'r4': 'Save the report, the sender, the Monday time, and the stated flaw before you change the live form. Reproduce the flaw on a test copy only. Do not threaten the reporter. Do not post the details yourself during the waiting window. Patch the hole after the report is understood and note the patch in the ticket. If a public exploit appears the same day, write that the agreed wait was skipped.\n',
        'exam': 'Ethical disclosure wants a fix, uses an agreed channel, and waits. Hurting people the finder knows would be revenge instead. Seeking damage or conflict advantage would be chaos or war instead. A same-hour public exploit meant to embarrass the team skips that motive. High skill does not make the reporter an attacker. Patching after the report is understood is the fix. Treating the reporter as an intruder to eradicate is the wrong story.\n',
    },
    'Revenge': {
        'body': "Revenge is personal. The target is often a former employer, a coworker, or a partner. The fuel is a grudge. A public cause, a criminal market, and a wish to see a bug fixed are different fuels. Resources are often the keys the person already held. Skill may be high when the person was an administrator, which shows again that skill does not pick the motive. A government operator can be equally skilled and still be collecting secrets rather than settling a feud.\n\nR. Patel, a storage administrator at a clinic network, was walked out on a Thursday afternoon. The account was still enabled that night. At 22:40 the account opened a shell from Patel's home address and deleted the daily backup tree on the storage server. No ransom note was left. No slogan was left. No bug report went to a security mailbox. The damage aimed at the people who had just ended Patel's job. The artifact is the 22:40 shell from home and the missing backup tree. The system is the storage server. The person is the former administrator.\n\nPut budget and craft beside the grudge without mixing the columns. Patel's resources were an account that should already have been closed, not a ministry server farm. Patel's skill was real storage administration, higher than a person clicking through a borrowed kit. The motive was still personal harm to a former employer, not espionage and not a public cause. An unskilled stranger spraying the clinic from the internet would be external, noisy, and short of a named feud. A careful report that waits for a patch would be using skill to seek a fix. The 22:40 deletion is revenge because the target is personal.\n\nPreparation would have disabled that account before the termination meeting. Once deletion is underway, disabling the account and cutting the remote session are containment, not eradication. Shell history and storage logs should be preserved first. Restoring from offline copies the former admin cannot reach is recovery. Deleting leftover tools, after those logs are saved, is eradication. Do not call the account disable eradication. Do not call the deletion a helpful disclosure because Patel knew the systems well.\n\nEthical disclosure waits on an agreed channel and asks for a fix, without damaging the workplace. Chaos and war damage a system to spread fear or to gain advantage in a conflict, without a named personal feud. Blackmail would have demanded something before the backups died. Revenge harms people the actor knows. The personal grudge is what separates revenge from both a disclosure and a campaign of chaos.\n",
        'r0': "Revenge is a personal grudge, often against a former employer, a coworker, or a partner. Storage admin R. Patel was walked out Thursday, and the account stayed enabled. At 22:40 a shell from Patel's home address deleted the daily backup tree. No ransom, no slogan, and no security-mailbox report appeared. Preparation would have disabled the account before the meeting. Disabling it during the deletion is containment, not eradication. Restoring from offline copies the former admin cannot reach is recovery. Deleting leftover tools after logs are saved is eradication. Ethical disclosure would have asked for a fix and waited. Chaos would not be tied to this named firing.\n",
        'r1': 'The fuel is a personal grudge. The target is someone known. A former employer is a common target. A coworker or a partner can be the target too. High skill does not decide the motive. Keys already held are the usual resource. A bug report would ask for a fix. A war-like outage would not name one fired person. A ransom would ask for money. Save the shell history first. Disable the account as containment. Do not call that disable eradication. Restore from copies the person cannot reach.\n',
        'r2': 'A fired clinic cook throws out the reserve stock the night the key should already have been taken. The loss is aimed at the people who ended the job. No envelope is demanded. No speech is painted on the dining room for a public vote. A diner who reported a broken latch and waited did not touch the stock. A stranger who wrecks the town water tap during a wider fight is not settling a firing. Revenge is that dumped reserve: a grudge against known people. A repair report and a conflict attack are other motives aimed at other targets.\n',
        'r4': 'Write down the home address, the 22:40 time, and which backup tree disappeared. Copy shell history and storage logs before you change the host. Do not wipe the storage server yet. Disable the former admin account and cut the session, and mark both as containment, not eradication. Restore from offline copies that account cannot reach, and mark the restore as recovery.\n',
        'exam': 'Revenge is personal and aims at people the actor knows, such as a former employer. A flaw report that waits for a fix is ethical disclosure, not this grudge. Damage for a conflict, without that personal feud, is chaos or war. A ransom demand would be a different motive. Disabling the account is containment, not eradication. Restoring unreachable offline copies is recovery. Wiping logs before they are saved destroys the proof.\n',
    },
    'Chaos and war': {
        'body': "Chaos and war motives seek damage, fear, or advantage in a conflict, not a tidy ransom. Nobody is asking for a patch. Nobody is settling a score with one former boss. Chaos can belong to a person with modest resources who wants to watch breakage and does not need a strategic sponsor. War points at conflict advantage and often arrives with a government's resources and higher skill. The shared mark is the aim: harm or leverage in a fight, without a clean cash demand. Score budget and skill separately so a lone breaker is not filed as an army, and an army is not filed as a prank.\n\nA county water utility found its treatment pumps commanded off just after dawn. The operator console showed the stop commands at 05:12. Customer billing files were untouched. No payment demand arrived. No researcher had mailed a flaw report. The effect was fear and a loss of service while a regional conflict filled that week's news. The actor may be unknown at first. The system is the treatment console. The artifact is the 05:12 stop with an empty demand column and an intact billing store.\n\nAn outage by itself can serve several motives, including a protest or the start of extortion. Here the missing demand and the choice of a utility during a conflict push the motive toward chaos and war rather than money. Ethical disclosure sits at the far end of the scale. Ethical disclosure uses an agreed channel, waits, and wants the hole closed. Revenge is personal and names people the actor knows, such as a fired administrator deleting backups. Defenders move the plant to manual control, isolate the affected console, and preserve the command log before rebuilding the session. Isolating the console is containment.\n\nKeep the harm motive, then split the actors by spending power and by craft. A person who wants to watch breakage may have one laptop and clumsy tools, and still fit chaos when there is no ransom and no personal feud. A government unit that darkens a utility for advantage in a conflict may bring custom access and a long budget, and that motive is war rather than a wire. Do not average those two pictures into one mythical attacker. A researcher asks for a patch and waits, which is a different motive. A fired coworker fits only when the evidence names that person and that grudge.\n\nStudents call every outage either a ransom crime or a bug report. A ransom would be a tidy demand, which this motive does not make. A bug report would use the front door and would not command pumps off. Financial gain wants money. Political protest wants a message seen. Chaos and war want damage or conflict advantage, and they are not revenge and not ethical disclosure.\n",
        'r0': 'Chaos and war seek damage, fear, or advantage in a conflict, without a tidy ransom. A county water utility found treatment pumps commanded off at 05:12. Billing files were untouched and no payment demand arrived. No flaw report had been mailed. Chaos can be a modest actor who wants breakage. War often brings government resources and higher skill, so budget and skill still need their own notes. Isolating the operator console is containment. Manual operation keeps water moving while the log is preserved. Ethical disclosure would have requested a patch and waited. Revenge would name a personal target, such as a former boss. A ransom note would mark a money motive instead.\n',
        'r1': 'The aim is damage or fear. A conflict advantage can be the aim too. A tidy ransom is not the point. A patch request is not the point. A personal firing is not the point. A lone person can want pure breakage. A government can want leverage in a fight. A lone breaker and a government differ in budget and skill. The motive family is still harm. Billing files can stay untouched. Save the command log. Isolate the affected controls. Move the plant to hand operation. Do not call the outage a bug report. Do not invent a ransom that was never sent.\n',
        'r2': "Someone wrecks the town waterworks during a wider fight and never leaves a fee on the door. Fear and stopped service are the point. A diner who warned the kitchen about a broken latch wanted a repair and waited. A fired cook who dumped one kitchen's reserve stock was aiming at named coworkers. A banner on city hall wants readers, not a dry tap. Chaos and war are that wrecked waterworks: damage, fear, or advantage in a conflict, without a tidy ransom. Repair, grudge, and a public slogan are different motives and do not explain a wrecked waterworks by themselves.\n",
        'r4': 'Write down the 05:12 stop commands and confirm whether any payment demand exists. Note that billing files were untouched. Do not rebuild the console session before the command log is copied. Do not label the case a ransom crime without a demand. Isolate the console and mark that isolation as containment. Move the plant to manual control while the log is preserved.\n',
        'exam': 'Chaos and war want damage, fear, or conflict advantage, not a tidy ransom. Ethical disclosure asks for a fix through an agreed channel and waits. Revenge aims at people the actor knows. A missing demand separates this motive from financial gain. A missing slogan separates it from a political protest that wants to be seen. Isolating the console is containment. Budget and skill still vary between a lone breaker and a government unit.\n',
    },
}
